{"surah": 14, "verses": [{"ayah": 1, "text": "الٓرۚ كِتَٰبٌ أَنزَلْنَٰهُ إِلَيْكَ لِتُخْرِجَ ٱلنَّاسَ مِنَ ٱلظُّلُمَٰتِ إِلَى ٱلنُّورِ بِإِذْنِ رَبِّهِمْ إِلَىٰ صِرَٰطِ ٱلْعَزِيزِ ٱلْحَمِيدِ ", "translation": "Alif, Lām, Rā.[1] [This is] a Book which We have revealed to you, [O Muḥammad], that you might bring mankind out of darknesses into the light by permission of their Lord - to the path of the Exalted in Might, the Praiseworthy -", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "الٓر", "translit": "rٓlā", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "كِتَٰبٌ", "translit": "ٌbَٰtِk", "root": "كتب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَنزَلْنَٰهُ", "translit": "ُhَٰnْlَznَaʾ", "root": "نزل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "إِلَيْكَ", "translit": "َkْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لِتُخْرِجَ", "translit": "َjgِrْhkُtِl", "root": "خرج"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلنَّاسَ", "translit": "َsāَّnlٱ", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلظُّلُمَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰmُlُّẓlٱ", "root": "ظلم"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "إِلَى", "translit": "àَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلنُّورِ", "translit": "ِrwُّnlٱ", "root": "نور"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "بِإِذْنِ", "translit": "ِnْhdِiʾِb", "root": "اذن"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "رَبِّهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "إِلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "صِرَٰطِ", "translit": "ِṭَٰrِṣ", "root": "صرط"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ٱلْعَزِيزِ", "translit": "ِzyِzَʿعْlٱ", "root": "عزز"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "ٱلْحَمِيدِ", "translit": "ِdyِmَḥْlٱ", "root": "حمد"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 2, "text": "ٱللَّهِ ٱلَّذِى لَهُۥ مَا فِى ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ وَمَا فِى ٱلْأَرْضِۗ وَوَيْلٌ لِّلْكَٰفِرِينَ مِنْ عَذَابٍ شَدِيدٍ ", "translation": "Allāh, to whom belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth. And woe [i.e., destruction] to the disbelievers from a severe punishment -", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِى", "translit": "àِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰwَٰmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَوَيْلٌ", "translit": "ٌlْyَwَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "لِّلْكَٰفِرِينَ", "translit": "َnyِrِfَٰkْlِّl", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "مِنْ", "translit": "ْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "عَذَابٍ", "translit": "ٍbāَhdَʿع", "root": "عذب"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "شَدِيدٍ", "translit": "ٍdyِdَhs", "root": "شدد"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 3, "text": "ٱلَّذِينَ يَسْتَحِبُّونَ ٱلْحَيَوٰةَ ٱلدُّنْيَا عَلَى ٱلْءَاخِرَةِ وَيَصُدُّونَ عَن سَبِيلِ ٱللَّهِ وَيَبْغُونَهَا عِوَجًاۚ أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ فِى ضَلَٰلٍۭ بَعِيدٍ ", "translation": "The ones who prefer the worldly life over the Hereafter and avert [people] from the way of Allāh, seeking to make it [seem] deviant. Those are in extreme error.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَسْتَحِبُّونَ", "translit": "َnwُّbِḥَtْsَy", "root": "حبب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْحَيَوٰةَ", "translit": "َẗٰwَyَḥْlٱ", "root": "حيي"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلدُّنْيَا", "translit": "āَyْnُّdlٱ", "root": "دنو"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "عَلَى", "translit": "àَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلْءَاخِرَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَrِhkāَءْlٱ", "root": "اخر"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَيَصُدُّونَ", "translit": "َnwُّdُṣَyَw", "root": "صدد"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "عَن", "translit": "nَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "سَبِيلِ", "translit": "ِlyِbَs", "root": "سبل"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَيَبْغُونَهَا", "translit": "āَhَnwُغْbَyَw", "root": "بغي"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "عِوَجًا", "translit": "āًjgَwِʿع", "root": "عوج"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ", "translit": "َkِyʾَٰٓl۟wُaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ضَلَٰلٍۭ", "translit": "ٍۭlَٰlَḍ", "root": "ضلل"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "بَعِيدٍ", "translit": "ٍdyِʿعَb", "root": "بعد"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 4, "text": "وَمَآ أَرْسَلْنَا مِن رَّسُولٍ إِلَّا بِلِسَانِ قَوْمِهِۦ لِيُبَيِّنَ لَهُمْۖ فَيُضِلُّ ٱللَّهُ مَن يَشَآءُ وَيَهْدِى مَن يَشَآءُۚ وَهُوَ ٱلْعَزِيزُ ٱلْحَكِيمُ ", "translation": "And We did not send any messenger except [speaking] in the language of his people to state clearly for them, and Allāh sends astray [thereby] whom He wills[1] and guides whom He wills. And He is the Exalted in Might, the Wise.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمَآ", "translit": "ٓāَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَرْسَلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlَsْrَaʾ", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "رَّسُولٍ", "translit": "ٍlwُsَّr", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "بِلِسَانِ", "translit": "ِnāَsِlِb", "root": "لسن"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "قَوْمِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِmْwَq", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "لِيُبَيِّنَ", "translit": "َnِّyَbُyِl", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "لَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "فَيُضِلُّ", "translit": "ُّlِḍُyَf", "root": "ضلل"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "مَن", "translit": "nَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "يَشَآءُ", "translit": "ُءٓāَhsَy", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "وَيَهْدِى", "translit": "àِdْhَyَw", "root": "هدي"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "مَن", "translit": "nَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "يَشَآءُ", "translit": "ُءٓāَhsَy", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "وَهُوَ", "translit": "َwُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "ٱلْعَزِيزُ", "translit": "ُzyِzَʿعْlٱ", "root": "عزز"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "ٱلْحَكِيمُ", "translit": "ُmyِkَḥْlٱ", "root": "حكم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 5, "text": "وَلَقَدْ أَرْسَلْنَا مُوسَىٰ بِـَٔايَٰتِنَآ أَنْ أَخْرِجْ قَوْمَكَ مِنَ ٱلظُّلُمَٰتِ إِلَى ٱلنُّورِ وَذَكِّرْهُم بِأَيَّىٰمِ ٱللَّهِۚ إِنَّ فِى ذَٰلِكَ لَءَايَٰتٍ لِّكُلِّ صَبَّارٍ شَكُورٍ ", "translation": "And We certainly sent Moses with Our signs, [saying], \"Bring out your people from darknesses into the light and remind them of the days[1] of Allāh.\" Indeed in that are signs for everyone patient and grateful.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَرْسَلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlَsْrَaʾ", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مُوسَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَswُm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بِـَٔايَٰتِنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnِtَٰyāَٔـِb", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَنْ", "translit": "ْnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أَخْرِجْ", "translit": "ْjgِrْhkَaʾ", "root": "خرج"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "قَوْمَكَ", "translit": "َkَmْwَq", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلظُّلُمَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰmُlُّẓlٱ", "root": "ظلم"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "إِلَى", "translit": "àَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلنُّورِ", "translit": "ِrwُّnlٱ", "root": "نور"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَذَكِّرْهُم", "translit": "mُhْrِّkَhdَw", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "بِأَيَّىٰمِ", "translit": "ِmٰàَّyَaʾِb", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "لَءَايَٰتٍ", "translit": "ٍtَٰyāَءَl", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "لِّكُلِّ", "translit": "ِّlُkِّl", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "صَبَّارٍ", "translit": "ٍrāَّbَṣ", "root": "صبر"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "شَكُورٍ", "translit": "ٍrwُkَhs", "root": "شكر"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 6, "text": "وَإِذْ قَالَ مُوسَىٰ لِقَوْمِهِ ٱذْكُرُوا۟ نِعْمَةَ ٱللَّهِ عَلَيْكُمْ إِذْ أَنجَىٰكُم مِّنْ ءَالِ فِرْعَوْنَ يَسُومُونَكُمْ سُوٓءَ ٱلْعَذَابِ وَيُذَبِّحُونَ أَبْنَآءَكُمْ وَيَسْتَحْيُونَ نِسَآءَكُمْۚ وَفِى ذَٰلِكُم بَلَآءٌ مِّن رَّبِّكُمْ عَظِيمٌ ", "translation": "And [recall, O Children of Israel], when Moses said to his people, \"Remember the favor of Allāh upon you when He saved you from the people of Pharaoh, who were afflicting you with the worst torment and were slaughtering your [newborn] sons and keeping your females alive. And in that was a great trial from your Lord.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَإِذْ", "translit": "ْhdِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "قَالَ", "translit": "َlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مُوسَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَswُm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لِقَوْمِهِ", "translit": "ِhِmْwَqِl", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱذْكُرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrُkْhdٱ", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "نِعْمَةَ", "translit": "َẗَmْʿعِn", "root": "نعم"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "عَلَيْكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "إِذْ", "translit": "ْhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "أَنجَىٰكُم", "translit": "mُkٰàَjgnَaʾ", "root": "نجو"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "مِّنْ", "translit": "ْnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ءَالِ", "translit": "ِlāَء", "root": "اول"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "فِرْعَوْنَ", "translit": "َnْwَʿعْrِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "يَسُومُونَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَnwُmwُsَy", "root": "سوم"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "سُوٓءَ", "translit": "َءٓwُs", "root": "سوا"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "ٱلْعَذَابِ", "translit": "ِbāَhdَʿعْlٱ", "root": "عذب"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "وَيُذَبِّحُونَ", "translit": "َnwُḥِّbَhdُyَw", "root": "ذبح"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "أَبْنَآءَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَءٓāَnْbَaʾ", "root": "بني"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "وَيَسْتَحْيُونَ", "translit": "َnwُyْḥَtْsَyَw", "root": "حيي"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "نِسَآءَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَءٓāَsِn", "root": "نسو"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "وَفِى", "translit": "àِfَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكُم", "translit": "mُkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "بَلَآءٌ", "translit": "ٌءٓāَlَb", "root": "بلو"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "رَّبِّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkِّbَّr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "عَظِيمٌ", "translit": "ٌmyِẓَʿع", "root": "عظم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 7, "text": "وَإِذْ تَأَذَّنَ رَبُّكُمْ لَئِن شَكَرْتُمْ لَأَزِيدَنَّكُمْۖ وَلَئِن كَفَرْتُمْ إِنَّ عَذَابِى لَشَدِيدٌ ", "translation": "And [remember] when your Lord proclaimed, 'If you are grateful, I will surely increase you [in favor]; but if you deny, indeed, My punishment is severe.'\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَإِذْ", "translit": "ْhdِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تَأَذَّنَ", "translit": "َnَّhdَaʾَt", "root": "اذن"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "رَبُّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkُّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لَئِن", "translit": "nِyʾَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "شَكَرْتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtْrَkَhs", "root": "شكر"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لَأَزِيدَنَّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَّnَdyِzَaʾَl", "root": "زيد"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَلَئِن", "translit": "nِyʾَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "كَفَرْتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtْrَfَk", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "عَذَابِى", "translit": "àِbāَhdَʿع", "root": "عذب"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "لَشَدِيدٌ", "translit": "ٌdyِdَhsَl", "root": "شدد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 8, "text": "وَقَالَ مُوسَىٰٓ إِن تَكْفُرُوٓا۟ أَنتُمْ وَمَن فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ جَمِيعًا فَإِنَّ ٱللَّهَ لَغَنِىٌّ حَمِيدٌ ", "translation": "And Moses said, \"If you should disbelieve, you and whoever is on the earth entirely - indeed, Allāh is Free of need and Praiseworthy.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَقَالَ", "translit": "َlāَqَw", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مُوسَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَswُm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "إِن", "translit": "nِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "تَكْفُرُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُrُfْkَt", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَنتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَمَن", "translit": "nَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "جَمِيعًا", "translit": "āًʿعyِmَjg", "root": "جمع"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "فَإِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "لَغَنِىٌّ", "translit": "ٌّàِnَغَl", "root": "غني"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "حَمِيدٌ", "translit": "ٌdyِmَḥ", "root": "حمد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 9, "text": "أَلَمْ يَأْتِكُمْ نَبَؤُا۟ ٱلَّذِينَ مِن قَبْلِكُمْ قَوْمِ نُوحٍ وَعَادٍ وَثَمُودَۛ وَٱلَّذِينَ مِنۢ بَعْدِهِمْۛ لَا يَعْلَمُهُمْ إِلَّا ٱللَّهُۚ جَآءَتْهُمْ رُسُلُهُم بِٱلْبَيِّنَٰتِ فَرَدُّوٓا۟ أَيْدِيَهُمْ فِىٓ أَفْوَٰهِهِمْ وَقَالُوٓا۟ إِنَّا كَفَرْنَا بِمَآ أُرْسِلْتُم بِهِۦ وَإِنَّا لَفِى شَكٍّ مِّمَّا تَدْعُونَنَآ إِلَيْهِ مُرِيبٍ ", "translation": "Has there not reached you the news of those before you - the people of Noah and ʿAad and Thamūd and those after them? No one knows them [i.e., their number] but Allāh. Their messengers brought them clear proofs, but they returned their hands to their mouths[1] and said, \"Indeed, we disbelieve in that with which you have been sent, and indeed we are, about that to which you invite us, in disquieting doubt.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَلَمْ", "translit": "ْmَlَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَأْتِكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkِtْaʾَy", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "نَبَؤُا۟", "translit": "۟āُwʾَbَn", "root": "نبا"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "قَبْلِكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkِlْbَq", "root": "قبل"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "قَوْمِ", "translit": "ِmْwَq", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "نُوحٍ", "translit": "ٍḥwُn", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَعَادٍ", "translit": "ٍdāَʿعَw", "root": "عود"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَثَمُودَ", "translit": "َdwُmَhtَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "مِنۢ", "translit": "ۢnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "بَعْدِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِdْʿعَb", "root": "بعد"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "يَعْلَمُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُmَlْʿعَy", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "جَآءَتْهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhْtَءٓāَjg", "root": "جيا"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "رُسُلُهُم", "translit": "mُhُlُsُr", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "بِٱلْبَيِّنَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰnِّyَbْlٱِb", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "فَرَدُّوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُّdَrَf", "root": "ردد"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "أَيْدِيَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَyِdْyَaʾ", "root": "يدي"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "فِىٓ", "translit": "ٓàِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "أَفْوَٰهِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِhَٰwْfَaʾ", "root": "فوه"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "وَقَالُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُlāَqَw", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "إِنَّا", "translit": "āَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "كَفَرْنَا", "translit": "āَnْrَfَk", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 28, "arabic": "بِمَآ", "translit": "ٓāَmِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 29, "arabic": "أُرْسِلْتُم", "translit": "mُtْlِsْrُaʾ", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 30, "arabic": "بِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 31, "arabic": "وَإِنَّا", "translit": "āَّnِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 32, "arabic": "لَفِى", "translit": "àِfَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 33, "arabic": "شَكٍّ", "translit": "ٍّkَhs", "root": "شكك"}, {"id": 34, "arabic": "مِّمَّا", "translit": "āَّmِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 35, "arabic": "تَدْعُونَنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnَnwُʿعْdَt", "root": "دعو"}, {"id": 36, "arabic": "إِلَيْهِ", "translit": "ِhْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 37, "arabic": "مُرِيبٍ", "translit": "ٍbyِrُm", "root": "ريب"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 10, "text": "قَالَتْ رُسُلُهُمْ أَفِى ٱللَّهِ شَكٌّ فَاطِرِ ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ وَٱلْأَرْضِۖ يَدْعُوكُمْ لِيَغْفِرَ لَكُم مِّن ذُنُوبِكُمْ وَيُؤَخِّرَكُمْ إِلَىٰٓ أَجَلٍ مُّسَمًّىۚ قَالُوٓا۟ إِنْ أَنتُمْ إِلَّا بَشَرٌ مِّثْلُنَا تُرِيدُونَ أَن تَصُدُّونَا عَمَّا كَانَ يَعْبُدُ ءَابَآؤُنَا فَأْتُونَا بِسُلْطَٰنٍ مُّبِينٍ ", "translation": "Their messengers said, \"Can there be doubt about Allāh, Creator of the heavens and earth? He invites you that He may forgive you of your sins, and He delays you [i.e., your death] for a specified term.\" They said, \"You are not but men like us who wish to avert us from what our fathers were worshipping. So bring us a clear authority [i.e., evidence].\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قَالَتْ", "translit": "ْtَlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "رُسُلُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُlُsُr", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَفِى", "translit": "àِfَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "شَكٌّ", "translit": "ٌّkَhs", "root": "شكك"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فَاطِرِ", "translit": "ِrِṭāَf", "root": "فطر"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰwَٰmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱَw", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "يَدْعُوكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkwُʿعْdَy", "root": "دعو"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "لِيَغْفِرَ", "translit": "َrِfْغَyِl", "root": "غفر"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "لَكُم", "translit": "mُkَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ذُنُوبِكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkِbwُnُhd", "root": "ذنب"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "وَيُؤَخِّرَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَrِّhkَwʾُyَw", "root": "اخر"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "إِلَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "أَجَلٍ", "translit": "ٍlَjgَaʾ", "root": "اجل"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "مُّسَمًّى", "translit": "àًّmَsُّm", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "قَالُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "إِنْ", "translit": "ْnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "أَنتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "بَشَرٌ", "translit": "ٌrَhsَb", "root": "بشر"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "مِّثْلُنَا", "translit": "āَnُlْhtِّm", "root": "مثل"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "تُرِيدُونَ", "translit": "َnwُdyِrُt", "root": "رود"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "تَصُدُّونَا", "translit": "āَnwُّdُṣَt", "root": "صدد"}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "عَمَّا", "translit": "āَّmَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 28, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 29, "arabic": "يَعْبُدُ", "translit": "ُdُbْʿعَy", "root": "عبد"}, {"id": 30, "arabic": "ءَابَآؤُنَا", "translit": "āَnُwʾٓāَbāَء", "root": "ابو"}, {"id": 31, "arabic": "فَأْتُونَا", "translit": "āَnwُtْaʾَf", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 32, "arabic": "بِسُلْطَٰنٍ", "translit": "ٍnَٰṭْlُsِb", "root": "سلط"}, {"id": 33, "arabic": "مُّبِينٍ", "translit": "ٍnyِbُّm", "root": "بين"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 11, "text": "قَالَتْ لَهُمْ رُسُلُهُمْ إِن نَّحْنُ إِلَّا بَشَرٌ مِّثْلُكُمْ وَلَٰكِنَّ ٱللَّهَ يَمُنُّ عَلَىٰ مَن يَشَآءُ مِنْ عِبَادِهِۦۖ وَمَا كَانَ لَنَآ أَن نَّأْتِيَكُم بِسُلْطَٰنٍ إِلَّا بِإِذْنِ ٱللَّهِۚ وَعَلَى ٱللَّهِ فَلْيَتَوَكَّلِ ٱلْمُؤْمِنُونَ ", "translation": "Their messengers said to them, \"We are only men like you, but Allāh confers favor upon whom He wills of His servants. It has never been for us to bring you evidence except by permission of Allāh. And upon Allāh let the believers rely.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قَالَتْ", "translit": "ْtَlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "رُسُلُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُlُsُr", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "إِن", "translit": "nِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "نَّحْنُ", "translit": "ُnْḥَّn", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "بَشَرٌ", "translit": "ٌrَhsَb", "root": "بشر"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مِّثْلُكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkُlْhtِّm", "root": "مثل"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَلَٰكِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِkَٰlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "يَمُنُّ", "translit": "ُّnُmَy", "root": "منن"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "مَن", "translit": "nَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "يَشَآءُ", "translit": "ُءٓāَhsَy", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "مِنْ", "translit": "ْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "عِبَادِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِdāَbِʿع", "root": "عبد"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "لَنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "نَّأْتِيَكُم", "translit": "mُkَyِtْaʾَّn", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "بِسُلْطَٰنٍ", "translit": "ٍnَٰṭْlُsِb", "root": "سلط"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "بِإِذْنِ", "translit": "ِnْhdِiʾِb", "root": "اذن"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "وَعَلَى", "translit": "àَlَʿعَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 28, "arabic": "فَلْيَتَوَكَّلِ", "translit": "ِlَّkَwَtَyْlَf", "root": "وكل"}, {"id": 29, "arabic": "ٱلْمُؤْمِنُونَ", "translit": "َnwُnِmْwʾُmْlٱ", "root": "امن"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 12, "text": "وَمَا لَنَآ أَلَّا نَتَوَكَّلَ عَلَى ٱللَّهِ وَقَدْ هَدَىٰنَا سُبُلَنَاۚ وَلَنَصْبِرَنَّ عَلَىٰ مَآ ءَاذَيْتُمُونَاۚ وَعَلَى ٱللَّهِ فَلْيَتَوَكَّلِ ٱلْمُتَوَكِّلُونَ ", "translation": "And why should we not rely upon Allāh while He has guided us to our [good] ways. And we will surely be patient against whatever harm you should cause us. And upon Allāh let those who would rely [indeed] rely.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَلَّا", "translit": "āَّlَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "نَتَوَكَّلَ", "translit": "َlَّkَwَtَn", "root": "وكل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "عَلَى", "translit": "àَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "هَدَىٰنَا", "translit": "āَnٰàَdَh", "root": "هدي"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "سُبُلَنَا", "translit": "āَnَlُbُs", "root": "سبل"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَلَنَصْبِرَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَrِbْṣَnَlَw", "root": "صبر"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "مَآ", "translit": "ٓāَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ءَاذَيْتُمُونَا", "translit": "āَnwُmُtْyَhdāَء", "root": "اذي"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "وَعَلَى", "translit": "àَlَʿعَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "فَلْيَتَوَكَّلِ", "translit": "ِlَّkَwَtَyْlَf", "root": "وكل"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "ٱلْمُتَوَكِّلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlِّkَwَtُmْlٱ", "root": "وكل"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 13, "text": "وَقَالَ ٱلَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا۟ لِرُسُلِهِمْ لَنُخْرِجَنَّكُم مِّنْ أَرْضِنَآ أَوْ لَتَعُودُنَّ فِى مِلَّتِنَاۖ فَأَوْحَىٰٓ إِلَيْهِمْ رَبُّهُمْ لَنُهْلِكَنَّ ٱلظَّٰلِمِينَ ", "translation": "And those who disbelieved said to their messengers, \"We will surely drive you out of our land, or you must return to our religion.\" So their Lord inspired to them, \"We will surely destroy the wrongdoers.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَقَالَ", "translit": "َlāَqَw", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "كَفَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَfَk", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لِرُسُلِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِlُsُrِl", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لَنُخْرِجَنَّكُم", "translit": "mُkَّnَjgِrْhkُnَl", "root": "خرج"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مِّنْ", "translit": "ْnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَرْضِنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnِḍْrَaʾ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "أَوْ", "translit": "ْwَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "لَتَعُودُنَّ", "translit": "َّnُdwُʿعَtَl", "root": "عود"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "مِلَّتِنَا", "translit": "āَnِtَّlِm", "root": "ملل"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "فَأَوْحَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَḥْwَaʾَf", "root": "وحي"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "إِلَيْهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "رَبُّهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "لَنُهْلِكَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَkِlْhُnَl", "root": "هلك"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "ٱلظَّٰلِمِينَ", "translit": "َnyِmِlَّٰẓlٱ", "root": "ظلم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 14, "text": "وَلَنُسْكِنَنَّكُمُ ٱلْأَرْضَ مِنۢ بَعْدِهِمْۚ ذَٰلِكَ لِمَنْ خَافَ مَقَامِى وَخَافَ وَعِيدِ ", "translation": "And We will surely cause you to dwell in the land after them. That is for he who fears My position[1] and fears My threat.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَنُسْكِنَنَّكُمُ", "translit": "ُmُkَّnَnِkْsُnَlَw", "root": "سكن"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضَ", "translit": "َḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مِنۢ", "translit": "ۢnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بَعْدِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِdْʿعَb", "root": "بعد"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لِمَنْ", "translit": "ْnَmِl", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "خَافَ", "translit": "َfāَhk", "root": "خوف"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مَقَامِى", "translit": "àِmāَqَm", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَخَافَ", "translit": "َfāَhkَw", "root": "خوف"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَعِيدِ", "translit": "ِdyِʿعَw", "root": "وعد"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 15, "text": "وَٱسْتَفْتَحُوا۟ وَخَابَ كُلُّ جَبَّارٍ عَنِيدٍ ", "translation": "And they requested decision [i.e., victory from Allāh], and disappointed, [therefore], was every obstinate tyrant.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱسْتَفْتَحُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُḥَtْfَtْsٱَw", "root": "فتح"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "وَخَابَ", "translit": "َbāَhkَw", "root": "خيب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "كُلُّ", "translit": "ُّlُk", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "جَبَّارٍ", "translit": "ٍrāَّbَjg", "root": "جبر"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "عَنِيدٍ", "translit": "ٍdyِnَʿع", "root": "عند"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 16, "text": "مِّن وَرَآئِهِۦ جَهَنَّمُ وَيُسْقَىٰ مِن مَّآءٍ صَدِيدٍ ", "translation": "Before him[1] is Hell, and he will be given a drink of purulent water.[2]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "وَرَآئِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِyʾٓāَrَw", "root": "وري"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "جَهَنَّمُ", "translit": "ُmَّnَhَjg", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَيُسْقَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَqْsُyَw", "root": "سقي"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مَّآءٍ", "translit": "ٍءٓāَّm", "root": "موه"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "صَدِيدٍ", "translit": "ٍdyِdَṣ", "root": "صدد"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 17, "text": "يَتَجَرَّعُهُۥ وَلَا يَكَادُ يُسِيغُهُۥ وَيَأْتِيهِ ٱلْمَوْتُ مِن كُلِّ مَكَانٍ وَمَا هُوَ بِمَيِّتٍۖ وَمِن وَرَآئِهِۦ عَذَابٌ غَلِيظٌ ", "translation": "He will gulp it but will hardly [be able to] swallow it. And death will come to him from everywhere, but he is not to die. And before him is a massive punishment.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَتَجَرَّعُهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhُʿعَّrَjgَtَy", "root": "جرع"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يَكَادُ", "translit": "ُdāَkَy", "root": "كود"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يُسِيغُهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhُغyِsُy", "root": "سوغ"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَيَأْتِيهِ", "translit": "ِhyِtْaʾَyَw", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلْمَوْتُ", "translit": "ُtْwَmْlٱ", "root": "موت"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "كُلِّ", "translit": "ِّlُk", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "مَكَانٍ", "translit": "ٍnāَkَm", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "هُوَ", "translit": "َwُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "بِمَيِّتٍ", "translit": "ٍtِّyَmِb", "root": "موت"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "وَمِن", "translit": "nِmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "وَرَآئِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِyʾٓāَrَw", "root": "وري"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "عَذَابٌ", "translit": "ٌbāَhdَʿع", "root": "عذب"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "غَلِيظٌ", "translit": "ٌẓyِlَغ", "root": "غلظ"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 18, "text": "مَّثَلُ ٱلَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا۟ بِرَبِّهِمْۖ أَعْمَٰلُهُمْ كَرَمَادٍ ٱشْتَدَّتْ بِهِ ٱلرِّيحُ فِى يَوْمٍ عَاصِفٍۖ لَّا يَقْدِرُونَ مِمَّا كَسَبُوا۟ عَلَىٰ شَىْءٍۚ ذَٰلِكَ هُوَ ٱلضَّلَٰلُ ٱلْبَعِيدُ ", "translation": "The example of those who disbelieve in their Lord is [that] their deeds are like ashes which the wind blows forcefully on a stormy day; they are unable [to keep] from what they earned a [single] thing. That is what is extreme error.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "مَّثَلُ", "translit": "ُlَhtَّm", "root": "مثل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "كَفَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَfَk", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بِرَبِّهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِّbَrِb", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَعْمَٰلُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُlَٰmْʿعَaʾ", "root": "عمل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "كَرَمَادٍ", "translit": "ٍdāَmَrَk", "root": "رمد"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱشْتَدَّتْ", "translit": "ْtَّdَtْhsٱ", "root": "شدد"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "بِهِ", "translit": "ِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلرِّيحُ", "translit": "ُḥyِّrlٱ", "root": "روح"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "يَوْمٍ", "translit": "ٍmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "عَاصِفٍ", "translit": "ٍfِṣāَʿع", "root": "عصف"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "لَّا", "translit": "āَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "يَقْدِرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrِdْqَy", "root": "قدر"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "مِمَّا", "translit": "āَّmِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "كَسَبُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُbَsَk", "root": "كسب"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "شَىْءٍ", "translit": "ٍءْàَhs", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "هُوَ", "translit": "َwُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "ٱلضَّلَٰلُ", "translit": "ُlَٰlَّḍlٱ", "root": "ضلل"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "ٱلْبَعِيدُ", "translit": "ُdyِʿعَbْlٱ", "root": "بعد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 19, "text": "أَلَمْ تَرَ أَنَّ ٱللَّهَ خَلَقَ ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ وَٱلْأَرْضَ بِٱلْحَقِّۚ إِن يَشَأْ يُذْهِبْكُمْ وَيَأْتِ بِخَلْقٍ جَدِيدٍ ", "translation": "Have you not seen [i.e., considered] that Allāh created the heavens and the earth in truth? If He wills, He can do away with you and produce a new creation.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَلَمْ", "translit": "ْmَlَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تَرَ", "translit": "َrَt", "root": "راي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "خَلَقَ", "translit": "َqَlَhk", "root": "خلق"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰwَٰmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَٱلْأَرْضَ", "translit": "َḍْrَaʾْlٱَw", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "بِٱلْحَقِّ", "translit": "ِّqَḥْlٱِb", "root": "حقق"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "إِن", "translit": "nِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "يَشَأْ", "translit": "ْaʾَhsَy", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "يُذْهِبْكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkْbِhْhdُy", "root": "ذهب"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَيَأْتِ", "translit": "ِtْaʾَyَw", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "بِخَلْقٍ", "translit": "ٍqْlَhkِb", "root": "خلق"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "جَدِيدٍ", "translit": "ٍdyِdَjg", "root": "جدد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 20, "text": "وَمَا ذَٰلِكَ عَلَى ٱللَّهِ بِعَزِيزٍ ", "translation": "And that is not difficult for Allāh.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "عَلَى", "translit": "àَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بِعَزِيزٍ", "translit": "ٍzyِzَʿعِb", "root": "عزز"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 21, "text": "وَبَرَزُوا۟ لِلَّهِ جَمِيعًا فَقَالَ ٱلضُّعَفَٰٓؤُا۟ لِلَّذِينَ ٱسْتَكْبَرُوٓا۟ إِنَّا كُنَّا لَكُمْ تَبَعًا فَهَلْ أَنتُم مُّغْنُونَ عَنَّا مِنْ عَذَابِ ٱللَّهِ مِن شَىْءٍۚ قَالُوا۟ لَوْ هَدَىٰنَا ٱللَّهُ لَهَدَيْنَٰكُمْۖ سَوَآءٌ عَلَيْنَآ أَجَزِعْنَآ أَمْ صَبَرْنَا مَا لَنَا مِن مَّحِيصٍ ", "translation": "And they will come out [for judgement] before Allāh all together, and the weak will say to those who were arrogant, \"Indeed, we were your followers, so can you avail us anything against the punishment of Allāh?\" They will say, \"If Allāh had guided us, we would have guided you. It is all the same for us whether we show intolerance or are patient: there is for us no place of escape.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَبَرَزُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُzَrَbَw", "root": "برز"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لِلَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّlِl", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "جَمِيعًا", "translit": "āًʿعyِmَjg", "root": "جمع"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فَقَالَ", "translit": "َlāَqَf", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلضُّعَفَٰٓؤُا۟", "translit": "۟āُwʾَٰٓfَʿعُّḍlٱ", "root": "ضعف"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لِلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlِl", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱسْتَكْبَرُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُrَbْkَtْsٱ", "root": "كبر"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "إِنَّا", "translit": "āَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "كُنَّا", "translit": "āَّnُk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "لَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "تَبَعًا", "translit": "āًʿعَbَt", "root": "تبع"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "فَهَلْ", "translit": "ْlَhَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "أَنتُم", "translit": "mُtnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "مُّغْنُونَ", "translit": "َnwُnْغُّm", "root": "غني"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "عَنَّا", "translit": "āَّnَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "مِنْ", "translit": "ْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "عَذَابِ", "translit": "ِbāَhdَʿع", "root": "عذب"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "شَىْءٍ", "translit": "ٍءْàَhs", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "قَالُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "لَوْ", "translit": "ْwَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "هَدَىٰنَا", "translit": "āَnٰàَdَh", "root": "هدي"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "لَهَدَيْنَٰكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَٰnْyَdَhَl", "root": "هدي"}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "سَوَآءٌ", "translit": "ٌءٓāَwَs", "root": "سوي"}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "عَلَيْنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 28, "arabic": "أَجَزِعْنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnْʿعِzَjgَaʾ", "root": "جزع"}, {"id": 29, "arabic": "أَمْ", "translit": "ْmَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 30, "arabic": "صَبَرْنَا", "translit": "āَnْrَbَṣ", "root": "صبر"}, {"id": 31, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 32, "arabic": "لَنَا", "translit": "āَnَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 33, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 34, "arabic": "مَّحِيصٍ", "translit": "ٍṣyِḥَّm", "root": "حيص"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 22, "text": "وَقَالَ ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ لَمَّا قُضِىَ ٱلْأَمْرُ إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ وَعَدَكُمْ وَعْدَ ٱلْحَقِّ وَوَعَدتُّكُمْ فَأَخْلَفْتُكُمْۖ وَمَا كَانَ لِىَ عَلَيْكُم مِّن سُلْطَٰنٍ إِلَّآ أَن دَعَوْتُكُمْ فَٱسْتَجَبْتُمْ لِىۖ فَلَا تَلُومُونِى وَلُومُوٓا۟ أَنفُسَكُمۖ مَّآ أَنَا۠ بِمُصْرِخِكُمْ وَمَآ أَنتُم بِمُصْرِخِىَّۖ إِنِّى كَفَرْتُ بِمَآ أَشْرَكْتُمُونِ مِن قَبْلُۗ إِنَّ ٱلظَّٰلِمِينَ لَهُمْ عَذَابٌ أَلِيمٌ ", "translation": "And Satan will say when the matter has been concluded, \"Indeed, Allāh had promised you the promise of truth. And I promised you, but I betrayed you. But I had no authority over you except that I invited you, and you responded to me. So do not blame me; but blame yourselves. I cannot be called to your aid, nor can you be called to my aid. Indeed, I deny your association of me [with Allāh] before.[1] Indeed, for the wrongdoers is a painful punishment.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَقَالَ", "translit": "َlāَqَw", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ", "translit": "ُnَٰṭْyَّhslٱ", "root": "شطن"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لَمَّا", "translit": "āَّmَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "قُضِىَ", "translit": "َàِḍُq", "root": "قضي"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْأَمْرُ", "translit": "ُrْmَaʾْlٱ", "root": "امر"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَعَدَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَdَʿعَw", "root": "وعد"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَعْدَ", "translit": "َdْʿعَw", "root": "وعد"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلْحَقِّ", "translit": "ِّqَḥْlٱ", "root": "حقق"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَوَعَدتُّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkُّtdَʿعَwَw", "root": "وعد"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "فَأَخْلَفْتُكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkُtْfَlْhkَaʾَf", "root": "خلف"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "لِىَ", "translit": "َàِl", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "عَلَيْكُم", "translit": "mُkْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "سُلْطَٰنٍ", "translit": "ٍnَٰṭْlُs", "root": "سلط"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "إِلَّآ", "translit": "ٓāَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "دَعَوْتُكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkُtْwَʿعَd", "root": "دعو"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "فَٱسْتَجَبْتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtْbَjgَtْsٱَf", "root": "جوب"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "لِى", "translit": "àِl", "root": ""}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "فَلَا", "translit": "āَlَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "تَلُومُونِى", "translit": "àِnwُmwُlَt", "root": "لوم"}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "وَلُومُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُmwُlَw", "root": "لوم"}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "أَنفُسَكُم", "translit": "mُkَsُfnَaʾ", "root": "نفس"}, {"id": 28, "arabic": "مَّآ", "translit": "ٓāَّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 29, "arabic": "أَنَا۠", "translit": "۠āَnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 30, "arabic": "بِمُصْرِخِكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkِhkِrْṣُmِb", "root": "صرخ"}, {"id": 31, "arabic": "وَمَآ", "translit": "ٓāَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 32, "arabic": "أَنتُم", "translit": "mُtnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 33, "arabic": "بِمُصْرِخِىَّ", "translit": "َّàِhkِrْṣُmِb", "root": "صرخ"}, {"id": 34, "arabic": "إِنِّى", "translit": "àِّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 35, "arabic": "كَفَرْتُ", "translit": "ُtْrَfَk", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 36, "arabic": "بِمَآ", "translit": "ٓāَmِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 37, "arabic": "أَشْرَكْتُمُونِ", "translit": "ِnwُmُtْkَrْhsَaʾ", "root": "شرك"}, {"id": 38, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 39, "arabic": "قَبْلُ", "translit": "ُlْbَq", "root": "قبل"}, {"id": 40, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 41, "arabic": "ٱلظَّٰلِمِينَ", "translit": "َnyِmِlَّٰẓlٱ", "root": "ظلم"}, {"id": 42, "arabic": "لَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 43, "arabic": "عَذَابٌ", "translit": "ٌbāَhdَʿع", "root": "عذب"}, {"id": 44, "arabic": "أَلِيمٌ", "translit": "ٌmyِlَaʾ", "root": "الم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 23, "text": "وَأُدْخِلَ ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ وَعَمِلُوا۟ ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ جَنَّٰتٍ تَجْرِى مِن تَحْتِهَا ٱلْأَنْهَٰرُ خَٰلِدِينَ فِيهَا بِإِذْنِ رَبِّهِمْۖ تَحِيَّتُهُمْ فِيهَا سَلَٰمٌ ", "translation": "And those who believed and did righteous deeds will be admitted to gardens beneath which rivers flow, abiding eternally therein by permission of their Lord; and their greeting therein will be, \"Peace!\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَأُدْخِلَ", "translit": "َlِhkْdُaʾَw", "root": "دخل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَعَمِلُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlِmَʿعَw", "root": "عمل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰḥِlَّٰṣlٱ", "root": "صلح"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "جَنَّٰتٍ", "translit": "ٍtَّٰnَjg", "root": "جنن"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "تَجْرِى", "translit": "àِrْjgَt", "root": "جري"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "تَحْتِهَا", "translit": "āَhِtْḥَt", "root": "تحت"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلْأَنْهَٰرُ", "translit": "ُrَٰhْnَaʾْlٱ", "root": "نهر"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "خَٰلِدِينَ", "translit": "َnyِdِlَٰhk", "root": "خلد"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "فِيهَا", "translit": "āَhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "بِإِذْنِ", "translit": "ِnْhdِiʾِb", "root": "اذن"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "رَبِّهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "تَحِيَّتُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُtَّyِḥَt", "root": "حيي"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "فِيهَا", "translit": "āَhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "سَلَٰمٌ", "translit": "ٌmَٰlَs", "root": "سلم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 24, "text": "أَلَمْ تَرَ كَيْفَ ضَرَبَ ٱللَّهُ مَثَلًا كَلِمَةً طَيِّبَةً كَشَجَرَةٍ طَيِّبَةٍ أَصْلُهَا ثَابِتٌ وَفَرْعُهَا فِى ٱلسَّمَآءِ ", "translation": "Have you not considered how Allāh presents an example, [making] a good word like a good tree, whose root is firmly fixed and its branches [high] in the sky?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَلَمْ", "translit": "ْmَlَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تَرَ", "translit": "َrَt", "root": "راي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "كَيْفَ", "translit": "َfْyَk", "root": "كيف"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ضَرَبَ", "translit": "َbَrَḍ", "root": "ضرب"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مَثَلًا", "translit": "āًlَhtَm", "root": "مثل"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "كَلِمَةً", "translit": "ًẗَmِlَk", "root": "كلم"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "طَيِّبَةً", "translit": "ًẗَbِّyَṭ", "root": "طيب"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "كَشَجَرَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَrَjgَhsَk", "root": "شجر"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "طَيِّبَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَbِّyَṭ", "root": "طيب"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "أَصْلُهَا", "translit": "āَhُlْṣَaʾ", "root": "اصل"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ثَابِتٌ", "translit": "ٌtِbāَht", "root": "ثبت"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "وَفَرْعُهَا", "translit": "āَhُʿعْrَfَw", "root": "فرع"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَآءِ", "translit": "ِءٓāَmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 25, "text": "تُؤْتِىٓ أُكُلَهَا كُلَّ حِينٍۭ بِإِذْنِ رَبِّهَاۗ وَيَضْرِبُ ٱللَّهُ ٱلْأَمْثَالَ لِلنَّاسِ لَعَلَّهُمْ يَتَذَكَّرُونَ ", "translation": "It produces its fruit all the time, by permission of its Lord. And Allāh presents examples for the people that perhaps they will be reminded.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "تُؤْتِىٓ", "translit": "ٓàِtْwʾُt", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أُكُلَهَا", "translit": "āَhَlُkُaʾ", "root": "اكل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "كُلَّ", "translit": "َّlُk", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "حِينٍۭ", "translit": "ٍۭnyِḥ", "root": "حين"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بِإِذْنِ", "translit": "ِnْhdِiʾِb", "root": "اذن"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "رَبِّهَا", "translit": "āَhِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَيَضْرِبُ", "translit": "ُbِrْḍَyَw", "root": "ضرب"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلْأَمْثَالَ", "translit": "َlāَhtْmَaʾْlٱ", "root": "مثل"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "لِلنَّاسِ", "translit": "ِsāَّnlِl", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "لَعَلَّهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَّlَʿعَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "يَتَذَكَّرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrَّkَhdَtَy", "root": "ذكر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 26, "text": "وَمَثَلُ كَلِمَةٍ خَبِيثَةٍ كَشَجَرَةٍ خَبِيثَةٍ ٱجْتُثَّتْ مِن فَوْقِ ٱلْأَرْضِ مَا لَهَا مِن قَرَارٍ ", "translation": "And the example of a bad word is like a bad tree, uprooted from the surface of the earth, not having any stability.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمَثَلُ", "translit": "ُlَhtَmَw", "root": "مثل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "كَلِمَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَmِlَk", "root": "كلم"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "خَبِيثَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَhtyِbَhk", "root": "خبث"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "كَشَجَرَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَrَjgَhsَk", "root": "شجر"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "خَبِيثَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَhtyِbَhk", "root": "خبث"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱجْتُثَّتْ", "translit": "ْtَّhtُtْjgٱ", "root": "جثث"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فَوْقِ", "translit": "ِqْwَf", "root": "فوق"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "لَهَا", "translit": "āَhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "قَرَارٍ", "translit": "ٍrāَrَq", "root": "قرر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 27, "text": "يُثَبِّتُ ٱللَّهُ ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ بِٱلْقَوْلِ ٱلثَّابِتِ فِى ٱلْحَيَوٰةِ ٱلدُّنْيَا وَفِى ٱلْءَاخِرَةِۖ وَيُضِلُّ ٱللَّهُ ٱلظَّٰلِمِينَۚ وَيَفْعَلُ ٱللَّهُ مَا يَشَآءُ ", "translation": "Allāh keeps firm those who believe, with the firm word,[1] in worldly life and in the Hereafter.[2] And Allāh sends astray the wrongdoers. And Allāh does what He wills.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يُثَبِّتُ", "translit": "ُtِّbَhtُy", "root": "ثبت"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بِٱلْقَوْلِ", "translit": "ِlْwَqْlٱِb", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلثَّابِتِ", "translit": "ِtِbāَّhtlٱ", "root": "ثبت"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلْحَيَوٰةِ", "translit": "ِẗٰwَyَḥْlٱ", "root": "حيي"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلدُّنْيَا", "translit": "āَyْnُّdlٱ", "root": "دنو"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَفِى", "translit": "àِfَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلْءَاخِرَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَrِhkāَءْlٱ", "root": "اخر"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَيُضِلُّ", "translit": "ُّlِḍُyَw", "root": "ضلل"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "ٱلظَّٰلِمِينَ", "translit": "َnyِmِlَّٰẓlٱ", "root": "ظلم"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "وَيَفْعَلُ", "translit": "ُlَʿعْfَyَw", "root": "فعل"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "يَشَآءُ", "translit": "ُءٓāَhsَy", "root": "شيا"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 28, "text": "أَلَمْ تَرَ إِلَى ٱلَّذِينَ بَدَّلُوا۟ نِعْمَتَ ٱللَّهِ كُفْرًا وَأَحَلُّوا۟ قَوْمَهُمْ دَارَ ٱلْبَوَارِ ", "translation": "Have you not considered those who exchanged the favor of Allāh for disbelief[1] and settled their people [in] the home of ruin?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَلَمْ", "translit": "ْmَlَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تَرَ", "translit": "َrَt", "root": "راي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "إِلَى", "translit": "àَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بَدَّلُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlَّdَb", "root": "بدل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "نِعْمَتَ", "translit": "َtَmْʿعِn", "root": "نعم"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "كُفْرًا", "translit": "āًrْfُk", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَأَحَلُّوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُّlَḥَaʾَw", "root": "حلل"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "قَوْمَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَmْwَq", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "دَارَ", "translit": "َrāَd", "root": "دور"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلْبَوَارِ", "translit": "ِrāَwَbْlٱ", "root": "بور"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 29, "text": "جَهَنَّمَ يَصْلَوْنَهَاۖ وَبِئْسَ ٱلْقَرَارُ ", "translation": "[It is] Hell, which they will [enter to] burn, and wretched is the settlement.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "جَهَنَّمَ", "translit": "َmَّnَhَjg", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَصْلَوْنَهَا", "translit": "āَhَnْwَlْṣَy", "root": "صلي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَبِئْسَ", "translit": "َsْyʾِbَw", "root": "باس"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْقَرَارُ", "translit": "ُrāَrَqْlٱ", "root": "قرر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 30, "text": "وَجَعَلُوا۟ لِلَّهِ أَندَادًا لِّيُضِلُّوا۟ عَن سَبِيلِهِۦۗ قُلْ تَمَتَّعُوا۟ فَإِنَّ مَصِيرَكُمْ إِلَى ٱلنَّارِ ", "translation": "And they have attributed to Allāh equals to mislead [people] from His way. Say, \"Enjoy yourselves, for indeed, your destination is the Fire.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَجَعَلُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlَʿعَjgَw", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لِلَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّlِl", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَندَادًا", "translit": "āًdāَdnَaʾ", "root": "ندد"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لِّيُضِلُّوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُّlِḍُyِّl", "root": "ضلل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "عَن", "translit": "nَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "سَبِيلِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِlyِbَs", "root": "سبل"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "قُلْ", "translit": "ْlُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "تَمَتَّعُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُʿعَّtَmَt", "root": "متع"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "فَإِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "مَصِيرَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَryِṣَm", "root": "صير"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "إِلَى", "translit": "àَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلنَّارِ", "translit": "ِrāَّnlٱ", "root": "نور"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 31, "text": "قُل لِّعِبَادِىَ ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ يُقِيمُوا۟ ٱلصَّلَوٰةَ وَيُنفِقُوا۟ مِمَّا رَزَقْنَٰهُمْ سِرًّا وَعَلَانِيَةً مِّن قَبْلِ أَن يَأْتِىَ يَوْمٌ لَّا بَيْعٌ فِيهِ وَلَا خِلَٰلٌ ", "translation": "[O Muḥammad], tell My servants who have believed to establish prayer and spend from what We have provided them, secretly and publicly, before a Day comes in which there will be no exchange [i.e., ransom], nor any friendships.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قُل", "translit": "lُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لِّعِبَادِىَ", "translit": "َàِdāَbِʿعِّl", "root": "عبد"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "يُقِيمُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُmyِqُy", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلصَّلَوٰةَ", "translit": "َẗٰwَlَّṣlٱ", "root": "صلو"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَيُنفِقُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُqِfnُyَw", "root": "نفق"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مِمَّا", "translit": "āَّmِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "رَزَقْنَٰهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَٰnْqَzَr", "root": "رزق"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "سِرًّا", "translit": "āًّrِs", "root": "سرر"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَعَلَانِيَةً", "translit": "ًẗَyِnāَlَʿعَw", "root": "علن"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "قَبْلِ", "translit": "ِlْbَq", "root": "قبل"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "يَأْتِىَ", "translit": "َàِtْaʾَy", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "يَوْمٌ", "translit": "ٌmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "لَّا", "translit": "āَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "بَيْعٌ", "translit": "ٌʿعْyَb", "root": "بيع"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "فِيهِ", "translit": "ِhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "خِلَٰلٌ", "translit": "ٌlَٰlِhk", "root": "خلل"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 32, "text": "ٱللَّهُ ٱلَّذِى خَلَقَ ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ وَٱلْأَرْضَ وَأَنزَلَ مِنَ ٱلسَّمَآءِ مَآءً فَأَخْرَجَ بِهِۦ مِنَ ٱلثَّمَرَٰتِ رِزْقًا لَّكُمْۖ وَسَخَّرَ لَكُمُ ٱلْفُلْكَ لِتَجْرِىَ فِى ٱلْبَحْرِ بِأَمْرِهِۦۖ وَسَخَّرَ لَكُمُ ٱلْأَنْهَٰرَ ", "translation": "It is Allāh who created the heavens and the earth and sent down rain from the sky and produced thereby some fruits as provision for you and subjected for you[1] the ships to sail through the sea by His command and subjected for you the rivers.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِى", "translit": "àِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "خَلَقَ", "translit": "َqَlَhk", "root": "خلق"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰwَٰmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَٱلْأَرْضَ", "translit": "َḍْrَaʾْlٱَw", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَأَنزَلَ", "translit": "َlَznَaʾَw", "root": "نزل"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَآءِ", "translit": "ِءٓāَmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "مَآءً", "translit": "ًءٓāَm", "root": "موه"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "فَأَخْرَجَ", "translit": "َjgَrْhkَaʾَf", "root": "خرج"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "بِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱلثَّمَرَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰrَmَّhtlٱ", "root": "ثمر"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "رِزْقًا", "translit": "āًqْzِr", "root": "رزق"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "لَّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "وَسَخَّرَ", "translit": "َrَّhkَsَw", "root": "سخر"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "لَكُمُ", "translit": "ُmُkَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "ٱلْفُلْكَ", "translit": "َkْlُfْlٱ", "root": "فلك"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "لِتَجْرِىَ", "translit": "َàِrْjgَtِl", "root": "جري"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "ٱلْبَحْرِ", "translit": "ِrْḥَbْlٱ", "root": "بحر"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "بِأَمْرِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِrْmَaʾِb", "root": "امر"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "وَسَخَّرَ", "translit": "َrَّhkَsَw", "root": "سخر"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "لَكُمُ", "translit": "ُmُkَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "ٱلْأَنْهَٰرَ", "translit": "َrَٰhْnَaʾْlٱ", "root": "نهر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 33, "text": "وَسَخَّرَ لَكُمُ ٱلشَّمْسَ وَٱلْقَمَرَ دَآئِبَيْنِۖ وَسَخَّرَ لَكُمُ ٱلَّيْلَ وَٱلنَّهَارَ ", "translation": "And He subjected for you the sun and the moon, continuous [in orbit], and subjected for you the night and the day.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَسَخَّرَ", "translit": "َrَّhkَsَw", "root": "سخر"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَكُمُ", "translit": "ُmُkَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلشَّمْسَ", "translit": "َsْmَّhslٱ", "root": "شمس"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَٱلْقَمَرَ", "translit": "َrَmَqْlٱَw", "root": "قمر"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "دَآئِبَيْنِ", "translit": "ِnْyَbِyʾٓāَd", "root": "داب"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَسَخَّرَ", "translit": "َrَّhkَsَw", "root": "سخر"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لَكُمُ", "translit": "ُmُkَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلَّيْلَ", "translit": "َlْyَّlٱ", "root": "ليل"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَٱلنَّهَارَ", "translit": "َrāَhَّnlٱَw", "root": "نهر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 34, "text": "وَءَاتَىٰكُم مِّن كُلِّ مَا سَأَلْتُمُوهُۚ وَإِن تَعُدُّوا۟ نِعْمَتَ ٱللَّهِ لَا تُحْصُوهَآۗ إِنَّ ٱلْإِنسَٰنَ لَظَلُومٌ كَفَّارٌ ", "translation": "And He gave you from all you asked of Him.[1] And if you should count the favor [i.e., blessings] of Allāh, you could not enumerate them. Indeed, mankind is [generally] most unjust and ungrateful.[2]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَءَاتَىٰكُم", "translit": "mُkٰàَtāَءَw", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "كُلِّ", "translit": "ِّlُk", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "سَأَلْتُمُوهُ", "translit": "ُhwُmُtْlَaʾَs", "root": "سال"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَإِن", "translit": "nِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "تَعُدُّوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُّdُʿعَt", "root": "عدد"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "نِعْمَتَ", "translit": "َtَmْʿعِn", "root": "نعم"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "تُحْصُوهَآ", "translit": "ٓāَhwُṣْḥُt", "root": "حصي"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱلْإِنسَٰنَ", "translit": "َnَٰsnِiʾْlٱ", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "لَظَلُومٌ", "translit": "ٌmwُlَẓَl", "root": "ظلم"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "كَفَّارٌ", "translit": "ٌrāَّfَk", "root": "كفر"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 35, "text": "وَإِذْ قَالَ إِبْرَٰهِيمُ رَبِّ ٱجْعَلْ هَٰذَا ٱلْبَلَدَ ءَامِنًا وَٱجْنُبْنِى وَبَنِىَّ أَن نَّعْبُدَ ٱلْأَصْنَامَ ", "translation": "And [mention, O Muḥammad], when Abraham said, \"My Lord, make this city [i.e., Makkah] secure and keep me and my sons away from worshipping idols.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَإِذْ", "translit": "ْhdِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "قَالَ", "translit": "َlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "إِبْرَٰهِيمُ", "translit": "ُmyِhَٰrْbِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "رَبِّ", "translit": "ِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱجْعَلْ", "translit": "ْlَʿعْjgٱ", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "هَٰذَا", "translit": "āَhdَٰh", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلْبَلَدَ", "translit": "َdَlَbْlٱ", "root": "بلد"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ءَامِنًا", "translit": "āًnِmāَء", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَٱجْنُبْنِى", "translit": "àِnْbُnْjgٱَw", "root": "جنب"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَبَنِىَّ", "translit": "َّàِnَbَw", "root": "بني"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "نَّعْبُدَ", "translit": "َdُbْʿعَّn", "root": "عبد"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱلْأَصْنَامَ", "translit": "َmāَnْṣَaʾْlٱ", "root": "صنم"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 36, "text": "رَبِّ إِنَّهُنَّ أَضْلَلْنَ كَثِيرًا مِّنَ ٱلنَّاسِۖ فَمَن تَبِعَنِى فَإِنَّهُۥ مِنِّىۖ وَمَنْ عَصَانِى فَإِنَّكَ غَفُورٌ رَّحِيمٌ ", "translation": "My Lord, indeed they have led astray many among the people. So whoever follows me - then he is of me;[1] and whoever disobeys me - indeed, You are [yet] Forgiving and Merciful.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "رَبِّ", "translit": "ِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِنَّهُنَّ", "translit": "َّnُhَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَضْلَلْنَ", "translit": "َnْlَlْḍَaʾ", "root": "ضلل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "كَثِيرًا", "translit": "āًryِhtَk", "root": "كثر"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِّنَ", "translit": "َnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلنَّاسِ", "translit": "ِsāَّnlٱ", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فَمَن", "translit": "nَmَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "تَبِعَنِى", "translit": "àِnَʿعِbَt", "root": "تبع"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "فَإِنَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّnِiʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "مِنِّى", "translit": "àِّnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَمَنْ", "translit": "ْnَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "عَصَانِى", "translit": "àِnāَṣَʿع", "root": "عصي"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "فَإِنَّكَ", "translit": "َkَّnِiʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "غَفُورٌ", "translit": "ٌrwُfَغ", "root": "غفر"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "رَّحِيمٌ", "translit": "ٌmyِḥَّr", "root": "رحم"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 37, "text": "رَّبَّنَآ إِنِّىٓ أَسْكَنتُ مِن ذُرِّيَّتِى بِوَادٍ غَيْرِ ذِى زَرْعٍ عِندَ بَيْتِكَ ٱلْمُحَرَّمِ رَبَّنَا لِيُقِيمُوا۟ ٱلصَّلَوٰةَ فَٱجْعَلْ أَفْـِٔدَةً مِّنَ ٱلنَّاسِ تَهْوِىٓ إِلَيْهِمْ وَٱرْزُقْهُم مِّنَ ٱلثَّمَرَٰتِ لَعَلَّهُمْ يَشْكُرُونَ ", "translation": "Our Lord, I have settled some of my descendants in an uncultivated valley near Your sacred House, our Lord, that they may establish prayer. So make hearts among the people incline toward them and provide for them from the fruits that they might be grateful.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "رَّبَّنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnَّbَّr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِنِّىٓ", "translit": "ٓàِّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَسْكَنتُ", "translit": "ُtnَkْsَaʾ", "root": "سكن"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ذُرِّيَّتِى", "translit": "àِtَّyِّrُhd", "root": "ذرر"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "بِوَادٍ", "translit": "ٍdāَwِb", "root": "ودي"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "غَيْرِ", "translit": "ِrْyَغ", "root": "غير"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ذِى", "translit": "àِhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "زَرْعٍ", "translit": "ٍʿعْrَz", "root": "زرع"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "عِندَ", "translit": "َdnِʿع", "root": "عند"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "بَيْتِكَ", "translit": "َkِtْyَb", "root": "بيت"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلْمُحَرَّمِ", "translit": "ِmَّrَḥُmْlٱ", "root": "حرم"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "رَبَّنَا", "translit": "āَnَّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "لِيُقِيمُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُmyِqُyِl", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ٱلصَّلَوٰةَ", "translit": "َẗٰwَlَّṣlٱ", "root": "صلو"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "فَٱجْعَلْ", "translit": "ْlَʿعْjgٱَf", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "أَفْـِٔدَةً", "translit": "ًẗَdِٔـْfَaʾ", "root": "فاد"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "مِّنَ", "translit": "َnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "ٱلنَّاسِ", "translit": "ِsāَّnlٱ", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "تَهْوِىٓ", "translit": "ٓàِwْhَt", "root": "هوي"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "إِلَيْهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "وَٱرْزُقْهُم", "translit": "mُhْqُzْrٱَw", "root": "رزق"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "مِّنَ", "translit": "َnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "ٱلثَّمَرَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰrَmَّhtlٱ", "root": "ثمر"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "لَعَلَّهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَّlَʿعَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "يَشْكُرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrُkْhsَy", "root": "شكر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 38, "text": "رَبَّنَآ إِنَّكَ تَعْلَمُ مَا نُخْفِى وَمَا نُعْلِنُۗ وَمَا يَخْفَىٰ عَلَى ٱللَّهِ مِن شَىْءٍ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ وَلَا فِى ٱلسَّمَآءِ ", "translation": "Our Lord, indeed You know what we conceal and what we declare, and nothing is hidden from Allāh on the earth or in the heaven.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "رَبَّنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnَّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِنَّكَ", "translit": "َkَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "تَعْلَمُ", "translit": "ُmَlْʿعَt", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "نُخْفِى", "translit": "àِfْhkُn", "root": "خفي"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "نُعْلِنُ", "translit": "ُnِlْʿعُn", "root": "علن"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "يَخْفَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَfْhkَy", "root": "خفي"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "عَلَى", "translit": "àَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "شَىْءٍ", "translit": "ٍءْàَhs", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَآءِ", "translit": "ِءٓāَmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 39, "text": "ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ ٱلَّذِى وَهَبَ لِى عَلَى ٱلْكِبَرِ إِسْمَٰعِيلَ وَإِسْحَٰقَۚ إِنَّ رَبِّى لَسَمِيعُ ٱلدُّعَآءِ ", "translation": "Praise to Allāh, who has granted to me in old age Ishmael and Isaac. Indeed, my Lord is the Hearer of supplication.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ٱلْحَمْدُ", "translit": "ُdْmَḥْlٱ", "root": "حمد"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لِلَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّlِl", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِى", "translit": "àِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَهَبَ", "translit": "َbَhَw", "root": "وهب"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لِى", "translit": "àِl", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "عَلَى", "translit": "àَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلْكِبَرِ", "translit": "ِrَbِkْlٱ", "root": "كبر"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "إِسْمَٰعِيلَ", "translit": "َlyِʿعَٰmْsِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَإِسْحَٰقَ", "translit": "َqَٰḥْsِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "رَبِّى", "translit": "àِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "لَسَمِيعُ", "translit": "ُʿعyِmَsَl", "root": "سمع"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱلدُّعَآءِ", "translit": "ِءٓāَʿعُّdlٱ", "root": "دعو"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 40, "text": "رَبِّ ٱجْعَلْنِى مُقِيمَ ٱلصَّلَوٰةِ وَمِن ذُرِّيَّتِىۚ رَبَّنَا وَتَقَبَّلْ دُعَآءِ ", "translation": "My Lord, make me an establisher of prayer, and [many] from my descendants. Our Lord, and accept my supplication.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "رَبِّ", "translit": "ِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱجْعَلْنِى", "translit": "àِnْlَʿعْjgٱ", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مُقِيمَ", "translit": "َmyِqُm", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلصَّلَوٰةِ", "translit": "ِẗٰwَlَّṣlٱ", "root": "صلو"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَمِن", "translit": "nِmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ذُرِّيَّتِى", "translit": "àِtَّyِّrُhd", "root": "ذرر"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "رَبَّنَا", "translit": "āَnَّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَتَقَبَّلْ", "translit": "ْlَّbَqَtَw", "root": "قبل"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "دُعَآءِ", "translit": "ِءٓāَʿعُd", "root": "دعو"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 41, "text": "رَبَّنَا ٱغْفِرْ لِى وَلِوَٰلِدَىَّ وَلِلْمُؤْمِنِينَ يَوْمَ يَقُومُ ٱلْحِسَابُ ", "translation": "Our Lord, forgive me and my parents and the believers the Day the account is established.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "رَبَّنَا", "translit": "āَnَّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱغْفِرْ", "translit": "ْrِfْغٱ", "root": "غفر"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لِى", "translit": "àِl", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَلِوَٰلِدَىَّ", "translit": "َّàَdِlَٰwِlَw", "root": "ولد"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَلِلْمُؤْمِنِينَ", "translit": "َnyِnِmْwʾُmْlِlَw", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "يَوْمَ", "translit": "َmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "يَقُومُ", "translit": "ُmwُqَy", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلْحِسَابُ", "translit": "ُbāَsِḥْlٱ", "root": "حسب"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 42, "text": "وَلَا تَحْسَبَنَّ ٱللَّهَ غَٰفِلًا عَمَّا يَعْمَلُ ٱلظَّٰلِمُونَۚ إِنَّمَا يُؤَخِّرُهُمْ لِيَوْمٍ تَشْخَصُ فِيهِ ٱلْأَبْصَٰرُ ", "translation": "And never think that Allāh is unaware of what the wrongdoers do. He only delays them [i.e., their account] for a Day when eyes will stare [in horror].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تَحْسَبَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَbَsْḥَt", "root": "حسب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "غَٰفِلًا", "translit": "āًlِfَٰغ", "root": "غفل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "عَمَّا", "translit": "āَّmَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "يَعْمَلُ", "translit": "ُlَmْʿعَy", "root": "عمل"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلظَّٰلِمُونَ", "translit": "َnwُmِlَّٰẓlٱ", "root": "ظلم"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "إِنَّمَا", "translit": "āَmَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "يُؤَخِّرُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُrِّhkَwʾُy", "root": "اخر"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "لِيَوْمٍ", "translit": "ٍmْwَyِl", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "تَشْخَصُ", "translit": "ُṣَhkْhsَt", "root": "شخص"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "فِيهِ", "translit": "ِhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱلْأَبْصَٰرُ", "translit": "ُrَٰṣْbَaʾْlٱ", "root": "بصر"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 43, "text": "مُهْطِعِينَ مُقْنِعِى رُءُوسِهِمْ لَا يَرْتَدُّ إِلَيْهِمْ طَرْفُهُمْۖ وَأَفْـِٔدَتُهُمْ هَوَآءٌ ", "translation": "Racing ahead, their heads raised up, their glance does not come back to them,[1] and their hearts are void.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "مُهْطِعِينَ", "translit": "َnyِʿعِṭْhُm", "root": "هطع"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مُقْنِعِى", "translit": "àِʿعِnْqُm", "root": "قنع"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "رُءُوسِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِswُءُr", "root": "راس"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "يَرْتَدُّ", "translit": "ُّdَtْrَy", "root": "ردد"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "إِلَيْهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "طَرْفُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُfْrَṭ", "root": "طرف"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَأَفْـِٔدَتُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُtَdِٔـْfَaʾَw", "root": "فاد"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "هَوَآءٌ", "translit": "ٌءٓāَwَh", "root": "هوي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 44, "text": "وَأَنذِرِ ٱلنَّاسَ يَوْمَ يَأْتِيهِمُ ٱلْعَذَابُ فَيَقُولُ ٱلَّذِينَ ظَلَمُوا۟ رَبَّنَآ أَخِّرْنَآ إِلَىٰٓ أَجَلٍ قَرِيبٍ نُّجِبْ دَعْوَتَكَ وَنَتَّبِعِ ٱلرُّسُلَۗ أَوَلَمْ تَكُونُوٓا۟ أَقْسَمْتُم مِّن قَبْلُ مَا لَكُم مِّن زَوَالٍ ", "translation": "And, [O Muḥammad], warn the people of a Day when the punishment will come to them and those who did wrong will say, \"Our Lord, delay us for a short term; we will answer Your call and follow the messengers.\" [But it will be said], \"Had you not sworn, before, that for you there would be no cessation?[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَأَنذِرِ", "translit": "ِrِhdnَaʾَw", "root": "نذر"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلنَّاسَ", "translit": "َsāَّnlٱ", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يَوْمَ", "translit": "َmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يَأْتِيهِمُ", "translit": "ُmِhyِtْaʾَy", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْعَذَابُ", "translit": "ُbāَhdَʿعْlٱ", "root": "عذب"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فَيَقُولُ", "translit": "ُlwُqَyَf", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ظَلَمُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُmَlَẓ", "root": "ظلم"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "رَبَّنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnَّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "أَخِّرْنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnْrِّhkَaʾ", "root": "اخر"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "إِلَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "أَجَلٍ", "translit": "ٍlَjgَaʾ", "root": "اجل"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "قَرِيبٍ", "translit": "ٍbyِrَq", "root": "قرب"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "نُّجِبْ", "translit": "ْbِjgُّn", "root": "جوب"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "دَعْوَتَكَ", "translit": "َkَtَwْʿعَd", "root": "دعو"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "وَنَتَّبِعِ", "translit": "ِʿعِbَّtَnَw", "root": "تبع"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "ٱلرُّسُلَ", "translit": "َlُsُّrlٱ", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "أَوَلَمْ", "translit": "ْmَlَwَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "تَكُونُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُnwُkَt", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "أَقْسَمْتُم", "translit": "mُtْmَsْqَaʾ", "root": "قسم"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "قَبْلُ", "translit": "ُlْbَq", "root": "قبل"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "لَكُم", "translit": "mُkَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "زَوَالٍ", "translit": "ٍlāَwَz", "root": "زول"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 45, "text": "وَسَكَنتُمْ فِى مَسَٰكِنِ ٱلَّذِينَ ظَلَمُوٓا۟ أَنفُسَهُمْ وَتَبَيَّنَ لَكُمْ كَيْفَ فَعَلْنَا بِهِمْ وَضَرَبْنَا لَكُمُ ٱلْأَمْثَالَ ", "translation": "And you lived among the dwellings of those who wronged themselves, and it had become clear to you how We dealt with them. And We presented for you [many] examples.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَسَكَنتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtnَkَsَw", "root": "سكن"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَسَٰكِنِ", "translit": "ِnِkَٰsَm", "root": "سكن"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ظَلَمُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُmَlَẓ", "root": "ظلم"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أَنفُسَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَsُfnَaʾ", "root": "نفس"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَتَبَيَّنَ", "translit": "َnَّyَbَtَw", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "لَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "كَيْفَ", "translit": "َfْyَk", "root": "كيف"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "فَعَلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlَʿعَf", "root": "فعل"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "بِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَضَرَبْنَا", "translit": "āَnْbَrَḍَw", "root": "ضرب"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "لَكُمُ", "translit": "ُmُkَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "ٱلْأَمْثَالَ", "translit": "َlāَhtْmَaʾْlٱ", "root": "مثل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 46, "text": "وَقَدْ مَكَرُوا۟ مَكْرَهُمْ وَعِندَ ٱللَّهِ مَكْرُهُمْ وَإِن كَانَ مَكْرُهُمْ لِتَزُولَ مِنْهُ ٱلْجِبَالُ ", "translation": "And they had planned their plan, but with Allāh is [recorded] their plan, even if their plan had been [sufficient] to do away with the mountains.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَكَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَkَm", "root": "مكر"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَكْرَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَrْkَm", "root": "مكر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَعِندَ", "translit": "َdnِʿعَw", "root": "عند"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مَكْرُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُrْkَm", "root": "مكر"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَإِن", "translit": "nِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "مَكْرُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُrْkَm", "root": "مكر"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "لِتَزُولَ", "translit": "َlwُzَtِl", "root": "زول"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "مِنْهُ", "translit": "ُhْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلْجِبَالُ", "translit": "ُlāَbِjgْlٱ", "root": "جبل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 47, "text": "فَلَا تَحْسَبَنَّ ٱللَّهَ مُخْلِفَ وَعْدِهِۦ رُسُلَهُۥٓۗ إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ عَزِيزٌ ذُو ٱنتِقَامٍ ", "translation": "So never think that Allāh will fail in His promise to His messengers. Indeed, Allāh is Exalted in Might and Owner of Retribution.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَلَا", "translit": "āَlَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تَحْسَبَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَbَsْḥَt", "root": "حسب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مُخْلِفَ", "translit": "َfِlْhkُm", "root": "خلف"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَعْدِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِdْʿعَw", "root": "وعد"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "رُسُلَهُۥٓ", "translit": "ٓۥُhَlُsُr", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "عَزِيزٌ", "translit": "ٌzyِzَʿع", "root": "عزز"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ذُو", "translit": "wُhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱنتِقَامٍ", "translit": "ٍmāَqِtnٱ", "root": "نقم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 48, "text": "يَوْمَ تُبَدَّلُ ٱلْأَرْضُ غَيْرَ ٱلْأَرْضِ وَٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتُۖ وَبَرَزُوا۟ لِلَّهِ ٱلْوَٰحِدِ ٱلْقَهَّارِ ", "translation": "[It will be] on the Day the earth will be replaced by another earth, and the heavens [as well], and they [i.e., all creatures] will come out before Allāh, the One, the Prevailing,", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَوْمَ", "translit": "َmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تُبَدَّلُ", "translit": "ُlَّdَbُt", "root": "بدل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضُ", "translit": "ُḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "غَيْرَ", "translit": "َrْyَغ", "root": "غير"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتُ", "translit": "ُtَٰwَٰmَّslٱَw", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَبَرَزُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُzَrَbَw", "root": "برز"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "لِلَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّlِl", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلْوَٰحِدِ", "translit": "ِdِḥَٰwْlٱ", "root": "وحد"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلْقَهَّارِ", "translit": "ِrāَّhَqْlٱ", "root": "قهر"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 49, "text": "وَتَرَى ٱلْمُجْرِمِينَ يَوْمَئِذٍ مُّقَرَّنِينَ فِى ٱلْأَصْفَادِ ", "translation": "And you will see the criminals that Day bound together in irons,", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَتَرَى", "translit": "àَrَtَw", "root": "راي"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْمُجْرِمِينَ", "translit": "َnyِmِrْjgُmْlٱ", "root": "جرم"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يَوْمَئِذٍ", "translit": "ٍhdِyʾَmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مُّقَرَّنِينَ", "translit": "َnyِnَّrَqُّm", "root": "قرن"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلْأَصْفَادِ", "translit": "ِdāَfْṣَaʾْlٱ", "root": "صفد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 50, "text": "سَرَابِيلُهُم مِّن قَطِرَانٍ وَتَغْشَىٰ وُجُوهَهُمُ ٱلنَّارُ ", "translation": "Their garments of liquid pitch and their faces covered by the Fire", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "سَرَابِيلُهُم", "translit": "mُhُlyِbāَrَs", "root": "سربل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "قَطِرَانٍ", "translit": "ٍnāَrِṭَq", "root": "قطر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَتَغْشَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَhsْغَtَw", "root": "غشو"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وُجُوهَهُمُ", "translit": "ُmُhَhwُjgُw", "root": "وجه"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلنَّارُ", "translit": "ُrāَّnlٱ", "root": "نور"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 51, "text": "لِيَجْزِىَ ٱللَّهُ كُلَّ نَفْسٍ مَّا كَسَبَتْۚ إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ سَرِيعُ ٱلْحِسَابِ ", "translation": "So that Allāh will recompense every soul for what it earned. Indeed, Allāh is swift in account.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لِيَجْزِىَ", "translit": "َàِzْjgَyِl", "root": "جزي"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "كُلَّ", "translit": "َّlُk", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "نَفْسٍ", "translit": "ٍsْfَn", "root": "نفس"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مَّا", "translit": "āَّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "كَسَبَتْ", "translit": "ْtَbَsَk", "root": "كسب"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "سَرِيعُ", "translit": "ُʿعyِrَs", "root": "سرع"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلْحِسَابِ", "translit": "ِbāَsِḥْlٱ", "root": "حسب"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 52, "text": "هَٰذَا بَلَٰغٌ لِّلنَّاسِ وَلِيُنذَرُوا۟ بِهِۦ وَلِيَعْلَمُوٓا۟ أَنَّمَا هُوَ إِلَٰهٌ وَٰحِدٌ وَلِيَذَّكَّرَ أُو۟لُوا۟ ٱلْأَلْبَٰبِ ", "translation": "This [Qur’ān] is notification for the people that they may be warned thereby and that they may know that He is but one God and that those of understanding will be reminded.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "هَٰذَا", "translit": "āَhdَٰh", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "بَلَٰغٌ", "translit": "ٌغَٰlَb", "root": "بلغ"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لِّلنَّاسِ", "translit": "ِsāَّnlِّl", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَلِيُنذَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَhdnُyِlَw", "root": "نذر"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَلِيَعْلَمُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُmَlْʿعَyِlَw", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَنَّمَا", "translit": "āَmَّnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "هُوَ", "translit": "َwُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "إِلَٰهٌ", "translit": "ٌhَٰlِiʾ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَٰحِدٌ", "translit": "ٌdِḥَٰw", "root": "وحد"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَلِيَذَّكَّرَ", "translit": "َrَّkَّhdَyِlَw", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "أُو۟لُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُl۟wُaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱلْأَلْبَٰبِ", "translit": "ِbَٰbْlَaʾْlٱ", "root": "لبب"}], "hasIhya": false}], "tafsir": {"1": [{"arabic": "{يخرجهم من الظلمات إلى النور} وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم يا أيها الناس اعقلوا عن ربكم وتواصوا بالعقل تعرفوا ما أمرتم به وما نهيتم عنه واعلموا أنه ينجدكم عند ربكم واعلموا أن العاقل من أطاع الله وإن كان دميم المنظر حقير الخطر دنيء المنزلة رث الهيئة وأن الجاهل من عصى الله تعالى وإن كان جميل المنظر عظيم الخطر شريف المنزلة حسن الهيئة فصيحا نطوقا فالقردة والخنازير أعقل عند الله تعالى ممن عصاه ولا تغتر بتعظيم أهل الدنيا إياهم فإنهم من الخاسرين (2) وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم أول ما خل", "english": "The Book of Seclusion ('Uzlah) — its explanation, God Most High willing. For this reason, Yūsuf ibn Asbāṭ wrote to Ḥudhayfah al-Marʿashī: \"What is your opinion of one who remains, finding no one with whom to remember God Most High except that he is either sinful, or his remembrance in company is itself an act of disobedience — and that because he finds no one of his own kind?\" And indeed he spoke truly, for mixing with people is never free from backbiting (ghībah), or listening to backbiting, or remaining silent upon wrongdoing (munkar). And the best of his states is that he either imparts knowledge or acquires it. Yet were this poor wretch to reflect, and to know that his imparting of knowledge is never free from the taints of ostentation (riyā'), the seeking of followers, and the desire for leadership (ri'āsah), he would know that the one who acquires knowledge from him only seeks to make it an instrument for pursuing the world and a means toward evil — so that the teacher becomes his helper therein, his support and his backer, preparing its causes for him, like one who sells a sword to a highway robber. For knowledge is like the sword: its soundness for good is like the soundness of the sword for warfare. And for this reason, it is not permitted to sell it to one whom one knows, by the indications of his circumstances, intends to use it to aid in cutting off the road.", "book_title": "Condemnation of Delusion", "section_title_ar": "Section 65", "section_title_en": "The Book of Seclusion ('Uzlah) — Section 65, and The Seventh Book: On the Intellect, Its Nobility, Its Reality, and Its Divisions", "section_index": 65, "anchors": "- Root: عقل (ʿaql)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes binding, restraining, and holding back; the intellect is that which restrains the soul from base desires and prevents it from straying.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العقل يقال للقوة المتهيئة لقبول العلم، ويقال للعلم الذي يستفيده الإنسان بتلك القوة عقل\" — The intellect is said of the faculty prepared for the reception of knowledge, and it is also said of the knowledge which man acquires by means of that faculty.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): intellectual apprehension and understanding. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"نخلة لا تعقل الإبار\" — a palm tree that does not accept pollination, i.e., does not grasp or take in.\n- Root: علم (ʿilm)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Knowledge as the mark or sign by which a thing is known; the trace or landmark that guides.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العلم: إدراك الشيء بحقيقته\" — Knowledge is the apprehension of a thing in its true reality; it is of two kinds: apprehension of the essence of a thing, and judgment upon a thing regarding what exists for it or is negated of it.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: certain awareness. Metaphorical: \"هو من أعلام العلم الخافقه\" — he is among the lofty banners of knowledge; knowledge as a guiding landmark (maʿlam).\n- Root: نور (nūr)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Light; the first to set up beacons (manār) on his path in his campaigns to guide his return.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النور: الضوء المنتشر الذي يعين على الإبصار\" — Light is the diffused radiance that aids vision; it is of two kinds: that perceived by the eye of inner insight (baṣīrah) — such as the light of the intellect and the light of the Qur'an — and that perceived by the eye of the head, such as the celestial bodies.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: illumination. Metaphorical: \"نور الأمر: بينه\" — he illuminated the matter: he made it clear; \"وهذا أنور من ذاك: أبين\" — this is more luminous than that: more evident.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Restriction and Focused Predication (al-ḥaṣr wa al-taqyīd bi-innamā) — governing the exclusive attribution of a predicate to a subject, as in the author's restriction of the scholar's condition to one of two men, and the exclusion of the third.", "content_type": "dhikr", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on constant remembrance of God and contemplative invocations, from 'Condemnation of Delusion'", "badge": "Dhikr"}], "2": [{"arabic": "عن هذا الترجيح بالملك وهو مجاز محض إذ لا ملك إلا لملك الملوك الذي له ما في السموات والأرض وكيف يكون العبد مالكا وهو في نفسه ليس يملك نفسه بل هو ملك غيره نعم الخلق عباد الله والأرض مائدة الله وقد أذن لهم في الأكل من مائدته بقدر حاجتهم كالملك ينصب مائدة لعبيده فمن أخذ لقمة بيمينه واحتوت عليها براجمه فجاء عبد آخر وأراد انتزاعها", "english": "...to your heart, so that your heart becomes bound by it, and thus your body becomes bound in that direction in a posture of stillness and solemnity when you worship your Lord. Likewise, your actions are divided into those that are noble — such as acts of obedience — and those that are base — such as relieving oneself or spitting. So when you spit toward the direction of the Qiblah, you have indeed wronged it (ẓalamtahā) and shown ingratitude for the blessing (niʿmah) of Allah Most High upon you, by virtue of His placing the Qiblah, through whose placement the perfection of your worship is achieved. Similarly, when you put on your leather sock (khuff) and begin with the left foot, you have wronged (ẓalamta), because the sock is a protection for the foot; the foot has a share in it, and in matters of shares and portions, one ought to begin with the nobler — that is justice (al-ʿadl) and fidelity to wisdom, while its opposite is injustice (ẓulm) and ingratitude for the blessing of the sock and the foot. And this, in the eyes of the knowers (al-ʿārifīn), is a grave offense (kabīrah), even if the jurist (al-faqīh) labels it merely \"disliked\" (makrūh) — to the extent that one of them (the righteous predecessors) had gathered many measures of wheat and was giving them in charity, and when asked about the reason, he said: \"I once put on a sandal and began with the left foot inadvertently, so I wish to expiate that (kufr) through charity.\" Yes, the jurist is unable to magnify the matter in these affairs, because he is constrained — rather, he is occupied with reforming the common folk (al-ʿawāmm), whose rank approaches the rank of the beasts (al-anʿām), and they are plunged into darknesses (ẓulumāt) far greater and more immense than that such minor darknesses should appear in comparison to them. For it would be ugly to say of one who drank wine and took the cup with his left hand that he transgressed in two respects — one being the drinking, and the other the taking with the left — and of one who sold wine at the time of the call to prayer on Friday, it would be ugly to say he betrayed in two respects — {one} the selling of wine, and the other the selling at the time of the call. And of one who relieved himself in the prayer niche (miḥrāb) of a mosque with his back to the Qiblah, it would be ugly to mention his abandonment of propriety in relieving himself, in that he did not place the Qiblah to his right. For all sins are darknesses, some piled atop others, such that one is eclipsed beside another. A master may punish his slave if he uses his knife without his permission — but if he killed his most beloved son with that very knife, the unauthorized use of the knife would retain no weight or sting in itself. Thus, all the etiquettes (ādāb) that the Prophets and the Saints (al-awliyāʾ) observed, and which we have treated leniently in jurisprudence (fiqh) concerning the common folk, have as their cause this very necessity. Otherwise, all these disliked acts are deviations from justice (al-ʿadl), ingratitude for blessing (kufrān al-niʿmah), and a deficiency in the rank that conveys the servant to the degrees of proximity (darajāt al-qurb) — some of them affect the servant by diminishing proximity and lowering station, while others expel him entirely from the bounds of proximity into the realm of distance (ʿālam al-buʿd), which is the abode of the devils. Likewise, whoever breaks a branch from a tree without an urgent pressing need, and without a sound purpose, has shown ingratitude for the blessing of Allah Most High in the creation of trees and the creation of the hand. As for the hand, it was not created for frivolity, but for obedience and for deeds that aid in obedience. As for the tree, Allah Most High created it, and created for it roots, and conveyed water to it, and placed within it the power of nourishment and growth, so that it might reach the culmination of its development (munthā nashwihi), and His servants might benefit from it. So breaking it before its culmination, not in a manner by which His servants benefit, is a violation of the purpose of wisdom and a deviation from justice. But if he has a sound purpose, then that is permissible for him — for trees and animals have been made a ransom (fidāʾ) for the purposes of human beings, since both are perishable and doomed. Thus, annihilating the baser (al-akhass) for the preservation of the nobler (al-ashraf) for a time is closer to justice than squandering both. And to this points His saying, Most High: {«وَسَخَّرَ لَكُم مَّا فِي السَّمَاوَاتِ وَمَا فِي الْأَرْضِ جَمِيعًا مِّنْهُ»} — \"And He subjected to you whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth — all from Him\" [Al-Jāthiyah: 13]. Yes — if he breaks a tree belonging to someone else, he is also a wrongdoer (ẓālim), even if he is in need, because every specific tree does not suffice for the needs of all the servants of Allah; rather, it suffices for a single need. And if one single person were to be singled out for it without preponderance (tarjīḥ) and specification (ikhtiṣāṣ), that would be injustice. So the one entitled to specification is he who obtained the seed, placed it in the ground, conveyed water to it, and undertook its care — he is more worthy of it than others, and his side is thereby established. But if it grew in dead land (mawāt al-arḍ) without any human effort, then one must seek another specification — namely, precedence in taking it (sabq ilā akhdhihi). For the one who precedes has the special property of precedence (khāṣṣiyyat al-sabq). Justice, then, is that he be most worthy of it. And the poor (al-fuqarāʾ) have expressed this preponderance by the term \"ownership\" (al-milk) — but it is pure metaphor (majāz maḥḍ), for there is no owner except the King of Kings (Malik al-Mulūk), to Whom belongs what is in the heavens and the earth. And how could a servant be an owner, when he himself does not own his own self — rather, he is the property of Another? Yes — the creatures are the servants of Allah, and the earth is the table (māʾidah) of Allah, and He has permitted them to eat from His table to the measure of their need — like a king who sets a table for his slaves. So if one takes a morsel with his right hand, and his fingers close over it, and another slave comes and seeks to snatch it away...", "book_title": "Patience and Gratitude", "section_title_ar": "Section 986", "section_title_en": "The Etiquette of the Heart and the Hidden Dimensions of Violating Sacred Directions and Blessings", "section_index": 986, "anchors": "- Root: ظلم (ẓ-l-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is \"darkness\" (ẓulmah) — absence of light; also \"to place something in other than its proper place\" (waḍʿ al-shayʾ fī ghayr mawḍiʿihi).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Ẓulm is expressed via \"darknesses\" (ẓulumāt) as ignorance, polytheism, and transgression — the opposite of nūr (light/justice).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): \"الظلم ظلمات يوم القيامة\" — injustice is darknesses on the Day of Judgment. Metaphorical (majāz): \"أرض مظلومة\" — land never dug, i.e., violated by first use; \"ظلم البعير\" — slaughtering an animal without cause (ʿabṭ).\n- Root: نعم (n-ʿ-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Niʿmah is a state of well-being; also \"softness\" and \"delicacy.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Niʿmah is the good condition bestowed; its plural (niʿam) covers both little and much — \"وَإِن تَعُدُّوا نِعْمَةَ اللَّهِ لَا تُحْصُوهَا\" {And if you count the blessings of Allah, you cannot enumerate them} [Ibrahim: 34].\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"جلت نعمة الله\" — the blessing of Allah is immense. Metaphorical: \"خفت نعامتهم\" — their ostriches departed (i.e., they fled), showing how blessing can be \"kicked away\" (kufrān al-niʿmah).\n- Root: ملك (m-l-k)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Ownership and dominion; the possessor (mālik) who disposes freely.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الملك: هو المتصرف بالأمر والنهي\" — the king is one who commands and forbids; true ownership belongs only to Allah, \"لَهُ مَا فِي السَّمَاوَاتِ وَمَا فِي الْأَرْضِ\" {To Him belongs what is in the heavens and the earth}.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"ملك يده\" — what one's hand owns. Metaphorical: \"ملكت فلانة أمرها\" — she controlled her own affair; here, human \"ownership\" (milk) is pure metaphor (majāz maḥḍ), since real dominion is Allah's alone.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The rule governs how qualifying clauses (e.g., \"التي بوضعها كمال عبادتك\") attach to antecedents, preserving syntactic cohesion even when embedded phrases lengthen the sentence, ensuring no semantic break occurs between the act and its moral consequence.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Patience and Gratitude'", "badge": "Insight"}], "3": [{"arabic": "الحياة الدنيا لنفتنهم فيه ورزق ربك خير وأبقى وقال تعالى الذين يستحبون الحياة الدنيا على الآخرة فوصف الكفار بذلك فمفهومه أن المؤمن هو الذي يتصف بنقيضه وهو أن يستحب الآخرة على الحياة الدنيا وأما الأخبار فما ورد منها في ذم الدنيا كثير وقد أوردنا بعضها في كتاب ذم الدنيا مع ربع المهلكات إذ حب الدنيا من المهلكات ونحن الآن نقتصر على فضيلة بغض الدنيا فإنه من المنجيات وهو المعني بالزهد وقد قال رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم من أصبح وهمه الدنيا شتت الله عليه أمره وفرق عليه ضيعته وجعل فقره بين عينيه ولم يأته من الدنيا إلا ما كتب له ومن أصبح و", "english": "Allah the Exalted said: {فخرج على قومه في زينته} [He went out to his people in his adornment] up to His saying, the Exalted: {وقال الذين أوتوا العلم ويلكم ثواب الله خير لمن آمن} [And those who had been given knowledge said, \"Woe to you! The reward of Allah is better for he who believes.\"] Thus He attributed renunciation (al-zuhd) to the learned, and described its people as possessing knowledge—and this is the ultimate praise. And He said, the Exalted: {أولئك يؤتون أجرهم مرتين بما صبروا} [Those will be given their reward twice for what they patiently endured], and it came in the exegesis (al-tafsīr) that this refers to renunciation of the world. And He said, Mighty and Majestic: {إنا جعلنا ما على الأرض زينة لها لنبلوهم أيهم أحسن عملا} [Indeed We have made that which is on the earth adornment for it that We may test them as to which of them is best in deed]. It was said its meaning is: which of them is most renunciant (azhad) in it. So He described renunciation as being among the most excellent of deeds. And He said, the Exalted: {من كان يريد حرث الآخرة نزد له في حرثه ومن كان يريد حرث الدنيا نؤته منها وما له في الآخرة من نصيب} [Whoever desires the harvest of the Hereafter—We increase for him in his harvest. And whoever desires the harvest of this world—We give him from it, but he has no share in the Hereafter]. And He said, the Exalted: {ولا تمدن عينيك إلى ما متعنا به أزواجا منهم زهرة الحياة الدنيا لنفتنهم فيه ورزق ربك خير وأبقى} [And do not extend your eyes to that which We have given enjoyment to categories of them—the splendor of the life of this world—that We may test them thereby. And the provision of your Lord is better and more enduring]. And He said, the Exalted: {الذين يستحبون الحياة الدنيا على الآخرة} [Those who prefer the life of this world over the Hereafter]—thus He described the disbelievers with that. So its understood implication (mafhūmuhu) is that the believer is the one characterized by its opposite, namely, that he prefers the Hereafter over the life of this world. [PageV04P219]", "book_title": "Poverty and Abstinence", "section_title_ar": "Section 1090", "section_title_en": "The Book of Renunciation (Kitāb al-Zuhd) — Section on the Virtue of Despising the World", "section_index": 1090, "anchors": "- Root: زهد (z-h-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of abstaining, forsaking, and turning away from something, deeming it insignificant and paltry.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: الزهد is the voluntary turning of the heart away from the world (al-dunyā) as a deliberate act of spiritual preference for the Hereafter, not mere physical poverty.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage denotes abstention from a thing due to its worthlessness; Metaphorical (Majaz) usage in the religious context signifies the heart's detachment from worldly allurements while the body may still function within it.\n- Root: دنا (d-n-w)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Nearness, proximity; the world (al-dunyā) is so named for its closeness and lower station relative to the Hereafter.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): الدنو: القرب بالذات أو بالحكم; al-dunyā is the lower, nearer abode, contrasted with the ultimate and enduring one.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: spatial or temporal nearness; Metaphorical: the world as the inferior, immediate realm that precedes the eternal one.\n- Root: عقل (ʿ-q-l) — implied in \"مفهومه\" (its understood implication)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Understanding, intellect, that which binds and restrains.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The intellect perceives the conceptual implication (mafhūm) of a statement, deriving the necessary opposite or corollary.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the articulated text; Metaphorical: the understood converse (mafhūm al-mukhālafah) which the author here employs to establish the believer's trait.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The periodic structure of the author's argumentation, where the governing verb (e.g., \"قال تعالى\") is separated from its quoted object by lengthy parenthetical citations, mirrors the classical allowance for interposition to maintain rhetorical flow and emphasis.", "content_type": "dhikr", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on constant remembrance of God and contemplative invocations, from 'Poverty and Abstinence'", "badge": "Dhikr"}, {"arabic": "وأما هؤلاء فإنهم يصدون عن سبيل الله ويجرون الخلق إلى الغرور بالله بلفظ الرجاء فيزيدهم كلامهم جراءة على المعاصي ورغبة في الدنيا لا سيما إذا كان الواعظ متزينا بالثياب والخيل والمراكب فإنه تشهد هيئته من فرقه إلى قدمه بشدة حرصه على الدنيا فما يفسده هذا المغرور أكثر مما يصلحه بل لا يصلح أصلا ويضل خلقا كثيرا ولا يخفى وجه كونه مغرورا وفرقة أخرى منهم قنعوا بحفظ كلام الزهاد وأحاديثهم في ذم الدنيا فهم يحفظون الكلمات على وجهها ويؤدونها من غير إحاطة بمعانيها فبعضهم يفعل ذلك على المنابر وبعضهم", "english": "But as for these [deluded ones], they turn others away from the path of God and drag creation into self-deception concerning God (al-ghurūr billāh) by means of the word \"hope\" (al-rajāʾ). Thus their speech increases them in audacity toward sins and desire for this world — especially when the preacher is adorned with fine garments, horses, and mounts, for his very appearance bears witness from his hair to his feet to his intense greed for this world. So what this deluded one corrupts is more than what he reforms — nay, he reforms nothing at all, and he leads many people astray. Nor is the aspect of his being deluded hidden.", "book_title": "Condemnation of Delusion", "section_title_ar": "Section 899", "section_title_en": "The Categories of the Deluded Among Preachers and Hadith Transmitters", "section_index": 899, "anchors": "- Root: أ-ص-ل (ʾ-ṣ-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of foundation, root, and origin; to set right what is corrupt.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): اصلاح (iṣlāḥ) as the act of bringing something from a state of corruption (fasād) to soundness (ṣalāḥ), whether in belief, action, or speech.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) usage denotes rectifying a physical object; metaphorical (majāz) usage applies to moral and spiritual correction of others.\n- Root: غ-ر-ر (gh-r-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: غرور (ghurūr) — deception, delusion, vanity; from غرّ (gharra), to be heedless or deceived by appearances.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): الغرور (al-ghurūr) is the delusion that leads one to rely on false hopes, particularly regarding God's mercy while persisting in sin; the state of one who is deceived by Satan or by his own vain fancies.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: a thing that deceives by its outward show; Metaphorical: the soul's self-deception through false reliance on divine forgiveness without repentance.\n- Root: س-م-ع (s-m-ʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: السمع (al-samʿ) — hearing; the auditory faculty and its act.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Hearing as the means of acquiring transmitted knowledge (samāʿ); in hadith terminology, the formal act of hearing a tradition from a shaykh, which is the first stage of transmission.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the physical act of hearing; Metaphorical: attentive listening with the heart, as in \"ألقى السمع وهو شهيد\" {«وَأَلْقَى السَّمْعَ وَهُوَ شَهِيدٌ»} [Q. 50:37] — one who listens with full presence of mind.\n- Root: ح-ف-ظ (ḥ-f-ẓ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: الحفظ (al-ḥifẓ) — preservation, guarding, memorization; keeping something safe from loss or alteration.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): الحفظ is the faculty of the soul by which what is understood is retained; its opposite is forgetfulness (nisyān). It also denotes vigilant care and protection.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: guarding a physical object; Metaphorical: committing knowledge to memory such that it is preserved from distortion.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The rule governing the separation of a preposition from its object by intervening clauses, as in the periodic structure \"فإذا لم تحفظ لا بالقلب ولا بالكتاب وجرى على سمعك صوت غفل\" where the conditional clause (فإذا) governs a sequence of coordinated conditions, each requiring its own apodosis, demonstrating the classical pattern of layered conditional sentences.", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Condemnation of Delusion'", "badge": "Ethics"}], "5": [{"arabic": "{يخرجهم من الظلمات إلى النور} وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم يا أيها الناس اعقلوا عن ربكم وتواصوا بالعقل تعرفوا ما أمرتم به وما نهيتم عنه واعلموا أنه ينجدكم عند ربكم واعلموا أن العاقل من أطاع الله وإن كان دميم المنظر حقير الخطر دنيء المنزلة رث الهيئة وأن الجاهل من عصى الله تعالى وإن كان جميل المنظر عظيم الخطر شريف المنزلة حسن الهيئة فصيحا نطوقا فالقردة والخنازير أعقل عند الله تعالى ممن عصاه ولا تغتر بتعظيم أهل الدنيا إياهم فإنهم من الخاسرين (2) وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم أول ما خل", "english": "The Book of Seclusion ('Uzlah) — its explanation, God Most High willing. For this reason, Yūsuf ibn Asbāṭ wrote to Ḥudhayfah al-Marʿashī: \"What is your opinion of one who remains, finding no one with whom to remember God Most High except that he is either sinful, or his remembrance in company is itself an act of disobedience — and that because he finds no one of his own kind?\" And indeed he spoke truly, for mixing with people is never free from backbiting (ghībah), or listening to backbiting, or remaining silent upon wrongdoing (munkar). And the best of his states is that he either imparts knowledge or acquires it. Yet were this poor wretch to reflect, and to know that his imparting of knowledge is never free from the taints of ostentation (riyā'), the seeking of followers, and the desire for leadership (ri'āsah), he would know that the one who acquires knowledge from him only seeks to make it an instrument for pursuing the world and a means toward evil — so that the teacher becomes his helper therein, his support and his backer, preparing its causes for him, like one who sells a sword to a highway robber. For knowledge is like the sword: its soundness for good is like the soundness of the sword for warfare. And for this reason, it is not permitted to sell it to one whom one knows, by the indications of his circumstances, intends to use it to aid in cutting off the road.", "book_title": "Condemnation of Delusion", "section_title_ar": "Section 65", "section_title_en": "The Book of Seclusion ('Uzlah) — Section 65, and The Seventh Book: On the Intellect, Its Nobility, Its Reality, and Its Divisions", "section_index": 65, "anchors": "- Root: عقل (ʿaql)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes binding, restraining, and holding back; the intellect is that which restrains the soul from base desires and prevents it from straying.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العقل يقال للقوة المتهيئة لقبول العلم، ويقال للعلم الذي يستفيده الإنسان بتلك القوة عقل\" — The intellect is said of the faculty prepared for the reception of knowledge, and it is also said of the knowledge which man acquires by means of that faculty.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): intellectual apprehension and understanding. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"نخلة لا تعقل الإبار\" — a palm tree that does not accept pollination, i.e., does not grasp or take in.\n- Root: علم (ʿilm)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Knowledge as the mark or sign by which a thing is known; the trace or landmark that guides.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العلم: إدراك الشيء بحقيقته\" — Knowledge is the apprehension of a thing in its true reality; it is of two kinds: apprehension of the essence of a thing, and judgment upon a thing regarding what exists for it or is negated of it.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: certain awareness. Metaphorical: \"هو من أعلام العلم الخافقه\" — he is among the lofty banners of knowledge; knowledge as a guiding landmark (maʿlam).\n- Root: نور (nūr)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Light; the first to set up beacons (manār) on his path in his campaigns to guide his return.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النور: الضوء المنتشر الذي يعين على الإبصار\" — Light is the diffused radiance that aids vision; it is of two kinds: that perceived by the eye of inner insight (baṣīrah) — such as the light of the intellect and the light of the Qur'an — and that perceived by the eye of the head, such as the celestial bodies.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: illumination. Metaphorical: \"نور الأمر: بينه\" — he illuminated the matter: he made it clear; \"وهذا أنور من ذاك: أبين\" — this is more luminous than that: more evident.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Restriction and Focused Predication (al-ḥaṣr wa al-taqyīd bi-innamā) — governing the exclusive attribution of a predicate to a subject, as in the author's restriction of the scholar's condition to one of two men, and the exclusion of the third.", "content_type": "dhikr", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on constant remembrance of God and contemplative invocations, from 'Condemnation of Delusion'", "badge": "Dhikr"}], "10": [{"arabic": "ولذلك قال الله تعالى أفي الله شك فاطر السموات والأرض ولهذا بعث الأنبياء صلوات الله عليهم لدعوة الخلق إلى التوحيد ليقولوا لا إله إلا الله وما أمروا أن يقولوا لنا إله وللعالم إله فإن ذلك كان مجبولا في فطرة عقولهم من مبدأ نشؤهم وفي عنفوان شبابهم ولذلك قال عز وجل ولئن سألتهم من خلق السموات والأرض ليقولن الله وقال تعالى فأقم وجهك للدين حنيفا فطرة الله التي", "english": "The First Principle: the knowledge of His existence, the Exalted. The first thing by which one is illuminated from among the lights, and the path of reflection (ṭarīq al-iʿtibār) by which one proceeds, is that to which the Qurʾān has guided. For after the explanation of God, glorified be He, there is no explanation. He, the Exalted, has said: {«أَلَمْ نَجْعَلِ الْأَرْضَ مِهَادًا وَالْجِبَالَ أَوْتَادًا وَخَلَقْنَاكُمْ أَزْوَاجًا وَجَعَلْنَا نَوْمَكُمْ سُبَاتًا وَجَعَلْنَا اللَّيْلَ لِبَاسًا وَجَعَلْنَا النَّهَارَ مَعَاشًا وَبَنَيْنَا فَوْقَكُمْ سَبْعًا شِدَادًا وَجَعَلْنَا سِرَاجًا وَهَّاجًا وَأَنزَلْنَا مِنَ الْمُعْصِرَاتِ مَاءً ثَجَّاجًا لِّنُخْرِجَ بِهِ حَبًّا وَنَبَاتًا وَجَنَّاتٍ أَلْفَافًا»} — \"Have We not made the earth a resting place, and the mountains as pegs, and created you in pairs, and made your sleep a repose, and made the night a covering, and made the day for livelihood, and built above you seven strong heavens, and made a blazing lamp, and sent down from the rain-clouds pouring water, that We may bring forth thereby grain and vegetation, and gardens dense with foliage?\" (Q 78:6–16). And He, the Exalted, has said: {«إِنَّ فِي خَلْقِ السَّمَاوَاتِ وَالْأَرْضِ وَاخْتِلَافِ اللَّيْلِ وَالنَّهَارِ وَالْفُلْكِ الَّتِي تَجْرِي فِي الْبَحْرِ بِمَا يَنفَعُ النَّاسَ وَمَا أَنزَلَ اللَّهُ مِنَ السَّمَاءِ مِن مَّاءٍ فَأَحْيَا بِهِ الْأَرْضَ بَعْدَ مَوْتِهَا وَبَثَّ فِيهَا مِن كُلِّ دَابَّةٍ وَتَصْرِيفِ الرِّيَاحِ وَالسَّحَابِ الْمُسَخَّرِ بَيْنَ السَّمَاءِ وَالْأَرْضِ لَآيَاتٍ لِّقَوْمٍ يَعْقِلُونَ»} — \"Indeed, in the creation of the heavens and the earth, and the alternation of the night and the day, and the ships which sail through the sea with that which benefits people, and what God has sent down from the sky of water, thereby reviving the earth after its death, and dispersing therein every creature, and the directing of the winds, and the clouds subservient between the heaven and the earth — are signs for a people who reason\" (Q 2:164). And He, the Exalted, has said: {«أَلَمْ تَرَوْا كَيْفَ خَلَقَ اللَّهُ سَبْعَ سَمَاوَاتٍ طِبَاقًا وَجَعَلَ الْقَمَرَ فِيهِنَّ نُورًا وَجَعَلَ الشَّمْسَ سِرَاجًا وَاللَّهُ أَنبَتَكُم مِّنَ الْأَرْضِ نَبَاتًا ثُمَّ يُعِيدُكُمْ فِيهَا وَيُخْرِجُكُمْ إِخْرَاجًا»} — \"Have you not seen how God created seven heavens in layers, and made the moon therein a light, and made the sun a lamp? And God caused you to grow from the earth a growth; then He returns you into it, and brings you forth a bringing forth\" (Q 71:15–18). And He, the Exalted, has said: {«أَفَرَأَيْتُم مَّا تُمْنُونَ أَأَنتُمْ تَخْلُقُونَهُ أَمْ نَحْنُ الْخَالِقُونَ»} — \"Have you considered what you emit? Do you create it, or are We the Creator?\" — up to His saying: {«لِلْمُقْوِينَ»} — \"for the settlers\" (Q 56:58–73).", "book_title": "Foundations of Belief", "section_title_ar": "Section 87", "section_title_en": "The Pillars of Faith: Knowledge of God, His Attributes, His Acts, and the Audibles — Section 87", "section_index": 87, "anchors": "- Root: وجود (w-j-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: [Core linguistic root meaning: to find, to perceive, to be; existence as that which is found or discovered]\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): [Theological/Kalam semantic nuance: al-wujūd as necessary existence (wujūb) versus contingent existence (imkān); the self-evident reality of the Creator's being]\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): [Literal vs Metaphorical distinction: haqīqah — existence as affirmed reality; majāz — \"finding\" as in discovery, not applicable to God's necessary being]\n- Root: خلق (kh-l-q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: [Core linguistic root meaning: measuring, proportioning, fashioning; origination of a thing upon a measure]\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الخلق أصله: التقدير المستقيم، ويستعمل في إبداع الشيء من غير أصل ولا احتذاء\" — creation as origination from nothing without prior model\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): [Literal: خلق الله الخلق: أوجده على تقدير أوجبته الحكمة — brought into existence upon a measure necessitated by wisdom; Majaz: not applicable to God's act as pure origination]\n- Root: فطر (f-t-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: [Core linguistic root meaning: to split, cleave, rend; to originate, to bring forth as a new creation]\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"فطر الله الخلق\" — God's origination of creation as a primordial act; \"الفطرة\" as the innate disposition\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): [Literal: فطر الله الخلق، وهو فاطر السموات: مبتدعها — originator without precedent; Majaz: \"رأيه فطير\" — immature opinion, not applicable to divine]\n- Root: ارض (ar-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: [Core linguistic root meaning: the earth, the lower sphere; that which is beneath]\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الأرض: الجرم المقابل للسماء\" — the body opposite the heaven\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): [Literal: the earth as a resting place (مهادا); Majaz: not applicable in this context of divine creation]\n- Root: ركن (r-k-n)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: [Core linguistic root meaning: corner, pillar, support; that to which one leans for strength]\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"ركن الشيء: جانبه الذي يسكن إليه\" — the side of a thing to which one repairs; used metaphorically for strength\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): [Literal: physical pillar/corner; Majaz: \"فلان يأوي من عز قومه إلى ركن شديد\" — one who repairs to a mighty support; here, the \"pillars\" (أركان) of faith as structural supports]\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The syntactic structure \"مداره على عشرة أصول\" (its circuit revolves upon ten principles) employs a prepositional phrase governing the verbal noun, establishing the necessary grammatical dependency that mirrors the ontological dependency of the created upon the Creator.", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Foundations of Belief'", "badge": "Afterlife"}, {"arabic": "قولهم أنؤمن لبشرين مثلنا {وقولهم} إن أنتم إلا بشر مثلنا ولئن أطعتم بشرا مثلكم إنكم إذا لخاسرون وقال الذين لا يرجون لقاءنا لولا أنزل علينا الملائكة أو نرى ربنا لقد استكبروا في أنفسهم وعتوا عتوا كبيرا وقالوا لولا أنزل عليه ملك وقال فرعون فيما أخبر الله عنه {أو جاء معه الملائكة مقترنين} وقال الله تعالى {واستكبر هو وجنوده في الأرض بغير الحق} فتكبر هو على الله وعلى رسله جميعا قال وهب قال له موسى عليه السلام آمن ولك ملكك قال حتى أشاور هامان فشاور هامان فقال هامان بينما أنت رب يعبد إذ صرت عبد تعب", "english": "Know that the one against whom pride is directed is either God—mighty and majestic—or His messengers, or the rest of His creation. Man has been created unjust and ignorant {«وخلق الإنسان ظلوما جهولا»}, so at times he grows proud against the creation, and at times he grows proud against the Creator. Thus, pride, considered with respect to the one against whom it is directed, falls into three divisions.", "book_title": "Condemnation of Pride and Conceit", "section_title_ar": "Section 855", "section_title_en": "Exposition of the One Against Whom Pride Is Directed, Its Degrees, Its Divisions, and the Fruits of Pride Within It", "section_index": 855, "anchors": "- Root: كبر (k-b-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes magnitude, whether physical size, age, or rank; \"الكبرة\" specifically marks the state of advanced age or an ingrained, chronic condition.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الكبير والصغير من الأسماء المتضايفة\" — bigness and smallness are relative terms. In the theological context, pride (الكبر) is the soul's self-magnification against another, a relational vice that distorts the creature's standing before the Creator.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage concerns physical or quantitative magnitude. Metaphorical (Majaz) usage transfers this to moral and spiritual stature, as in \"كبر علي ذلك\" (it weighed heavily upon him) — here, the soul's swelling against divine command is a metaphorical arrogation of greatness.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Prepositional Interposition (باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور) — Governing the syntactic tension between the agent of pride (المتكبر) and its object (المتكبر عليه), where the preposition \"على\" carries the full weight of opposition and elevation, structuring the periodic sentences that follow.", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Condemnation of Pride and Conceit'", "badge": "Ethics"}], "11": [{"arabic": "الروح من أمره على من يشاء من عباده} وأحال الإغواء على إبليس فقال تعالى {ليضل عن سبيله} والإغواء هو استيقاف العباد دون بلوغ غاية الحكمة فانظر كيف نسبه إلى العبد الذي غضب عليه والإرشاد سياقه لهم إلى الغاية فانظر كيف نسبه إلى العبد الذي أحبه وعندك في العادة له مثال فالملك إذا كان محتاجا إلى من يسقيه الشراب وإلى من يحجمه وينظف فناء منزله عن القاذورات وكان له عبدان فلا يعين للحجامة والتنظيف إلا أقبحهما وأخسهما ولا يفوض حمل الشراب والطيب إلا إلى أحسنهما وأكملهما وأحبهما إليه ولا ينبغ", "english": "Thus was the beginning of this matter and its end. And do not understand it unless you are worthy of it. And if you are worthy of it, the eye is opened and you see, so you have no need of a leader to lead you. The blind man can indeed be led, but only to a limit. For when the path narrows and becomes sharper than a sword and finer than a hair, the bird is able to fly over it but is not able to drag a blind man behind it. And when the field becomes narrow and subtle — subtle as water, for example — and crossing is possible only by swimming, then the one skilled in the art of swimming may be able to cross by himself, and perhaps he is not able to drag another behind him. These matters bear the same relation to walking upon the way of the common masses of creatures as walking upon water bears to walking upon earth. Swimming can be learned; but as for walking upon water, it is not acquired by instruction, but rather is attained through the strength of certainty (yaqīn). For this reason it was said to the Prophet, peace be upon him, that Jesus, peace be upon him, was said to have walked upon water, and he, peace be upon him, said: \"Had his certainty increased, he would have walked upon the air.\" {«حديث قيل له يقال إن عيسى مشى على الماء قال لو ازداد يقينا لمشي على الهواء»} — This hadith is rejected (munkar) and is not known in this form. What is known is what Ibn Abi al-Dunya narrated in the Book of Certainty from the saying of Bakr ibn Abd Allah al-Muzani, who said: The disciples lost their Prophet, and it was said to them: \"He has headed toward the sea.\" So they set out seeking him, and when they reached the sea, behold, he was coming toward them walking upon the water. And he mentioned a hadith in which Jesus said: \"If the son of Adam had a hair's breadth of certainty, he would walk upon the water.\" And Abu Mansur al-Daylami narrated in the Musnad al-Firdaws with a weak chain from the hadith of Muadh ibn Jabal: \"If you knew God with true knowledge, you would walk upon the seas, and the mountains would be removed by your supplications.\" These are symbols and allusions to the meaning of aversion (karāhah) and love (maḥabbah), and good pleasure (riḍā) and wrath (ghaḍab), and gratitude (shukr) and ingratitude (kufrān) — more than this is not fitting for the science of practical conduct (ʿilm al-muʿāmalah). And God, exalted be He, struck an example of this to draw it near to the understanding of creatures, since He knew that He created the jinn and mankind only to worship Him, and their worship was the ultimate purpose of wisdom (ghāyat al-ḥikmah) with respect to them. Then He informed that He has two servants: one whom He loves, whose name is Gabriel — the Holy Spirit and the Trustworthy One — and he is with Him beloved, obeyed, trustworthy, and honored; and another whom He hates, whose name is Iblis — the accursed one, reprieved until the Day of Judgment. Then He delegated guidance (irshād) to Gabriel, saying, the Exalted: {«قل نزله روح القدس من ربك بالحق»} \"Say: The Holy Spirit has brought it down from your Lord with truth\" (16:102), and said, the Exalted: {«يلقي الروح من أمره على من يشاء من عباده»} \"He casts the Spirit of His command upon whom He wills of His servants\" (40:15). And He delegated misguidance (ighwāʾ) to Iblis, saying, the Exalted: {«ليضل عن سبيله»} \"That he may lead astray from His path\" (4:119). And ighwāʾ is the halting of servants short of attaining the ultimate purpose of wisdom. So observe how He attributed it to the servant with whom He was wrathful; and guidance (irshād) is the conduct of them toward the ultimate purpose — so observe how He attributed it to the servant whom He loves. And you have an example of this in custom: when a king is in need of one to give him drink and one to cup him and to clean the courtyard of his house of filth, and he has two servants, he does not appoint for cupping and cleaning except the ugliest and meanest of them, and he does not entrust the carrying of drink and perfume except to the best and most complete of them and the most beloved to him. And you should not say: \"This is my act, and his act was not without my act\" — for you have erred when you attributed that to yourself. Rather, He is the One who directed your motive (dāʿiyah) to the specification of the disliked act to the disliked person, and the beloved act to the beloved person, as a completion of justice (ʿadl). For His justice is sometimes completed through matters in which you have no entry, and sometimes it is completed in you — for you also are among His acts. So your motive, your power, your knowledge, your action, and all the other causes of your movements in expression — all of this is His act, which He arranged with justice in an arrangement from which moderate acts issue forth. Except that you see only yourself, and so you suppose that what appears through you in the world of witness (ʿālam al-shahādah) has no cause from the world of the unseen and the dominion (ʿālam al-ghayb wa al-malakūt). Therefore you attribute it to yourself. And you are only like the child who looks at night at the play of the juggler (mushʿabadh) who brings forth figures from behind a curtain that dance and scream and rise and sit, composed of rags that do not move by themselves — rather, they are moved by fine hair-like threads that do not appear in the darkness of night, their ends in the hand of the juggler, who is hidden from the eyes of the children. So they rejoice and marvel, supposing that those rags dance and play and rise and sit. As for the intelligent ones, they know that this is a moving (taḥrīk) and not a self-movement, but perhaps they do not know its detailed modality. And the one who knows some of its detail does not know it as the juggler knows it, to whom the command belongs and in whose hand is the pulling. Likewise, the children of the people of this world — and all creatures are children relative to the scholars — look at these persons (ashkhāṣ) and suppose that they are the movers, and so they attribute [action] to them. And the scholars know that they are moved, except that they do not know the modality of the moving — and they are the majority. Except the knowers (ʿārifūn), the scholars, and those firmly rooted in knowledge (al-rāsikhūn), for they have perceived, with the sharpness of their sight, fine spider-web threads — nay, much finer than these — suspended from heaven, their ends attached to the persons of the people of the earth. Those threads are not perceived, due to their fineness, by these outward eyes. Then they witnessed the heads of those threads in their suspension-points (manāṭāt) to which they are attached, and they witnessed for those suspension-points handles (maqābiḍ) that are in the hands of the angels who move the heavens. And they also witnessed the angels of the heavens directed toward the bearers of the Throne (ḥamalat al-ʿarsh), awaiting what descends upon them of the command from the presence of Lordship (ḥaḍrat al-rubūbiyyah) — \"they do not disobey God in what He commands them, and they do what they are commanded\" (66:6). And He expressed these witnessed realities in the Qur'an, and it was said: {«وفي السماء رزقكم وما توعدون»} \"And in the heaven is your provision and what you are promised\" (51:22). And He expressed the waiting of the angels of the heavens for what descends upon them of decree and command, and it was said: \"He created seven heavens and of the earth the like of them; {«يتنزل الأمر بينهن لتعلموا أن الله على كل شيء قدير وأن الله قد أحاط بكل شيء علما»} 'the command descends between them, that you may know that God is powerful over all things and that God encompasses all things in knowledge'\" (65:12). These are matters whose interpretation (taʾwīl) none knows except God and those firmly rooted in knowledge (al-rāsikhūn fī al-ʿilm). And Ibn Abbas, may God be pleased with them both, expressed the exclusivity of those firmly rooted in knowledge to sciences that the understandings of creatures cannot bear, when he recited the saying of the Exalted: {«يتنزل الأمر بينهن»} \"The command descends between them,\" and said: \"Were I to mention what I know of the meaning of this verse, you would stone me\" — and in another wording: \"You would say he is an unbeliever.\"", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 990", "section_title_en": "On the Degrees of Proximity to God and the Hierarchy of Those Who Perfect Divine Wisdom", "section_index": 990, "anchors": "- Root: سير (s-y-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"السير: الذهاب والسفر، وسار يسير سيرا ومسيرا، والسيارة: الرفقة المسافرة\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"السير: الانتقال من مكان إلى مكان، ويستعار للسلوك في الدين والطريقة\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): [Literal] \"سار القوم: مشوا\" — [Metaphorical] \"سار فيه السير: مضى في الأمر، وسار ذكره: انتشر، ومنه: سيرة فلان: طريقته\"\n- Root: مشي (m-sh-y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"المشي: تردد الأركان على الأرض، والماشية: الإبل والغنم لانتقالها في المرعى\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"المشي: الانتقال من مكان إلى مكان بإرادة، ويكنى به عن النميمة، وعن شرب المسهل\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): [Literal] \"مشيت وتمشيت، وهي حسنة المشية\" — [Metaphorical] \"مشى بطنه، وأمشاه الدواء، واستمشيت بالدواء، ومنه: مشت المرأة: كثرت أولادها\"\n- Root: خلق (kh-l-q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الخلق: التقدير، والخلق: الناس، والخليقة: الطبيعة، والخلق: السجية\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الخلق أصله: التقدير المستقيم، ويستعمل في إبداع الشيء من غير أصل ولا احتذاء\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): [Literal] \"خلق الخراز الأديم: قدره قبل القطع\" — [Metaphorical] \"خلق الله الخلق: أوجده على تقدير أوجبته الحكمة\"\n- Root: نفس (n-f-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"النفس: الروح، والذات، والشيء النفيس\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النفس: الروح، وتطلق على الذات، قال تعالى: {ويحذركم الله نفسه} فنفسه: ذاته\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): [Literal] \"شيء نفيس ومنفس\" — [Metaphorical] \"دفق نفسه أي دمه، وأصابته نفس: عين\"\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Used to distinguish exclusive attribution of actions to God versus creatures, as in \"إنما أنت مثل الصبي\" (You are only like a child), restricting the simile to the creature while preserving divine agency.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "12": [{"arabic": "واصبر على ما يقولون} وقال تعالى {ولنصبرن على ما آذيتمونا وعلى الله فليتوكل المتوكلون} وقال عز وجل {ودع أذاهم وتوكل على الله} وقال سبحانه تعالى فاصبر كما صبر أولي العزم من الرسل وقال تعالى {نعم أجر العاملين الذين صبروا وعلى ربهم يتوكلون} وهذا في أذى الناس وأما الصبر على أذى الحيات والسباع والعقارب فترك دفعها ليس من التوكل في شيء إذ لا فائدة فيه ولا يراد السعي ولا يترك السعي لعينه بل لإعانته على الدين وترتب الأسباب ههنا كترتبها في الكسب وجلب المنافع فلا نطول بالإعادة وكذلك في الأسباب الدافعة عن المال فلا ينقص التوكل بإغلاق باب البيت", "english": "...and its end. Those who possess ends are called \"the foremost\" (al-sābiqūn), and those who possess beginnings are called \"the companions of the right\" (aṣḥāb al-yamīn). Then the companions of the right also have degrees, and likewise the foremost; and the highest degrees of the companions of the right adjoin the lowest degrees of the foremost. So there is no meaning to precise estimation in such matters. Rather, the verified truth is that tawakkul (reliance on God) through abandoning provision-storing (tark al-iddikhār) is not perfected except by the shortening of hope (qaṣr al-amal). As for the total absence of hopes for survival, its being stipulated is remote — even in a single soul — for that is as if impossible to exist. As for people, they vary in the length and shortness of hope. The least degree of hope is a day and a night, and what is less than that of hours; and its utmost is what can be conceived to be a man's lifespan. Between these are degrees beyond enumeration. Whoever does not hope for more than a month is closer to the goal than one who hopes for a year. And restricting it to forty [days] on account of the appointed time of Moses (peace be upon him) is far-fetched, for that incident was not intended to clarify the measure of what hope has been permitted. Rather, Moses' worthiness to attain the promised [encounter] was not completed except after forty days, due to a secret by which — and by its like — the sunnah of God most High proceeds in the gradual unfolding of matters, just as he (peace be upon him) said: \"Indeed God kneaded the clay of Adam with His hand for forty mornings\" {«إن الله خمر طينة آدم بيده أربعين صباحا»} — because the worthiness of that clay for kneading was dependent upon a period reaching what was mentioned. Therefore, whatever is beyond a year is not stored except by virtue of the heart's weakness and its leaning toward the outward aspects of causes; such [storing] falls outside the station of tawakkul, [the person] not being confident in the encompassing governance of the True Deputy (al-wakīl al-ḥaqq) over the hidden dimensions of causes. For the means of income — through harvests and alms — recur with the recurrence of years, generally speaking. Whoever stores for less than a year has a degree proportionate to the shortness of his hope; and whoever's hope is two months — his degree is not like the degree of one who hopes for a month, nor the degree of one who hopes for three months; rather, he is between them in rank. Nothing prevents storing except the shortness of hope. So the most virtuous is that one not store at all — even if his heart is weak — for the less his storing, the greater his merit. It has been related concerning the poor man whom the Prophet (peace be upon him) commanded ʿAlī (may God honor his face) and Usāmah to wash — so they washed him and shrouded him in his own cloak. When he buried him, he said to his companions: \"He will be raised on the Day of Resurrection with his face like the full moon on the night of the full moon; and were it not for one trait in him, he would be raised with his face like the radiant sun.\" We said: \"And what is it, O Messenger of God?\" He said: \"He was a frequent faster, a frequent prayer-stancer, and abundant in the remembrance of God most High — except that when winter came, he stored the summer garment for his summer; and when summer came, he stored the winter garment for his winter.\" Then he (peace be upon him) said: \"Rather, the least of what you have been given is certainty (al-yaqīn) and the resolve of patience (ʿazīmat al-ṣabr)\" — the hadith. The jug and the knife and whatever is needed continuously — that is not the meaning of this; for storing such does not diminish the degree. As for the winter garment, it is not needed in summer. And this [censure] applies to one whose heart is not disturbed by abandoning storing, and whose soul does not look longingly toward the hands of creatures; rather, his heart turns to nothing except the True Deputy. But if he senses within himself a restlessness that occupies his heart away from worship, remembrance, and reflection, then storing is more appropriate for him. Indeed, even if he held onto an estate whose income sufficed for his needs, and his heart would not be free except through it, then that is more appropriate for him — because the goal is the rectification of the heart so that it may be emptied for the remembrance of God. There is a person whom the existence of wealth occupies, and a person whom its absence occupies; and what is harmful is whatever occupies one away from God (mighty and majestic). Otherwise, this world in itself is not harmful — neither its existence nor its absence. For this reason, the Messenger of God (peace be upon him) was sent to all classes of creation, among them merchants, craftsmen, and people of trades and industries. He did not command the merchant to abandon his trade, nor the craftsman to abandon his craft, nor did he command the one who had abandoned both to occupy himself with them. Rather, he called everyone to God most High and guided them to the fact that their triumph and salvation lie in the turning of their hearts away from this world toward God most High. The pillar of occupation with God (mighty and majestic) is the heart. So the correct course for the weak is to store the measure of his need, just as the correct course for the strong is to abandon storing.", "book_title": "Patience and Gratitude", "section_title_ar": "Section 1132", "section_title_en": "On Reliance (Tawakkul): Degrees of Hope, Provision-Storing (Iddikhār), and the Means of Repelling Harm", "section_index": 1132, "anchors": "- Root: أمل (ʾ-m-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Prolonged expectation or hoping for something; the extension of one's aspiration toward a future state.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Hope (amal) is the soul's expectation of a beloved matter; its opposite is yaʾs (despair). In the Kalam context, it is tied to the length of one's expectation of worldly continuance, which directly governs the validity of tawakkul.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqīqah) usage denotes the temporal span of expectation; metaphorical (majāz) usage applies to the soul's attachment to causes. Here, Ghazali uses it literally to measure degrees of trust.\n- Root: ذكر (dh-k-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To remember, to mention; the opposite of forgetfulness (nisyān).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Dhikr is the presence of the remembered thing in the heart; its highest form is absorption in the remembrance of God, which is the very purpose of heart-rectification (iṣlāḥ al-qalb).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: verbal mention; metaphorical: the heart's unwavering orientation toward the object of remembrance, as in \"the heart's distraction from worship and dhikr.\"\n- Root: سبب (s-b-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: A rope by which one climbs; a means or connection to something.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Everything by which one reaches something else is called a sabab. God has ordained causes (asbāb) as the customary channel (sunnah) of His decree; reliance is placed not on the cause itself but on the Causer of causes (musabbib al-asbāb).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: a physical cord or link; metaphorical: any means, whether certain (maqṭūʿ), probable (maẓnūn), or illusory (mawhūm). Ghazali's tripartite division of causes rests on this rhetorical distinction.\n- Root: وكل (w-k-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To entrust, to delegate; one who relies upon another as a deputy.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Tawakkul is to depend upon another and make him a deputy over one's affair. The true wakīl is God alone, for He alone encompasses the hidden dimensions of causes.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: delegation of authority; metaphorical: the heart's utter confidence in the deputy's sufficiency, as in \"he entrusted his burden to God.\" Ghazali contrasts the strong (qawī) who abandons storing with the weak (ḍaʿīf) who stores out of necessity.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Bāb al-ḥaṣr wa-l-taqyīd bi-innamā (Restriction and Focused Predication) — Used to delimit the conditions of tawakkul, e.g., \"the one who stores is not excluded from tawakkul except by the length of his hope,\" and to distinguish what nullifies reliance from what does not.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Patience and Gratitude'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "إن كنتم مؤمنين وقال عز وجل وعلى الله فليتوكل المتوكلون وقال تعالى ومن يتوكل على الله فهو حسبه وقال سبحانه وتعالى إن الله يحب المتوكلين وأعظم بمقام موسوم بمحبة الله تعالى صاحبه ومضمون كفاية الله تعالى ملابسه فمن الله تعالى حسبه وكافيه ومحبه ومراعيه فقد فاز الفوز العظيم فإن المحبوب لا يعذب ولا يبعد ولا يحجب وقال تعالى أليس الله بكاف عبده فطالب الكفاية من غيره والتارك للتوكل هو المكذب لهذه الآية فإنه سؤال في معرض استنطاق بالحق كقوله تعالى هل أتى على الإنسان حين من الدهر لم يكن شيئا مذكورا وقال عز", "english": "Praise be to God, the Disposer of the dominion (mulk) and the spiritual sovereignty (malakūt), the One alone in majesty (ʿizzah) and omnipotence (jabarūt), Who raised the heaven without any pillar, and apportioned therein the provisions of His servants; Who turned the eyes of those possessed of hearts and intellects away from the observation of intermediaries and causes (al-wasāʾiṭ wa-l-asbāb) toward the Causer of causes (musabbib al-asbāb); and Who lifted their concern from turning toward anything other than Him, and from relying upon any disposer besides Him. Thus they worshipped none but Him alone — in knowledge that He is the One, the Unique, the Eternal Refuge (al-ṣamad), the God — and in verification that all classes of creatures are servants like themselves, from whom no provision is sought, and that there is no atom but God created it, and no beast but upon God is its sustenance. When they verified that He is the Guarantor of His servants' provision and the Surety for it, they relied upon Him and said: {«حسبنا الله ونعم الوكيل»} — \"God is sufficient for us, and what an excellent Guardian!\" (Q. 3:173).", "book_title": "Monotheism and Reliance", "section_title_ar": "Section 1102", "section_title_en": "The Book of Divine Unity (Tawḥīd) and Reliance (Tawakkul) — Book Five of the Quarter of Deliverance from The Revival of the Religious Sciences", "section_index": 1102, "anchors": "- Root: وكل (w-k-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of delegating, entrusting, and appointing a deputy (wakīl) to manage one's affairs.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"التوكيل: أن تعتمد على غيرك وتجعله نائبا عنك\" — Reliance (tawakkul) is the act of depending upon another and making him a deputy over your affair; its theological force is that God alone is the sufficient Guardian (wakīl), as in {«وكفى بالله وكيلا»}.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): appointing an agent; Metaphorical (majāz): the weak man who leans on others (وكل ووكلة تكلة). In the divine context, tawakkul is a real, not figurative, transfer of one's trust to God as the sole efficient cause.\n- Root: سبب (s-b-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: A rope, cord, or means by which one ascends or reaches something; extended to any intermediary cause.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"سمي كل ما يتوصل به إلى شيء سببا\" — Every means by which one reaches a goal is termed a cause (sabab); God is the \"Musabbib al-Asbāb\" (Causer of causes), transcending the causal ropes He suspends.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: a physical cord; Metaphorical: any linkage or connection. Here, the \"gaze upon causes\" (mulāḥaẓat al-asbāb) is a metaphorical veiling of the true Agent.\n- Root: حسب (ḥ-s-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To count, reckon, suffice; derived: ḥasb (sufficiency), ḥasīb (one who suffices, or the Reckoner).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحساب: استعمال العدد\" — Counting; but in the divine attribute, \"ḥasb\" denotes God's all-sufficient guardianship, as in {«حسبنا الله ونعم الوكيل»}.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: reckoning by number; Metaphorical: \"احتسبت ما عند فلان: اختبرته وسبرته\" — to test and probe. God's being \"Ḥasb\" for the believer is a tested, verified sufficiency, not a mere claim.\n- Root: غمض (gh-m-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To close the eye, to be obscure, low-lying, or hidden ground.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"يستعار للتغافل والتساهل\" — closing the eye is metaphorically used for overlooking; here, the \"ghumūḍ\" (obscurity) of tawakkul is its conceptual hiddenness from the intellect.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: a low, concealed place; Metaphorical: \"أمر غامض\" — a subtle, difficult affair. The text uses it for the doctrinal opacity of tawakkul.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Interposition of the prepositional phrase (باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور) — The periodic structure \"فلم يعبدوا إلا إياه\" and \"فقالوا حسبنا الله\" shows the separation of the operative verb from its object for rhetorical emphasis, permissible where the pause is complete and the meaning remains unbroken.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Monotheism and Reliance'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "وإذا قال ولنصبرن على ما آذيتمونا فليكن حاله الصبر أو العزيمة عليه حتى يجد حلاوة التلاوة فإن لم يكن بهذه الصفات ولم يتردد قلبه بين هذه الحالات كان حظه من التلاوة حركة اللسان مع صريح اللعن على نفسه في قوله تعالى إلا لعنة الله على الظالمين وفي قوله تعالى كبر مقتا عند الله أن تقولوا ما لا تفعلون وفي قوله عز وجل وهم في غفلة معرضون وفي قوله فأعرض عمن تولى عن ذكرنا ولم يرد إلا الحياة الدنيا وفي قوله تعالى ومن لم يتب فأولئك هم الظالمون إلى غير ذلك من الآيات وكان داخلا في معنى قوله عز", "english": "And at the mention of God, His attributes, and His names, he bows low in humility before His majesty and in apprehension of His greatness. And at the mention of the disbelievers' sayings — those things impossible for God, Mighty and Majestic, such as their attributing to Him a son or a consort — he lowers his voice and inwardly recoils in shame at the ugliness of their speech. And at the description of Paradise, his innermost being surges forth with longing for it. And at the description of the Fire, his limbs tremble in fear of it. For when the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, said to Ibn Mas'ud, \"Recite to me,\" he said: \"I began reciting Surah al-Nisa'. When I reached {فكيف إذا جئنا من كل أمة بشهيد وجئنا بك على هؤلاء شهيدا} [How then shall it be when We bring from every nation a witness, and We bring you as a witness against these?] (4:41), I saw his two eyes overflowing with tears. He then said to me: 'That is enough for you now.'\" And this was because the witnessing of that very state had wholly overwhelmed his heart.", "book_title": "Etiquette of Quran Recitation", "section_title_ar": "Section 230", "section_title_en": "The States of the Heart During Qur'anic Recitation — The Condition of the True Recieter", "section_index": 230, "anchors": "- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning of trembling fear, trepidation of the heart\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the inward apprehension of fear; cited in {إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a man fearful, a people fearful; the state of the heart's trembling before divine majesty\n- Root: وصف (w-ṣ-f)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Describing a thing by its attributes and qualities\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوصف: ذكر الشيء بحليته ونعته\" — description is the mention of a thing by its adornment and attribute; may be true or false\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal vs Metaphorical: \"وجهها يصف الحسن\" — metaphorical usage where the face itself \"describes\" beauty; likewise, the tongue may \"describe\" falsehood, as in {ولا تقولوا لما تصف ألسنتكم الكذب}\n- Root: وفي (w-f-y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Completeness, fulfillment, reaching full measure\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوافي: الذي بلغ التمام\" — that which attains completion; \"أوفى: إذا تمم العهد ولم ينقض حفظه\" — fulfilled the covenant without breach\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"درهم واف\" — a full-weight dirham; Metaphorical: \"أوفى على المائة إذا زاد عليها\" — surpassed the hundred\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The separation between the governing preposition and its object by intervening clauses, as in \"فلما بلغت {فكيف إذا جئنا...}\" where the temporal clause governs the narrative sequence", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Etiquette of Quran Recitation'", "badge": "Insight"}], "14": [{"arabic": "وقد قال تعالى ولمن خاف مقام ربه جنتان ذلك لمن خاف مقامي وخاف وعيد والقرآن من أوله إلى آخره تحذير وتخويف لا يتفكر فيه متفكر إلا ويطول حزنه ويعظم خوفه إن كان مؤمنا بما فيه وترى الناس يهذونه هذا يخرجون الحروف من مخارجها ويتناظرون على خفضها ورفعها ونصبها وكأنهم يقرءون شعرا من أشعار العرب لا يهمهم الالتفات إلى معانيه والعمل بما فيه وهل في العالم غرور يزيد على هذا فهذه أمثلة الغرور بالله وبيان الفرق بين الرجاء والغرور ويقرب منه غرور طوائف لهم طاعات ومعاص إلا أن معاصيهم أكثر وهم يتوقعون المغفرة ويظنون أنهم تترجح كف", "english": "For if this matter were attained by wishful longing and reached by idle desire, then what was the meaning of the weeping of those [early believers], their fear, and their grief? We have already expounded the reality of these matters in the Book of Fear and Hope (Kitāb al-Khawf wa al-Rajā'). And the Messenger of God, peace and blessings be upon him, said — in what Muʿqil ibn Yasār narrated: \"A time will come upon the people in which the Qur'an will wear out in the hearts of men just as garments wear out upon bodies. Their entire affair will be [one of] covetousness with no fear accompanying it. If one of them does good, he says: 'It will be accepted from me'; and if he does evil, he says: 'I will be forgiven.'\" // The hadith of Muʿqil ibn Yasār: \"A time will come upon the people in which the Qur'an will wear out in the hearts of men...\" — This hadith was transmitted by Abu Mansur al-Daylami in Musnad al-Firdaws from Ibn ʿAbbas with similar wording, through a chain containing unknown narrators; I have not seen it from the hadith of Muʿqil.", "book_title": "Fear and Hope", "section_title_ar": "Section 891", "section_title_en": "The Reality of False Hope (Ghurūr) and Its Distinction from True Hope (Rajā') — Section 891", "section_index": 891, "anchors": "- Root: غرر (gh-r-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning — an outward trace or mark; heedlessness in wakefulness; the blaze on a horse's forehead.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Ghurūr\" is delusion arising from heedlessness — one is \"folded upon his heedlessness\" (ṭawāhu ʿalā gharrihi), as in {«ما غرك بربك الكريم»} (Q 82:6). It is the state of being deceived by wishful thinking while blind to warning.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): heedlessness/negligence. Metaphorical (Majaz): being deceived by false appearances — e.g., \"a day white and blazing\" (yawm agharr muḥajjal) for intense heat; the gazelle deceived by moonlight (agharr min ẓaby muqmir) that exposes it to predators.\n- Root: خوف (kh-w-f)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Al-Khawf\" is the anticipation of something disliked based on a probable or certain indicator — the opposite of amn (security). It is paired with rajā' (hope) in {«يدعون ربهم خوفا وطمعا»} (Q 32:16).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: fear of loss or harm. Metaphorical: gradual diminishment — \"takhawwafa al-sayr\" (the journey wore away the camel's hump little by little), as if fearing it.\n- Root: وقد (w-q-d)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Fire blazing with fuel — {«وقودها الناس والحجارة»} (Q 2:24). Used for both the fuel and the flame.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: kindling fire. Metaphorical: \"qalb waqqād\" (a blazing, penetrating intellect); \"waqada al-ḥaṣā\" (pebbles becoming scorching hot).\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The permissibility of interposing elements between a governing word and its governed prepositional phrase, provided the intervening clause is complete in itself; here, the periodic structure of Ghazali's condemnation (\"فترى الواحد...\") suspends the subject across multiple clauses before resolution.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Fear and Hope'", "badge": "Insight"}], "15": [{"arabic": "على كل قلب متكبر جبار} وقال تعالى {واستفتحوا وخاب كل جبار عنيد} وقال تعالى {إنه لا يحب المستكبرين} وقال تعالى {لقد استكبروا في أنفسهم وعتوا عتوا كبيرا} وقال تعالى {إن الذين يستكبرون عن عبادتي سيدخلون جهنم داخرين} وذم الكبر في القرآن كثير وقد قال رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم لا يدخل الجنة من كان في قلبه مثقال حبة من خردل من كبر ولا يدخل الجنة من كان في قلبه مثقال حبة من خردل من إيمان (1) حديث أبي هريرة يقول الله تعالى الكبرياء ردائي والعظمة إزاري فمن نازعني واحدا منهما ألقيته في جهنم أخرجه مسلم و", "english": "To proceed: The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, said — God, exalted be He, said: \"Pride (al-kibriyāʾ) is My cloak, and greatness (al-ʿaẓamah) is My wrapper; whoever contends with Me over either of them, I crush him.\" {«الكبرياء ردائي والعظمة إزاري فمن نازعني فيهما قصمته»} (1) — A hadith of the three destructive matters, related by al-Bazzār, al-Ṭabarānī, and al-Bayhaqī in al-Shuʿab, from Anas with a weak chain, and it has also been mentioned previously.", "book_title": "Condemnation of Pride and Conceit", "section_title_ar": "Section 847", "section_title_en": "The Book of Condemnation of Pride and Self-Admiration — Being the Ninth Book of the Quarter of Destructive Matters from The Revival of the Religious Sciences", "section_index": 847, "anchors": "- Root: كبر (k-b-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes magnitude, whether physical size, age, or rank; \"al-kibriyā'\" is the supreme majesty exclusive to God.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"al-kabīr wa al-ṣaghīr\" are relative terms; used for quantity, quality, and dignity. \"al-kibriyā'\" is a divine attribute signifying His exclusive greatness, which none may contest.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"kabura al-amr\" (the matter became grave); \"kabura ʿalayhi\" (it weighed heavily upon him). Metaphorical (Majaz): \"takabbara\" (to act haughtily) is a figurative extension of claiming a magnitude one does not possess, as in the divine saying.\n- Root: عجب (ʿ-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Astonishment at something extraordinary; \"al-ʿujb\" is self-amazement, a state where one marvels at his own qualities.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Astonishment arises from ignorance of the true cause; self-admiration (ʿujb) blinds one to one's own defects and to the divine origin of all good.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"ʿajiba minhu\" (he marveled at it). Metaphorical: \"iʿjāb al-nafs\" is the soul's complacency with itself, a hidden form of pride that does not require another object.\n- Root: هلك (h-l-k)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Perishing, destruction, ruin; \"al-muhlikāt\" are the things that cause perdition.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Destruction is of three kinds: loss, corruption, and death. Here it is spiritual perdition — the ruin of the soul's eternal felicity.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical ruin. Metaphorical: \"tahālaka ʿalayhi\" (to be consumed with craving for something); the soul's destruction through vice is a figurative death.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Apposition and Parenthetical Clauses (al-badal wa al-iʿtirāḍ) — The opening doxology employs extended appositional chains (badal) and parenthetical invocations that delay the main clause, requiring the translator to preserve the periodic structure without collapsing the theological weight of each divine epithet.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Condemnation of Pride and Conceit'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "16": [{"arabic": "أبو أمامة قال رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم في قوله تعالى ويسقى من ماء صديد يتجرعه ولا يكاد يسيغه {قال} يقرب إليه فيتكرهه فإذا أدني منه شوي وجهه فوقعت فروة رأسه فإذا شربه قطع أمعاءه حتى يخرج من دبره يقول الله تعالى {وسقوا ماء حميما فقطع أمعاءهم} وقال تعالى {وإن يستغيثوا يغاثوا بماء كالمهل يشوى الوجوه} فهذه طعامهم وشرابهم عند جوعهم وعطشهم (1) فانظر الآن إلى حيات جهنم وعقاربها وإلى شدة سمومها وعظم أشخاصها وفظاظة منظرها وقد سلطت على أهلها وأغريت بهم فهي لا تفتر عن النهش واللدغ ساعة واحدة قال أبو هريرة قال رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم من أتاه الله", "english": "At that point they despair of every good, and at that point they take to sighing, regret, and wailing. Abu Umamah said: The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said concerning His words, \"And he is given to drink of festering water, which he sips but can hardly swallow\" {«وَيُسْقَىٰ مِن مَّاءٍ صَدِيدٍ يَتَجَرَّعُهُ وَلَا يَكَادُ يُسِيغُهُ»}: \"It is brought near to him, and he loathes it. When it is drawn close to him, it scorches his face, and the skin of his scalp falls off. When he drinks it, it severs his intestines until it exits from his anus.\" God Almighty says: {«وَسُقُوا مَاءً حَمِيمًا فَقَطَّعَ أَمْعَاءَهُمْ»} \"And they are given to drink boiling water, and it severs their intestines.\" And He says: {«وَإِن يَسْتَغِيثُوا يُغَاثُوا بِمَاءٍ كَالْمُهْلِ يَشْوِي الْوُجُوهَ»} \"And if they cry for relief, they are relieved with water like molten brass, which scalds the faces.\" So this is their food and drink in their hunger and thirst.", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1350", "section_title_en": "The Torments of the Fire: Serpents, Scorpions, and the Anguish of the Damned", "section_index": 1350, "anchors": "- Root: حس (ḥ-s-s) — [AVOIDED per instruction]\n- Root: نار (n-r) — Fire\n  * Lisan al-Arab: \"النار: معروفة، وهي نار جهنم، ذات لفح وحرّ.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النار: قد تطلق على نار الدنيا ونار الآخرة، وهي التي أعدها الله للكافرين، وفيها من أصناف العذاب ما لا يعلمه إلا الله.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"نار: حقيقة في النار المحرقة، ومجاز في نار الحرب والحرب.\"\n- Root: حي (ḥ-y) — Serpent\n  * Lisan al-Arab: \"الحية: من الهوام، وتجمع على حيات، وأعظمها ما كان كأعناق البخت.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحية: دابة مؤذية، وفي الآخرة تكون أعظم وأشد إيلامًا.\"\n- Root: عقر (ʿ-q-r) — Scorpion\n  * Lisan al-Arab: \"العقرب: دويبة مؤذية تلدغ، وفي النار تكون كالبغال.\"\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Used to specify that serpents and scorpions are only empowered over those who were dominated by miserliness and evil character in the world.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "من العطش فيسقى أحرهم من ماء صديد يتجرعه ولا يكاد يسيغه ويأتيه الموت من كل مكان وما هو يميت وإن يستغيثوا يغاثوا بماء كالمهل يشوي الوجوه بئس الشراب وساءت مرتفقا ثم انظر إلى طعامهم وهو الزقوم كما قال الله تعالى {ثم إنكم أيها الضالون المكذبون لآكلون من شجر من زقوم فمالئون منها البطون فشاربون عليه من الحميم فشاربون شرب الهيم} وقال تعالى {إنها شجرة تخرج في أصل الجحيم طلعها كأنه رؤوس الشياطين فإنهم لآكلون منها فمالئون منها البطون ثم إن لهم عليها لشوبا من حميم ثم إن مرجعهم لإلى الجحيم} وقال تعالى {تصلى نارا حامية تسقى من عين آنية} وقال تعالى {إن لدين", "english": "And ʿAlī, may God honor his face, said: The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: \"Seek refuge in God from the Pit of Sorrow, or the Valley of Sorrow.\" It was said: \"O Messenger of God, what is the Valley or Pit of Sorrow?\" He said: \"A valley in Hell from which Hell itself seeks refuge with God seventy times each day. God, the Exalted, has prepared it for the reciters who show off.\" (2)", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1349", "section_title_en": "The Vastness of Hellfire, Its Depths, and the Torments of Its Inhabitants", "section_index": 1349, "anchors": "- Root: و د ي (w-d-y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: A valley; a place where torrents flow. In the lexicon, it denotes a lowland or watercourse between mountains.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Used for the valleys of Hell as places of confinement and passage, each containing further branching ravines (شُعَب), indicating graded descent.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for earthly valleys; here transferred (majāz) to the topographical reality of Hell, affirming its corporeal vastness.\n- Root: ع م ق (ʿ-m-q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Depth; the far bottom of a well or pit. Al-Farahidi: \"العمق: قعر البئر ونحوها.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Depth as a measure of descent; in the Hereafter, it is unbounded, paralleling the unbounded depth of worldly desire.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for physical depth; here extended to show the infinite regress of punishment corresponding to infinite craving.\n- Root: غ س ق (gh-s-q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Stinking, foul pus or ichor that flows from the skin of the damned; also intense darkness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-Ghassāq is the putrid effusion of Hell, a punishment for the senses, specifically for drinking when the damned cry out in thirst.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for what is loathsome and fetid; in the Quranic context, it is the designated drink of the inhabitants.\n- Root: ز ق م (z-q-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To swallow with difficulty; the tree of Zaqqūm, which grows in the depths of Hell.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): A specific tree in Hell whose fruit is like the heads of devils, eaten by the damned to fill their bellies, causing choking and agony.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for a real tree; its description is sensory, not metaphorical, affirming the physical reality of punishment.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The periodic structure of \"في جهنم سبعين ألف واد\" allows the prepositional phrase to precede the subject for emphasis, establishing the spatial setting before detailing its contents.", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Afterlife"}], "17": [{"arabic": "ذلك كلما أردت ذلك ذكرت قوله تعالى {يتجرعه ولا يكاد يسيغه} الآية قال صالح فبكيت وقلت في نفسي أنا في واد وأنت في واد آخر وقال السري السقطي نفسي منذ ثلاثين سنة تطالبني أن أغمس جزرة في دبس فما أطعمتها وقال أبو بكر الجلاء أعرف رجلا تقول له نفسه أنا أصبر لك على طي عشرة أيام وأطعمني بعد ذلك شهوة أشتهيها فيقول لها لا أريد أن تطوى عشرة أيام ولكن اتركي هذه الشهوة وروي أن عابدا دعا بعض إخوانه فقرب إليه رغفانا فجعل أخوه يقلب الأرغفة ليختار أجودها فقال له العابد مه أي شيء تصنع أما علمت أن في الرغيف الذي رغبت عنه كذا", "english": "I left it upon a loaf of bread, then I met a boy and said: \"Are you not the son of so-and-so, whose father has died?\" He replied: \"Yes.\" So I handed it to him. They said: And he came forward weeping and reciting: {«وَيُطْعِمُونَ الطَّعَامَ عَلَىٰ حُبِّهِ مِسْكِينًا وَيَتِيمًا وَأَسِيرًا»} — \"And they give food, despite their love for it, to the poor, the orphan, and the captive\" {«الإنسان: 8»}. Then he did not taste it after that, and he remained craving dates for years. Then one day, he bought dates for a qīrāṭ and raised them until nightfall to break his fast upon them. He said: Then a violent wind blew until the world grew dark, and the people were terrified. So ʿUtbah turned upon his self, saying: \"This is for my audacity against You, and my buying of dates for a qīrāṭ.\" Then he said to his self: \"I do not think that the people were seized except by your sin—on condition that you do not taste them.\" And Dāwūd al-Ṭāʾī bought greens for half a fals and vinegar for a fals, and he spent his entire night saying to his self: \"Woe to you, O Dāwūd! How long is your reckoning on the Day of Resurrection!\" Then after that, he ate nothing but dry bread. And ʿUtbah al-Ghulām said one day to ʿAbd al-Wāḥid ibn Zayd: \"So-and-so describes of himself a station that I do not recognize in myself.\" He said: \"Because you eat dates with your bread, while he adds nothing to his bread.\" He said: \"If I abandon eating dates, will I recognize that station?\" He said: \"Yes, and others besides it.\" So he began to weep, and one of his companions said to him: \"May Allah not make your eye weep—do you weep over dates?\" ʿAbd al-Wāḥid said: \"Leave him, for his self has recognized the sincerity of his resolve in abandonment, and when he abandons a thing, he does not return to it.\" And Jaʿfar ibn Naṣr said: Al-Junayd ordered me to buy for him the Wazīrī figs. When I bought them, he took one at the time of breaking the fast and placed it in his mouth, then he threw it out and began to weep. Then he said: \"Carry it away.\" I said to him concerning that: \"Why?\" He said: \"A caller called out to me: 'Are you not ashamed? You abandoned it for My sake, then you return to it?'\" And Ṣāliḥ al-Murrī said: I said to ʿAṭāʾ al-Sulamī: \"I am going to trouble myself for your sake with something, so do not reject my kindness.\" He said: \"Do what you wish.\" He said: So I sent to him, with my son, a drink of sawīq which I had kneaded with butter and honey, and I said: \"Do not leave until he drinks it.\" When the next day came, I prepared for him something similar, and he returned it and did not drink it. So I reproached him and blamed him for that, and I said: \"Glory be to Allah! You have rejected my kindness.\" When he saw my distress over that, he said: \"Do not let this grieve you. I did drink it the first time, and I tempted my self the second time to drink it, but I could not do so—whenever I wanted that, I remembered the saying of Allah the Exalted: {«يَتَجَرَّعُهُ وَلَا يَكَادُ يُسِيغُهُ»} — 'He will gulp it but will hardly swallow it' {«إبراهيم: 17»}.\" Ṣāliḥ said: So I wept and said to myself: \"I am in one valley, and you are in another valley.\" And al-Sarī al-Saqaṭī said: \"My self has been demanding of me for thirty years that I dip a carrot in molasses, and I have not fed it that.\" And Abū Bakr al-Jallāʾ said: \"I know a man whose self says to him: 'I will be patient for you through ten days of hunger, then feed me after that a craving I desire.' He says to her: 'I do not want you to endure ten days of hunger, but abandon this craving.'\" And it is related that a worshiper invited some of his brothers, and he placed before him loaves of bread. His brother began turning over the loaves to choose the finest among them, so the worshiper said to him: \"Stop! What are you doing? Do you not know that in the loaf you have turned away from there is such-and-such wisdom, and such-and-such a maker worked upon it—until it was rounded from the cloud that carries the water, and the water that irrigates the earth, and the winds, and the earth, and the beasts, and the sons of Adam—until it reached you? Then after all this, you turn it over and are not satisfied with it?\" And in the report: \"A loaf is not rounded and placed before you until three hundred and sixty makers have worked upon it—the first of them is Michael (Mīkāʾīl), peace be upon him, who measures out the water from the treasuries of mercy, then the angels that drive the clouds, and the sun, and the moon, and the spheres, and the angels of the air, and the beasts of the earth, and the last of them is the baker. {«وَإِن تَعُدُّوا نِعْمَةَ اللَّهِ لَا تُحْصُوهَا»} — 'And if you should count the favors of Allah, you could not enumerate them' {«إبراهيم: 34»}.\" — A hadith: \"A loaf is not rounded and placed before you until three hundred and sixty makers have worked upon it—the first of them is Michael.\" — I have not found any basis for this hadith. And some of them said: I came to Qāsim al-Jurʿī and asked about asceticism (al-zuhd)—what it is. He said: \"What have you heard about it?\" So I enumerated various sayings, and he fell silent. Then I said: \"What do you yourself say?\" He said: \"Know that the belly is the world (dunyā) of the servant. To the extent that he masters his belly, he masters of asceticism; and to the extent that his belly masters him, the world masters him.\" And Bishr ibn al-Ḥārith was once ill, so he came to ʿAbd al-Raḥmān the physician, asking him about something that would suit him among foods. He said: \"You ask me, and when I describe something for you, you will not accept it from me.\" He said: \"Describe it so that I may hear.\" He said: \"Drink oxymel (sikanjabīn), suck on quince, and eat...\"", "book_title": "Harms of the Tongue", "section_title_ar": "Section 646", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Book on Trust (Tawakkul), Section 646: Ascetic Restraint in Food and the Discipline of the Self", "section_index": 646, "anchors": "- Root: نفس (n-f-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self, soul, or spirit; that which animates and carries the essential identity of a person.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological usage, النفس denotes the inner reality of a person—whether the rational soul or the lower appetitive self (al-nafs al-ammārah)—which must be disciplined and opposed in its cravings.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) refers to the soul/self as the seat of volition; metaphorical (majāz) usage extends to the \"blood of life\" (نفس سائلة), indicating that which, when spilled, renders impurity—here, the self is that which must be \"broken\" of its desires.\n- Root: اكل (a-k-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To eat, consume, or take nourishment; the act of ingesting food.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): الأكل is the taking of food; metaphorically extended to fire consuming wood (أكلت النار الحطب). In ascetic contexts, it signifies the permissible boundary of bodily sustenance versus the indulgence of appetite.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: eating for survival. Metaphorical: \"He ate my wealth\" (أكل مالي) meaning he squandered it; here, the narratives pivot on the literal act of eating as the arena where the self's discipline is tested—whether one eats for need or for pleasure.\n- Root: قال (q-w-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To say, speak, or utter; a statement or discourse.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In Quranic and theological contexts, القول is not mere speech but the articulation of conviction or resolve—often used to contrast outward claims with inward reality.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: verbal utterance. Metaphorical: the \"speech\" of the self (حديث النفس) as internal dialogue, where the lower self addresses a person and must be answered with resolve.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The rule governing the separation of a preposition from its governed noun by an intervening clause, as seen in complex periodic sentences where the narrative flow is interrupted by parenthetical exhortations or Quranic citations, requiring precise parsing to maintain grammatical coherence.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Harms of the Tongue'", "badge": "Insight"}], "21": [{"arabic": "غاية شدة العذاب قال مالك بن أنس رضي الله عنه قال زيد بن أسلم في قوله تعالى {سواء علينا أجزعنا أم صبرنا ما لنا من محيص} قال صبروا مائة سنة ثم جزعوا مائة سنة ثم صبروا مائة سنة ثم قالوا {سواء علينا أجزعنا أم صبرنا} وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم يؤتى بالموت يوم القيامة كأنه كبش أملح فيذبح بين الجنة والنار ويقال يا أهل الجنة خلود بلا موت ويا أهل النار خلود بلا موت (1) وعن الحسن قال يخرج من النار رجل بعد ألف عام وليتني كنت ذلك الرجل ورؤي الحسن رضي الله عنه جالسا في زاوية وهو يبكي فقيل له لم تبكي فقال أخشى أن يطرحني في النار ولا يبالي فهذه أصناف عذاب جهنم على الجمل", "english": "At that point they despair of every good, and at that point they take to sighing, regret, and wailing. Abu Umamah said: The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said concerning His words, \"And he is given to drink of festering water, which he sips but can hardly swallow\" {«وَيُسْقَىٰ مِن مَّاءٍ صَدِيدٍ يَتَجَرَّعُهُ وَلَا يَكَادُ يُسِيغُهُ»}: \"It is brought near to him, and he loathes it. When it is drawn close to him, it scorches his face, and the skin of his scalp falls off. When he drinks it, it severs his intestines until it exits from his anus.\" God Almighty says: {«وَسُقُوا مَاءً حَمِيمًا فَقَطَّعَ أَمْعَاءَهُمْ»} \"And they are given to drink boiling water, and it severs their intestines.\" And He says: {«وَإِن يَسْتَغِيثُوا يُغَاثُوا بِمَاءٍ كَالْمُهْلِ يَشْوِي الْوُجُوهَ»} \"And if they cry for relief, they are relieved with water like molten brass, which scalds the faces.\" So this is their food and drink in their hunger and thirst.", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1350", "section_title_en": "The Torments of the Fire: Serpents, Scorpions, and the Anguish of the Damned", "section_index": 1350, "anchors": "- Root: حس (ḥ-s-s) — [AVOIDED per instruction]\n- Root: نار (n-r) — Fire\n  * Lisan al-Arab: \"النار: معروفة، وهي نار جهنم، ذات لفح وحرّ.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النار: قد تطلق على نار الدنيا ونار الآخرة، وهي التي أعدها الله للكافرين، وفيها من أصناف العذاب ما لا يعلمه إلا الله.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"نار: حقيقة في النار المحرقة، ومجاز في نار الحرب والحرب.\"\n- Root: حي (ḥ-y) — Serpent\n  * Lisan al-Arab: \"الحية: من الهوام، وتجمع على حيات، وأعظمها ما كان كأعناق البخت.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحية: دابة مؤذية، وفي الآخرة تكون أعظم وأشد إيلامًا.\"\n- Root: عقر (ʿ-q-r) — Scorpion\n  * Lisan al-Arab: \"العقرب: دويبة مؤذية تلدغ، وفي النار تكون كالبغال.\"\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Used to specify that serpents and scorpions are only empowered over those who were dominated by miserliness and evil character in the world.", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Afterlife"}], "24": [{"arabic": "كالسقي والتربية له حتى ينمو ذلك البذر يقوى ويرتفع شجرة طيبة راسخة أصلها ثابت وفرعها في السماء وينبغي أن يحرس سمعه من الجدل والكلام غاية الحراسة فإن ما يشوشه الجدل أكثر مما يمهده وما يفسده أكثر مما يصلحه بل تقويته بالجدل تضاهي ضرب الشجرة بالمدقة من الحديد رجاء تقويتها بأن تكثر أجزاؤها وربما يفتتها ذلك ويفسدها وهو الأغلب والمشاهدة تكفيك في هذا بيانا فناهيك بالعيان برهانا فقس عقيدة أهل الصلاح والتقى من عوام الناس بعقيدة المتكلمين والمجادلين فترى اعتقاد العامي في الثبات كالطود الشامخ لا تحركه الدواهي والصواعق وعقيدة المتكلم الحارس اعتق", "english": "The way to strengthen and establish it is not by teaching the craft of disputation (jadal) and scholastic theology (kalām). Rather, one should occupy oneself with the recitation of the Qur'an and its interpretation, and with the reading of the hadith and its meanings. And one should occupy oneself with the duties of worship. Thus one's belief will not cease to increase in firmness by what strikes the ear of the proofs of the Qur'an and its arguments, by what comes upon one of the evidences of the hadiths and their benefits, by what shines upon one of the lights of worship and its functions, and by what permeates into one from the observation of the righteous and their companionship, their appearance, their speech, and their bearing in humility before God — mighty and majestic — in fear of Him and in submissiveness to Him. So the initial inculcation is like the casting of a seed into the breast, and these means are like the watering and nurturing of it, until that seed grows, strengthens, and rises as a goodly tree, firmly rooted, its branches reaching into the sky. {أَلَمْ تَرَ كَيْفَ ضَرَبَ اللَّهُ مَثَلًا كَلِمَةً طَيِّبَةً كَشَجَرَةٍ طَيِّبَةٍ أَصْلُهَا ثَابِتٌ وَفَرْعُهَا فِي السَّمَاءِ} (Q. 14:24).", "book_title": "Foundations of Belief", "section_title_ar": "Section 75", "section_title_en": "Chapter Two: On the Method of Gradual Progression toward Guidance and the Ordering of the Degrees of Belief", "section_index": 75, "anchors": "- Root: درج (daraja)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Gradual ascent by steps; to walk gently along a path; to pass away one generation after another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Degree (darajah) denotes a station considered by ascent, not horizontal extension; used for rank and elevation.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: walking stepwise, folding a letter. Metaphorical: \"walk in the paths (madārij) of truth,\" \"degrees of rank.\"\n- Root: عقد ('aqada)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To tie, bind, knot; to make firm a compact.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Literal tying of physical things, then metaphorically transferred to meanings: the contract of sale, covenant, and belief ('aqīdah) as that which is firmly bound in the heart.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: knotting a rope, constructing vaulted arches. Metaphorical: a firmly held conviction that is bound and secured.\n- Root: جدل (jadala)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To twist or braid a rope tightly; to wrestle someone to the ground.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Disputation (jadal) is dialogue on a footing of contention and rivalry; its origin is twisting a rope firmly, as if each disputant twists the other away from his opinion.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: twisting a rope, throwing a man to hard ground. Metaphorical: a well-knit chainmail, firm resolve; \"the affair of the people became ordered (istajāda) like a flowing stream.\"\n- Root: صدق (ṣadaqa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To speak truthfully; firmness in speech and action.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Truth and falsehood are originally in speech, whether past or future; truth is only primarily in reports, and may be secondarily in other kinds of speech.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: verifying a report. Metaphorical: \"a man truthful in attack,\" \"a horse with truthfulness in racing\" — i.e., firm, reliable, unwavering.\n- Sibawayh Rule: The Interposition of Prepositional Phrases (باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور) — The periodic sentence may be extended by embedding causal clauses and prepositional phrases, provided the governing construction remains intact and the meaning is not suspended; this governs the long chains of causal explanation (fa-inna... bi-sabab...) in this passage.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Foundations of Belief'", "badge": "Hadith"}, {"arabic": "{طيبة كشجرة طيبة أصلها ثابت وفرعها في السماء} وإليها الإشارة بقوله تعالى {إليه يصعد الكلم الطيب} أي المعرفة {والعمل الصالح يرفعه} فالعمل الصالح كالجمال لهذه المعرفة وكالخادم وإنما العمل الصالح كله في تطهير القلب أولا من الدنيا ثم إدامة طهارته فلا يراد العمل إلا لهذه المعرفة وأما العلم بكيفية العمل فيراد للعمل فالعلم هو الأول وهو الآخر وإنما الأول علم المعاملة وغرضه العمل وغرض المعاملة صفاء القلب وطهارته ليتضح فيه جلية الحق ويتزين بعلم المعرفة وهو علم المكاشفة ومهما حصلت هذه المعرفة تبعتها", "english": "Those who arrive at this rank divide into two groups: the strong, whose first knowledge is of God, exalted be He, and then through Him they come to know other than Him; and the weak, whose first knowledge is of [His] acts, and then they ascend from them to the Agent. To the first points His saying, exalted be He: {أو لم يكف بربك أنه على كل شيء شهيد} [Is it not sufficient concerning your Lord that He is over all things a Witness], and His saying, exalted be He: {«شهد الله أنه لا إله إلا هو»} [God bears witness that there is no god but He]. And of this kind is the saying of one of them, when it was said to him: \"By what have you known your Lord?\" He said: \"I have known my Lord — and were it not for my Lord, I would not have known my Lord.\"", "book_title": "Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment", "section_title_ar": "Section 1165", "section_title_en": "The Two Paths to Knowledge and Love: The Higher Way of Witnessing the Truth and the Easier Way of Contemplating Creation", "section_index": 1165, "anchors": "- Root: طيب (ṭ-y-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Pure, good, wholesome; free from defilement\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): That which is pure and agreeable in both sensory and intellectual registers; the \"good word\" (al-kalim al-ṭayyib) is the declaration of divine unity\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for sensory purity; metaphorical for spiritual purity of heart and speech\n- Root: عرف (ʿ-r-f)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To know by reflection and discernment of traces/effects\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Maʿrifah is knowledge of God attained through contemplation of His effects, not through direct apprehension of His essence; it is more specific than ʿilm\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to recognize someone; metaphorical: knowledge of God through His signs\n- Root: شهد (sh-h-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To witness, testify, be present\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Bearing witness to the truth of something based on direct presence or evidence\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: ocular presence; metaphorical: intellectual certitude\n- Root: نظر (n-ẓ-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To look, gaze, contemplate\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Contemplative reflection (naẓar) directed toward creation to derive knowledge of the Creator\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: visual perception; metaphorical: intellectual consideration and inference\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — used to restrict the purpose of works to purification of the heart, and to restrict the ultimate end to knowledge of unveiling", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "الكلمة الطيبة التي ضرب الله بها مثلا حيث قال {ضرب الله مثلا كلمة}", "english": "The first: severing the attachments of this world and expelling love of other-than-God from the heart. For the heart is like a vessel: it cannot accommodate vinegar, for example, unless the water is first poured out of it. {God has not made for any man two hearts in his breast} [Al-Ahzab/4]. The perfection of love consists in loving God — mighty and majestic — with one's entire heart. As long as he turns toward other than Him, a corner of his heart is occupied with other than Him. To the extent that he is occupied with other than God, love of God diminishes from it; and to the extent that water remains in the vessel, the vinegar poured into it is diminished.", "book_title": "Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment", "section_title_ar": "Section 1164", "section_title_en": "The Two Pillars of Love for God: Purification of the Heart and Strength of Knowledge", "section_index": 1164, "anchors": "- Root: ح ب ب (ḥ-b-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes steadfastness and permanence; the heart's fixed attachment to its object.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Love (maḥabbah) is the soul's inclination toward what pleases it; its perfection is exclusive devotion, as in the verse {يحبهم ويحبونه} [Al-Ma'idah/54].\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) usage denotes the heart's clinging; metaphorical (majāz) usage extends to acts of obedience and yearning, culminating in 'ishq (intense passionate love) when it overwhelms the lover.\n- Root: خ ل ص (kh-l-ṣ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Purity from admixture; the essence of a thing freed from foreign elements.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Ikhlāṣ is the purification of intention so that no share of creation remains in it; it is the condition for the validity of the shahādah.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: clear, unmixed honey or water; metaphorical: the heart emptied of all rivals so that God alone occupies it.\n- Root: ز ه د (z-h-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Abstention from what is not essential; renunciation of the superfluous.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Asceticism (zuhd) is the heart's detachment from worldly goods, not merely the abandonment of possessions; it is a preparatory station (maqām) for love.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: disinterest in a thing; metaphorical: the soul's indifference to the ephemeral in favor of the eternal.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Restriction and Specification (Bāb al-Ḥaṣr wa al-Taqyīd bi-innamā) — The particle innamā restricts the predicate to the subject alone, as in \"إنما يكتسب العبد حب الله\" — affirming love's acquisition solely through specified means, negating all others.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment'", "badge": "Insight"}], "27": [{"arabic": "وثبتني بالقول الثابت في الحياة الدنيا وفي الآخرة ولا تضلني وإن كنت ظالما سبحانك سبحانك يا علي يا عظيم يا بارىء يا رحيم يا عزيز يا جبار سبحان من سبحت له السموات بأكنافها وسبحان من سبحت له البحار بأمواجها وسبحان من سبحت له الجبال بأصدائها وسبحان من سبحت له الحيتان بلغاتها وسبحان من سبحت له النجوم في السماء بأبراجها وسبحان من سبحت له الأشجار بأصولها وثمارها وسبحان من سبحت له السموات السبع والأرضون السبع ومن فيهن ومن عليهن سبحان من سبح له كل شيء من مخلوقاته تباركت وتعاليت سبحانك سبحانك يا حي", "english": "It is narrated that Yūnus ibn ʿUbayd saw a man in a dream — a man who had been killed as a martyr in the lands of the Byzantines — and he said to him: \"What is the most excellent deed that you witnessed?\" He replied: \"I witnessed the glorifications (tasbīḥāt) of Ibn al-Muʿtamir holding a station with Allah, Mighty and Majestic.\" And they are these: \"Glory be to Allah (subḥāna Allāh), and praise be to Allah (al-ḥamdu li-Allāh), and there is no god but Allah (lā ilāha illā Allāh), and Allah is the Greatest (Allāhu akbar), and there is no might and no power except through Allah, the Most High, the Magnificent — to the number of what He has created, and the number of what He is creating, and the weight of what He has created, and the weight of what He is creating, and the fill of what He has created, and the fill of what He is creating, and the fill of His heavens and the fill of His earth, and the like of that, and multiples of that, and the number of His creation, and the weight of His Throne, and the utmost extent of His mercy, and the ink of His words, and the limit of His good pleasure, until He is pleased, and when He is pleased, [then] the number of what His creation has remembered Him with in all that has passed, and the number of what they will remember Him with in what remains, in every year, and month, and Friday, and day, and night, and hour of the hours, and breath of the breaths, and age of the ages, from eternity to eternity — the eternity of this world and the eternity of the Hereafter — and more than that, whose beginning does not cease and whose end does not come to an end.\"", "book_title": "On Invocations and Supplications", "section_title_ar": "Section 250", "section_title_en": "The Supplications of Ibn al-Muʿtamir (Sulaymān al-Taymī) and His Glorifications, and the Supplication of Ibrāhīm ibn Adham — May Allah Be Well Pleased with Them", "section_index": 250, "anchors": "- Root: سبح (s-b-ḥ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of swift motion (in water or air); extended to the act of prayer and glorification.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"التسبيح: تنزيه الله تعالى. وأصله: المر السريع في عبادة الله تعالى\" — Glorification is the declaration of Allah's transcendence; its root is the swift passage in worship of Allah.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"سبحت الله وسبحت له\" — to declare Him transcendent. Metaphorical: \"والنجوم تسبح في الفلك\" — the stars glide in their orbits, a metaphor for their obedient motion.\n- Root: حمد (ḥ-m-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of praise and commendation for a beneficial act.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Praise (ḥamd) is the acknowledgment of beneficence with reverence; it is due to Allah alone in His absolute perfection.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"أحمد الله تعالى بجميع محامده\" — to praise Allah with all His praiseworthy attributes. Metaphorical: \"أحمدت الأرض: رضيت سكناها\" — to find a thing agreeable, extending praise to non-verbal contexts.\n- Root: لهم (l-h-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of swift swallowing or devouring.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الإلهام: إلقاء الشيء في الروع\" — Inspiration is the casting of a thing into the heart/mind (al-rawʿ), specifically from Allah or the higher assembly.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"ألهمه الله الخير: ألقاه في روعه\" — Allah inspired him with good. Metaphorical: \"جواد يلتهم الأرض\" — a swift horse devours the ground, extending to rapid comprehension.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The permissibility of interposing elements between a preposition and its object when the clause remains grammatically sound, as seen in the extended supplicatory sentences.", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'On Invocations and Supplications'", "badge": "Du'a"}, {"arabic": "فعزمت على تركه فنوديت من جانب البيت يثبت الله الذين آمنوا بالقول الثابت في الحياة الدنيا وفي الآخرة وقال الشعبي ما أدري أيهما أبعد غور في النار الكذاب أو البخيل وقال ابن السماك ما أراني أوجر على ترك الكذب لأني إنما أدعه أنفه وقيل الخالدين صبيح أيسمى الرجل كاذبا بكذبة واحدة قال نعم وقال مالك بن دينار قرأت في بعض الكتب ما من خطيب إلا وتعرض خطبته على عمله فإن كان صادقا صدق وإن كان كاذبا قرضت شفتاه بمقاريض من نار كلما قرضتا نبتتا وقال مالك بن دينار الصدق والكذب يعتركان في القلب حتى يخرج أحدهما صاحبه وك", "english": "Among them is the hadith: \"And three whom God hates: the trader or seller who swears much.\" Ahmad narrated it, and the wording is his. In its chain is Ibn al-Aḥmaṣ, whose condition is unknown. Both Ahmad and al-Nasā'ī narrated it with another wording through a sound chain. And al-Nasā'ī has, from the hadith of Abū Hurayrah: \"Four whom God abhors: the seller who swears much\" — the hadith — and its chain is sound.", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 676", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 676: On Lying (al-Kadhib) — Hadith Reports and Traditions (Āthār)", "section_index": 676, "anchors": "- Root: كذب (Kadhaba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to report a falsehood, to utter what is contrary to reality; al-Kadhdhābah: a variegated cloth that deceives the eye into thinking it is part of the ceiling — indicating the deceptive semblance of truth.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Kadhib applies to both speech and action. God says: {«Only they forge falsehood who do not believe in God's signs»} (Q 16:105) — the hypocrites' speech was true, but their inward conviction was false, so God called them liars (kādhibūn) regarding their belief.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqīqah): \"He is kadhib, kadhūb, kadhāb, kaydhabān\" — one who lies habitually. Metaphorical (Majāz): \"He carried [the burden] then lied\" — i.e., he cowardly retreated, making his charge a false one; \"the she-camel lied\" — her pregnancy proved false.\n- Root: حدث (Ḥadatha)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to originate, to bring into being after non-existence; al-ḥadīth: speech that is new/recently originated.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Al-Ḥudūth: a thing's coming to be after it was not — whether accident or substance. Its creation (iḥdāth) belongs solely to God. Al-muḥdath: what is brought into existence after non-existence.\" God says: {«There comes not to them a fresh reminder (dhikr muḥdath) from their Lord»} (Q 21:2).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"He spoke a ḥadīth; fresh in age.\" Metaphorical: \"They became aḥādīth (topics of talk)\"; ʿUmar was called muḥaddath — one whose intuition is divinely inspired, as if truth were spoken into him.\n- Root: ويل (Wayl)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: An expression of intense woe, destruction, and chastisement; a word of threat and condemnation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Wayl denotes severe affliction and ruin; it is a declaration of impending punishment.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"Woe to him\" — a cry of ruin. Used in threats: \"Waylun lahu\" — destruction is his due.\n- Root: شنأ (Shana'a)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Intense hatred and abhorrence.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-Shan'ān: intense hatred that drives one to shun the object.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"He hated him with shan'an\" — vehement hatred.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Bāb al-Ḥaṣr wa al-Taqyīd bi-innamā (Restriction and Focused Predication) — The particle innamā restricts the predicate to the subject alone, negating it for all else. Applied in the verse: {«Only they forge falsehood who do not believe»} — restricting fabrication to the disbelievers, affirming that the believer does not lie.", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Ethics"}], "31": [{"arabic": "وسلم قال من كانت لأخيه عنده مظلمة في عرض أو مال فليستحللها منه من قبل أن يأتي يوم ليس هناك دينار ولا درهم إنما يؤخذ من حسناته فإن لم يكن له حسنات أخذ من سيئات صاحبه فزيدت على سيئاته (1) وقالت عائشة رضي الله عنها لامرأة قالت لأخرى أنها طويلة الذيل قد اغتبتيها فاستحليها فإذن لا بد من الاستحلال إن قدر عليه فإن كان غائبا أو ميتا فينبغي أن يكثر له الاستغفار والدعاء ويكثر من الحسنات فإن قلت فالتحليل هل يجب فأقول لا لأنه تبرع والتبرع فضل وليس بواجب ولكنه مستحسن وسبيل المعتذر أن يبالغ في الثناء عليه والتودد إليه", "english": "- Root: غيب (gh-y-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To be absent, hidden, concealed from sight; ghayb = that which is unseen/absent from perception\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الغيب: مصدر غابت الشمس وغيرها: إذا استترت عن العين — used of everything absent from the senses and from human knowledge; applied to things as ghayb/ghā'ib relative to people, not relative to God, for nothing is absent from Him\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqiqah: \"تكلم بذلك عن ظهر الغيب\" (speaking of the unseen); Majaz: \"أتونا في غابة\" (metaphorical extension to dense/thick things)\n- Root: حسن (ḥ-s-n)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Beauty, goodness, excellence; to be fair, comely, good\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحسن: عبارة عن كل مبهج مرغوب فيه — three kinds: good by reason, good by desire, good by sense; ḥasanah = everything that delights one, whether in self, body, or circumstances\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqiqah: \"أنظر إلى محاسن وجهه\" (physical beauty); Majaz: \"فلان لا يحسن شيئًا\" (to do well/excel — transferred to action)\n- Root: حرم (ḥ-r-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To forbid, prohibit, make unlawful; ḥarām = that which is forbidden\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحرام: الممنوع منه إما بتسخير إلهي وإما بشري — prohibition by divine decree, by human law, by coercive prevention, by reason, or by revelation\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqiqah: \"هتك رحمته\" (violating sanctity); Majaz: \"جلد محرم: لم يدبغ\" (raw hide — metaphorical extension to \"untreated/unlawful\")\n- Root: ظلم (ẓ-l-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Wrongdoing, injustice, oppression; to place something in other than its proper place\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الظلمة: عدم النور — darkness is absence of light; used metaphorically for ignorance, polytheism, and sin, just as light is used for their opposites\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqiqah: \"فلان يظلم فيظلم\" (to wrong and be wronged); Majaz: \"أرض مظلومة\" (land never dug — metaphorical: \"wronged\" land)\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — \"إنما يؤخذ من حسناته\" restricts the taking exclusively to good deeds, excluding any monetary compensation on that Day.", "book_title": "Condemnation of Anger and Hatred", "section_title_ar": "Section 690", "section_title_en": "Section 690", "section_index": 690, "anchors": "*(Anchors established)*", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Condemnation of Anger and Hatred'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "34": [{"arabic": "أستغفرك لظلمي وكفري فقيل له هذا الظلم فما بال الكفر فتلا قوله عز وجل إن الإنسان لظلوم كفار وقيل ليوسف ابن أسباط إذا قرأت القرآن بماذا تدعو فقال بماذا أدعو أستغفر الله عز وجل من تقصيري سبعين مرة فإذا رأى نفسه بصورة التقصير في القراءة كان رؤيته سبب قربه فإن من شهد البعد في القرب لطف به في الخوف حتى يسوقه الخوف إلى درجة أخرى في القرب وراءها ومن شهد القرب في البعد مكربه بالأمن الذي يفضيه إلى درجة أخرى في البعد أسفل مما هو فيه ومهما كان مشاهدا نفسه بعين الرضا صار محجوبا بنفسه فإذا جاوز حد الالتفات إلى نفسه ولم يشاهد إلا الله تعالى في", "english": "For this reason, Yūsuf ibn Asbāṭ said: \"I intend to recite the Qur'an, but when I recall what is in it, I fear abhorrence (al-maqt), so I turn instead to glorification (al-tasbīḥ) and seeking forgiveness (al-istighfār).\"", "book_title": "Etiquette of Quran Recitation", "section_title_ar": "Section 231", "section_title_en": "** The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 231: On the Inward Conditions of Qur'anic Recitation (Continued)", "section_index": 231, "anchors": "**\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the stirring of fear/apprehension within\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف. يقال: وجل يوجل وجلا، فهو وجل. قال تعالى: {إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم}\" — the theological state of the heart's trembling upon remembrance of God\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال، وقد وجل وجلا\" — the physical/emotional state of fear. Metaphorical (Majaz): The heart's trembling as the seat of spiritual perception\n- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — turning and transforming\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه، كقلب الثوب، وقلب الإنسان\" — the heart as that which is constantly turning/transforming between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" — physical turning. Metaphorical: \"قلب المعلم الصبيان: صرفهم إلى بيوتهم\" — the heart's turning toward or away from spiritual realities\n- Root: نفس (n-f-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self, soul, spirit — that which is breathed\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النفس: الروح\" — the soul as the animating principle; also the self as the locus of blameworthy attributes\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"شيء نفيس ومنفس\" — preciousness. Metaphorical: \"دفق نفسه أي دمه\" — the self as that which must be transcended in spiritual realization\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the precise delimitation of spiritual states (أحوال) and their hierarchical gradation (درجات)\n\n---\n\n#", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Etiquette of Quran Recitation'", "badge": "Insight"}], "36": [{"arabic": "إبراهيم عليه السلام {رب إنهن أضللن كثيرا من الناس فمن تبعني فإنه مني ومن عصاني فإنك غفور رحيم} وقول عيسى عليه السلام {إن تعذبهم فإنهم عبادك} ثم رفع يديه وقال أمتي أمتي ثم بكى فقال الله عز وجل يا جبريل اذهب إلى محمد فسله ما يبكيك فأتاه جبريل فسأله فأخبره والله أعلم به فقال يا جبريل اذهب إلى محمد فقل له إنا سنرضيك في أمتك ولا نسوءك (1) وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم أعطيت خمسا لم يعطهن أحد قبل نصرت بالرعب مسيرة شهر وأحلت لي الغنائم ولم تحل لأحد قبلي وجعلت لي الأرض مسجدا وترابها طهورا فأيما رجل من أمتي أدركته الصلاة فليصل وأعطيت الشفاعة وكل نبي بعث", "english": "The evidences for intercession in the Qurʾān and the reports (akhbār) are numerous. God—exalted be He—said: {«وَلَسَوْفَ يُعْطِيكَ رَبُّكَ فَتَرْضَىٰ»} [And verily thy Lord shall give thee, and thou shalt be well-pleased] (93:5). ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ narrated that the Messenger of God—peace and blessings be upon him—recited the saying of Abraham—peace be upon him—{«رَبِّ إِنَّهُنَّ أَضْلَلْنَ كَثِيرًا مِّنَ النَّاسِ ۖ فَمَن تَبِعَنِي فَإِنَّهُ مِنِّي ۖ وَمَنْ عَصَانِي فَإِنَّكَ غَفُورٌ رَّحِيمٌ»} [My Lord, verily they have led astray many of mankind; so whoso follows me, verily he is of me; and whoso disobeys me, verily Thou art Forgiving, Merciful] (14:36), and the saying of Jesus—peace be upon him—{«إِن تُعَذِّبْهُمْ فَإِنَّهُمْ عِبَادُكَ»} [If Thou chastise them, verily they are Thy servants] (5:118). Then he raised his hands and said: \"My community, my community!\" Then he wept. So God—mighty and majestic—said: \"O Gabriel, go to Muhammad and ask him what makes him weep.\" Gabriel came to him and asked him, and he informed him—and God knows best concerning him. Then He said: \"O Gabriel, go to Muhammad and say to him: 'Verily We shall please thee concerning thy community, and shall not cause thee grief.'\"", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1346", "section_title_en": "On the Reality of Intercession (Shafāʿah) and the Means of Attaining It", "section_index": 1346, "anchors": "- Root: شفع (Sh-F-ʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes pairing/joining (al-ḍamm), whence shafāʿah = the intercessor joining his own plea to the petitioner's, making it two (shafʿ) rather than one (watr).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance — intercession is a divinely granted honor (jāh) bestowed upon prophets and saints, not an independent power; its efficacy is wholly contingent upon divine will and pleasure.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) — \"شفعت الشيء\" (I made it even/pair). Metaphorical (majāz) — the intercessor \"pairs\" his rank with the sinner's need, elevating the latter through the former's proximity to God.\n- Root: خبأ (Kh-B-ʾ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To conceal, hide, store away secretly (khabaʾa). Implies deliberate concealment of something precious or dangerous within a locus.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance — God's \"hiding\" (takhbiʾah) of His friendship (wilāyah), wrath (ghaḍab), and pleasure (riḍā) within creatures and acts respectively teaches epistemic humility: no outward judgment can access the hidden decree.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — physical concealment. Metaphorical — the inscrutability of divine judgment (ḥukm) regarding ultimate states (ʿawāqib), rendering contempt for any person or act a potential act of defiance against God's hidden will.\n- Root: عله (not applicable here; avoided per instruction)\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — Governing the periodic suspension of meaning across lengthy narrative sequences (e.g., the extended ḥadīth of intercession), where the syntactic resolution (jawāb) is deferred to heighten rhetorical impact, yet remains grammatically anchored.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "38": [{"arabic": "لا إله إلا الله الملك الحق المبين (6) الثامنة قوله بسم الله الذي لا يضر مع اسمه شيء في الأرض ولا في السماء وهو السميع العليم (7) التاسعة اللهم صل على محمد عبدك ونبيك ورسولك النبي الأمي وعلى آله وصحبه وسلم (8) العاشرة قوله أعوذ بالله السميع العليم من الشيطان الرجيم رب أعوذ بك من همزات الشياطين وأعوذ بك رب أن يحضرون (9) فهذه العشر كلمات إذا كرر كل واحدة عشر مرات حصل له مائة مرة فهو أفضل من أن يكرر ذكرا واحدا مائة مرة لأن لكل واحدة من هؤلاء الكلمات فضلا على حياله وللقلب بكل واحد نوع تنبه وتلذذ وللنفس في الانتقال من كلمة إلى كلمة نوع استراحة وأمن", "english": "Now, the first watch (al-wird al-awwal) is that which extends from the dawn prayer (ṣalāt al-ṣubḥ) until sunrise (ṭulūʿ al-shams). When the servant has completed his obligatory prayer, he should remain seated in his place of prayer, occupying himself with the remembrance of God most High, until the sun has risen. For it has been reported that the Messenger of God, peace and blessings be upon him, said: \"That I should sit in my assembly, remembering God most High therein, from the morning prayer until sunrise, is more beloved to me than freeing four slaves.\" {«لأن أقعد في مجلسي أذكر الله تعالى فيه من صلاة الغداة إلى طلوع الشمس أحب إلي من أن أعتق أربع رقاب»} And it is related that he, peace and blessings be upon him, when he had prayed the morning prayer, would sit in his place of prayer until the sun rose; and in some versions [it is added] that he would pray two rakʿahs—that is, after sunrise. Concerning the virtue of this [practice], an incalculable number of reports have come down. Al-Ḥasan related that the Messenger of God, peace and blessings be upon him, used to mention, among what he recounted of his Lord's mercy, that He said: \"O son of Adam, remember Me for an hour after the dawn prayer and for an hour after the afternoon prayer, and I shall suffice you for what lies between them.\" {«يا ابن آدم اذكرني بعد صلاة الفجر ساعة وبعد صلاة العصر ساعة أكفك ما بينهما»}", "book_title": "Arrangement of Litanies", "section_title_ar": "Section 260", "section_title_en": "The Daily Liturgy of the Devout Servant: The First Watch (After Dawn Prayer until Sunrise)", "section_index": 260, "anchors": "- Root: ذكر (dhakara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Remembrance, invocation, mention; the opposite of forgetfulness (nisyān).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Remembrance of God (dhikr) is the heart's presence with the Divine, ranging from the tongue's utterance to the heart's contemplation; it is the noblest act when accompanied by presence of heart.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): verbal mention. Metaphorical (majāz): the heart's steadfastness in knowledge of God, which is the reality of dhikr.\n- Root: صلا (ṣallā)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Prayer (ṣalāh); root meaning of supplication and blessing; also \"to kindle a fire\" (as in ṣalā al-nār).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Ṣalāh is the servant's standing before God in humility and devotion, comprising praise, recitation, bowing, and prostration; its perfection lies in the heart's presence.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the ritual prayer with its pillars. Metaphorical: supplication (duʿā') and blessing (taṣliyah) upon the Prophet.\n- Root: ورد (warada)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To arrive at water; a portion or share of something that comes regularly.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The daily portion (wird) of devotion—a set liturgy of prayers, recitations, and remembrances that the servant regularly performs at appointed times.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: arriving at a watering place. Metaphorical: the soul's regular approach to the fountain of divine grace through prescribed acts of worship.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Restriction and Specification (al-ḥaṣr wa al-taqyīd) — governing the precise enumeration of the four functions (adʿiyah, adhkār, qirā'ah, fikr) after the dawn prayer, each specified by its own governing verb and object.", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'Arrangement of Litanies'", "badge": "Du'a"}, {"arabic": "رادك فقد حضر سفر بعيد {وما يخفى على الله من شيء في الأرض ولا في السماء} والسلام فهذه الأخبار والآثار تدل على ما في مخالطة السلاطين من الفتن وأنواع الفساد ولكن نفصل ذلك تفضيلا فقيها تميز فيه المحظور عن المكروه والمباح فنقول الداخل على السلطان متعرض لأن يعصي الله تعالى إما بفعله أو بسكوته وإما بقوله وإما باعتقاده فلا ينفك عن أحد هذه الأمور أما الفعل فالدخول عليهم في غالب الأحوال يكون إلى دور مغصوبة وتخطيها والدخول فيها بغير إذن الملاك حرام ولا يغرنك قول القائل إن ذلك مما يتسامح به الناس كتمرة أو فتات خبز ذلك صحيح", "english": "I say: Know — may Allah guide you — that the scholar who draws near to a tyrannical ruler has betrayed his Book and his knowledge of the Sunnah of his Prophet Muhammad, peace and blessings be upon him. It is not thus that Allah took the covenant upon the scholars. Allah the Exalted said: {«لَتُبَيِّنُنَّهُ لِلنَّاسِ وَلَا تَكْتُمُونَهُ»} — \"You shall surely make it clear to the people and not conceal it\" (Āl ʿImrān 3:187).", "book_title": "The Lawful and Prohibited", "section_title_ar": "Section 390", "section_title_en": "Section 390 — The Severe Condemnation of Associating with Tyrannical Rulers and the Juridical Distinction of Its Prohibitions", "section_index": 390, "anchors": "- Root: ظلم (ẓ-l-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is darkness (ẓulmah) — the absence of light; to place something in other than its proper position.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Ẓulm is expressed as darkness (ẓulumāt) contrasted with light (nūr); it signifies ignorance, polytheism, and transgression. The wrongdoer (ẓālim) veils truth and acts in spiritual darkness.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): \"الظلم ظلمات يوم القيامة\" — injustice is darknesses on the Day of Resurrection; justice is light. Metaphorical (majāz): \"أرض مظلومة\" — land never dug, untouched; the tyrant's soul is one upon which no ray of justice has fallen.\n- Root: ملك (m-l-k)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To possess, to own, to have dominion; al-milk is that which is under one's control.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-Mulk is sovereignty over the affairs of rational beings through command and prohibition. True ownership (milk) entails the right of exclusive disposal.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: possession of property and dominion. Metaphorical: \"ملك نفسه عند الغضب\" — mastery over oneself in anger; the tyrant's seizure of property (ghaṣb) is an illegitimate extension of ownership.\n- Root: جمع (j-m-')\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To gather, to bring together parts into a whole.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-Jam' is the bringing together of things by proximity; collective action compounds individual acts into a unified deed.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: assembling a crowd. Metaphorical: \"أمر بني فلان بجمع\" — a concealed matter, kept secret; the collective nature of transgression — each individual step contributing to a whole that constitutes usurpation.\n- Root: وجب (w-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To be incumbent, to be due, to be necessary; to fall down dead.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-Wujūb is fixedness and establishment; that which is obligatory (wājib) — its neglect incurs blame, whether by reason or by sacred law.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"وجب البيع\" — the sale became binding. Metaphorical: \"ضربه فوجب\" — struck him so he fell dead; the obligatory nature of retaliation (qiṣāṣ) when collective acts cause destruction.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Restriction and Focused Predication (باب الحصر والتقييد) — The conditional clauses (fa-in kāna... fa-in kāna...) structure a graduated juridical analysis, each hypothesis (taqdīr) isolating a distinct legal category (ḥarām, makrūh, mubāḥ) with precise syntactic partitioning.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'The Lawful and Prohibited'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "42": [{"arabic": "ويوم لا تجزي نفس عن نفس شيئا ويوم تشخص فيه الأبصار ويوم لا يغنى مولى عن مولى شيئا ويوم لا تملك نفس لنفس شيئا ويوم يدعون إلى نار جهنم دعا ويوم يسحبون فى النار على وجوههم ويوم تقلب وجوههم فى النار ويوم لا يجزى والد عن ولده ويوم يفر المرء من أخيه وأمه وأبيه ويوم لا ينطقون ولا يؤذن لهم فيعتذرون يوم لا مرد له من الله يوم هم بارزون ويوم هم على النار يفتنون يوم لا ينفع مال ولا بنون يوم لا ينفع الظالمين معذرتهم ولهم اللعنة ولهم سوء الدار يوم ترد فيه المعاذير وتبلى السرائر وتظهر الضمائر وتكشف الأستار يوم تخشع فيه الأبصار وتسكن الأصوات ويقل فيه الالتفات وتبرز الخفيات وتظهر الخطيئات يوم يساق العباد ومعهم الأشهاد ويشيب الصغير ويسكر الكبير فيومئذ وضعت الموازين ونشرت الدواوين وبرزت الجحيم وأغلى الحميم وزفرت النار ويئس الكفار وسعرت النيران وتغيرت الألوان وخرس اللسان ونطقت جوارح الإنسان فيا أيها الإنسان ما غرك بربك الكريم حيث أغلقت الأبواب وأرخيت الستور واستترت عن الخلائق فقارفت الفجور فماذا تفعل وقد شهدت عليك جوارحك فالويل كل الويل لنا معشر الغافلين يرسل الله لنا سيد المرسلين وينزل عليه الكتاب المبين ويخبرنا بهذه الصفات من نعوت يوم الدين ثم يعرفنا غفلتنا ويقول اقترب الناس حسابهم وهم في غفلة معرضون ما يأتيهم من ذكر من ربهم محدث إلا استمعوه وهم يلعبون لاهية قلوبهم ثم يعرفنا قرب القيامة فيقول اقتربت الساعة وانشق القمر إنهم يرونه بعيدا ونراه قريبا وما يدريك لعل الساعة تكون قريبا ثم يكون أحسن أحوالنا أن نتخذ دراسة هذا القرآن عملا فلا نتدبر معانيه ولا ننظر في كثرة أوصاف هذا اليوم وأساميه ولا نستعد للتخلص من دواهيه فنعوذ بالله من هذه الغفلة إن لم يداركنا الله بواسع رحمته", "english": "So prepare yourself, O wretched one, for this Day of immense magnitude, of prolonged duration, of overwhelming dominion, whose appointed time is near — a Day when you shall see the heaven split asunder, and the stars scattered in its terror, and the shining planets extinguished, and the sun folded up, and the mountains set in motion, and the pregnant camels neglected, and the wild beasts gathered together, and the seas set ablaze, and the souls paired with their bodies, and the Hellfire ignited, and Paradise brought near, and the mountains pulverized, and the earth stretched out — a Day when you shall see the earth shaken with its quaking, and the earth casts forth its burdens. On that Day shall people issue forth in scattered groups to be shown their deeds — a Day when the earth and the mountains are lifted and crushed with a single crushing. Then on that Day the Imminent Event shall come to pass, and the heaven shall split open, for on that Day it shall be frail, and the angels shall be at its edges, and eight shall bear the Throne of your Lord above them on that Day. On that Day you shall be presented — no secret of yours shall remain hidden. A Day when the mountains shall move and you shall see the earth laid bare — a Day when the earth shall be shaken with a violent shaking, and the mountains crumbled to dust, becoming scattered particles — a Day when people shall be like scattered moths, and the mountains like carded wool — a Day when every nursing mother shall forget what she nursed, and every pregnant female shall cast down her burden, and you shall see people drunk, yet they are not drunk, but the chastisement of God is severe — a Day when the earth shall be changed to other than this earth, and the heavens, and they shall appear before God, the One, the All-Compelling — a Day when the mountains shall be blown away as dust, leaving a level, bare plain, wherein you see no crookedness nor curve — a Day when you see the mountains and suppose them solid, yet they pass by like the passing of clouds — a Day when the heaven shall split open and become rosy like molten oil. Then on that Day no human nor jinn shall be asked about his sin. A Day when the disobedient shall be prevented from speech, and shall not be asked about their crimes, but shall be seized by their forelocks and their feet — a Day when every soul shall find what it did of good brought forth present, and what it did of evil, wishing that between it and that evil there were a far distance — a Day when every soul shall know what it has sent ahead, and shall witness what it has forwarded and what it has left behind — a Day when tongues shall be struck mute and the bodily members shall speak — a Day whose mention turned the hair of the Master of the Messengers white, when al-Ṣiddīq (may God be pleased with him) said to him: \"I see that you have turned white-haired, O Messenger of God.\" He said: \"Sūrat Hūd and its sisters have made me white-haired\" — namely, al-Wāqiʿah, al-Mursalāt, ʿAmma Yatasāʾalūn, and Idhā al-Shamsu Kuwwirat.", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1339", "section_title_en": "The Names and Attributes of the Day of Resurrection — An Exhortation to the Negligent", "section_index": 1339, "anchors": "- Root: يوم (yawm)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"اليوم: وقت طلوع الشمس إلى غروبها، وقد يعبر به عن مدة من الزمان أي مدة كانت\" — the day as a temporal span, extended metaphorically to any epoch or era.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"اليوم يعبر به عن وقت طلوع الشمس إلى غروبها. وقد يعبر به عن مدة من الزمان أي مدة كانت\" — theological usage denotes the appointed time of reckoning, magnified by divine attribution (أيام الله).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): a measured solar period. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"ذكر في أيام العرب كذا أي في وقائعها\" — days as epochal events; thus \"the Day\" is the supreme epochal event of divine justice.\n- Root: نفس (nafs)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: النفس: الروح والذات — the self, soul, or very essence.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النفس: الروح... وقوله: ويحذركم الله نفسه فنفسه: ذاته\" — nafs as the innermost reality of a being; in the eschatological context, souls are \"paired\" (زوجت) with bodies for judgment.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the soul as life-principle. Metaphorical: \"دفق نفسه أي دمه\" — nafs as the total self, accountable in its entirety.\n- Root: ارض (arḍ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: الأرض: الجرم المقابل للسماء — the earth as the corporeal substratum beneath the heavens.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الأرض: الجرم المقابل للسماء... ويعبر بها عن أسفل الشيء\" — the earth as the lowest register of creation, subject to total transformation on that Day.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the physical earth. Metaphorical: \"تأرض فلان: لزم الأرض\" — to cling low; on that Day the earth itself is leveled, flattened, and replaced.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The repeated \"يوم\" clauses function as temporal restrictors (ẓurūf) binding each predicate to that single, all-encompassing moment; the accumulation of names (أسامي) serves not as mere repetition but as focused predication upon the multifarious realities of a single event.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "وقال تعالى ولا تحسبن الله غافلا عما يعمل الظالمون إنما يؤخرهم ليوم تشخص فيه الأبصار إلى غير ذلك مما ورد في كتاب الله تعالى وسنة رسوله فمن آمن به تخلص من هذا الغرور فإن منشأ هذا الغرور الجهل بالله وبصفاته فإن من عرفه لا يأمن مكره ولا يغتر بأمثال هذه الخيالات الفاسدة وينظر إلى فرعون وهامان وقارون وإلى ملوك الأرض وما جرى لهم كيف أحسن الله إليهم ابتداء ثم دمرهم تدميرا فقال تعالى هل تحس منهم من أحد الآية وقد حذر الله تعالى من مكره واستدراجه فقال فلا يأمن مكر الله إلا القوم الخاسرون وقال تعالى ومكروا مكرا ومكرنا مكرا وهم لا يشعرون وقال", "english": "As for knowing it by way of authoritative transmission and affirmation (taṣdīq): it is that one believes in the Book of God — exalted is He — and affirms His Messenger. For God — exalted is He — has said: {«أيحسبون أن ما نمدهم به من مال وبنين * نسارع لهم في الخيرات بل لا يشعرون»} — \"Do they reckon that what We extend to them of wealth and sons — [that] We hasten for them good things? Nay, but they do not perceive\" (Q. 23:55-56). And He said — exalted is He: {«سنستدرجهم من حيث لا يعلمون»} — \"We shall draw them on by degrees from whence they do not know\" (Q. 7:182). And He said — exalted is He: {«فتحنا عليهم أبواب كل شيء حتى إذا فرحوا بما أوتوا أخذناهم بغتة فإذا هم مبلسون»} — \"We opened upon them the gates of everything, until when they rejoiced in what they were given, We seized them suddenly, and behold, they were in despair\" (Q. 6:44). And in the interpretation of His saying — exalted is He: {«سنستدرجهم من حيث لا يعلمون»} — it is said: \"Whenever they innovated a sin, We innovated for them a blessing, to increase their self-deception.\" And He said — exalted is He: {«إنما نملي لهم ليزدادوا إثما»} — \"We only grant them respite that they may increase in sin\" (Q. 3:178). And He said — exalted is He: {«ولا تحسبن الله غافلا عما يعمل الظالمون إنما يؤخرهم ليوم تشخص فيه الأبصار»} — \"And do not reckon God heedless of what the wrongdoers do; He only delays them to a Day when eyes will stare fixedly\" (Q. 14:42). And [there is] more of that kind which has come in the Book of God — exalted is He — and in the Sunnah of His Messenger. So whoever believes in it is delivered from this self-deception. For the origin of this self-deception is ignorance of God and of His attributes; for whoever knows Him does not feel secure from His stratagem (makr) and is not deceived by such corrupt fancies.", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 888", "section_title_en": "Section 888 — On the Remedy for Self-Deception (Ghurūr): Recognizing the Signs of Divine Honor (Karāmah) and Humiliation (Hawān)", "section_index": 888, "anchors": "- Root: غرر (gh-r-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of an outward trace or mark; heedlessness in wakefulness; the blaze on a horse's forehead.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Ghurūr\" is delusion stemming from heedlessness — the soul's being overtaken while imagining security, as in {«ما غرك بربك الكريم»} (Q. 82:6). It is the state of one who is deceived by apparent favors while blind to their underlying test.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): heedlessness and exposure to predation, like a gazelle deceived by moonlight. Metaphorical (Majaz): being \"deceived by God\" (ightirār billāh) — trusting in His mercy while persisting in disobedience.\n- Root: لهم (l-h-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Swallowing whole; the gullet.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Ilhām\" — God's casting a meaning into the heart (al-raw'), distinct from demonic whispering; the channel of inner vision for the knowers (ʿārifūn).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to swallow. Metaphorical: the heart's receptive \"swallowing\" of divine inspiration.\n- Root: حدث (ḥ-d-th)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Origination after non-existence; newness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Ḥudūth\" — a thing's coming to be after it was not; \"muḥdith\" — the originator, exclusively God for substances (jawāhir). \"Iḥdāth\" — bringing about a novel state or act.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: temporal newness. Metaphorical: \"muḥaddath\" — one inspired with correct discernment, as if newly spoken-to.\n- Root: نعم (n-ʿ-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Softness, ease, well-being; a state of comfort.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Niʿmah\" — the good state one inhabits; \"naʿīm\" — the act of living pleasantly. Blessings are both a test and a sign, per {«وإن تعدوا نعمة الله لا تحصوها»} (Q. 16:18).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: bounty and comfort. Metaphorical: \"khafat naʿāmatuhum\" — their prosperity vanished; blessings may signal either favor or gradual ruin (istidrāj).\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction and Focused Predication with \"innamā\")\n  * In {«إنما نملي لهم ليزدادوا إثما»} (Q. 3:178) and {«إنما يؤخرهم ليوم تشخص فيه الأبصار»} (Q. 14:42), \"innamā\" restricts the purpose of divine respite exclusively to increase of sin and deferred reckoning — negating any reading of it as benevolent indulgence.", "content_type": "dhikr", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on constant remembrance of God and contemplative invocations, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Dhikr"}], "44": [{"arabic": "يقولون {ربنا أخرنا إلى أجل قريب نجب دعوتك ونتبع الرسل} فيجيبهم الله تعالى أو لم تكونوا أقسمتم من قبل ما لكم من زوال فيقولون ربنا أخرجنا نعمل صالحا غير الذي كنا نعمل به فيجيبهم الله تعالى أو لم نعمركم ما يتذكر فيه من تذكر وجاءكم النذير فذوقوا فما للظالمين من نصير ثم يقولون {ربنا غلبت علينا شقوتنا وكنا قوما ضالين ربنا أخرجنا منها فإن عدنا فإنا ظالمون} فيجيبهم الله تعالى {اخسؤوا فيها ولا تكلمون} فلا يتكلمون بعدها أبدا وذلك غاية شدة العذاب قال مالك بن أنس رضي الله عنه قال زيد بن أسلم في قوله تع", "english": "At that point they despair of every good, and at that point they take to sighing, regret, and wailing. Abu Umamah said: The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said concerning His words, \"And he is given to drink of festering water, which he sips but can hardly swallow\" {«وَيُسْقَىٰ مِن مَّاءٍ صَدِيدٍ يَتَجَرَّعُهُ وَلَا يَكَادُ يُسِيغُهُ»}: \"It is brought near to him, and he loathes it. When it is drawn close to him, it scorches his face, and the skin of his scalp falls off. When he drinks it, it severs his intestines until it exits from his anus.\" God Almighty says: {«وَسُقُوا مَاءً حَمِيمًا فَقَطَّعَ أَمْعَاءَهُمْ»} \"And they are given to drink boiling water, and it severs their intestines.\" And He says: {«وَإِن يَسْتَغِيثُوا يُغَاثُوا بِمَاءٍ كَالْمُهْلِ يَشْوِي الْوُجُوهَ»} \"And if they cry for relief, they are relieved with water like molten brass, which scalds the faces.\" So this is their food and drink in their hunger and thirst.", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1350", "section_title_en": "The Torments of the Fire: Serpents, Scorpions, and the Anguish of the Damned", "section_index": 1350, "anchors": "- Root: حس (ḥ-s-s) — [AVOIDED per instruction]\n- Root: نار (n-r) — Fire\n  * Lisan al-Arab: \"النار: معروفة، وهي نار جهنم، ذات لفح وحرّ.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النار: قد تطلق على نار الدنيا ونار الآخرة، وهي التي أعدها الله للكافرين، وفيها من أصناف العذاب ما لا يعلمه إلا الله.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"نار: حقيقة في النار المحرقة، ومجاز في نار الحرب والحرب.\"\n- Root: حي (ḥ-y) — Serpent\n  * Lisan al-Arab: \"الحية: من الهوام، وتجمع على حيات، وأعظمها ما كان كأعناق البخت.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحية: دابة مؤذية، وفي الآخرة تكون أعظم وأشد إيلامًا.\"\n- Root: عقر (ʿ-q-r) — Scorpion\n  * Lisan al-Arab: \"العقرب: دويبة مؤذية تلدغ، وفي النار تكون كالبغال.\"\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Used to specify that serpents and scorpions are only empowered over those who were dominated by miserliness and evil character in the world.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "تعالى أو لم تكونوا أقسمتم من قبل ما لكم من زوال فيقولون ربنا أخرجنا نعمل صالحا غير الذي كنا نعمل به فيجيبهم الله تعالى أو لم نعمركم ما يتذكر فيه من تذكر وجاءكم النذير فذوقوا فما للظالمين من نصير ثم يقولون {ربنا غلبت علينا شقوتنا وكنا قوما ضالين ربنا أخرجنا منها فإن عدنا فإنا ظالمون} فيجيبهم الله تعالى {اخسؤوا فيها ولا تكلمون} فلا يتكلمون بعدها أبدا وذلك غاية شدة العذاب قال مالك بن أنس رضي الله عنه قال زيد بن أسلم في قوله تعالى {سواء علينا أجزعنا أم صبرنا ما لنا من محيص} قال صبروا مائ", "english": "At that point they despair of every good, and at that point they take to sighing, regret, and wailing. Abu Umamah said: The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said concerning His words, \"And he is given to drink of festering water, which he sips but can hardly swallow\" {«وَيُسْقَىٰ مِن مَّاءٍ صَدِيدٍ يَتَجَرَّعُهُ وَلَا يَكَادُ يُسِيغُهُ»}: \"It is brought near to him, and he loathes it. When it is drawn close to him, it scorches his face, and the skin of his scalp falls off. When he drinks it, it severs his intestines until it exits from his anus.\" God Almighty says: {«وَسُقُوا مَاءً حَمِيمًا فَقَطَّعَ أَمْعَاءَهُمْ»} \"And they are given to drink boiling water, and it severs their intestines.\" And He says: {«وَإِن يَسْتَغِيثُوا يُغَاثُوا بِمَاءٍ كَالْمُهْلِ يَشْوِي الْوُجُوهَ»} \"And if they cry for relief, they are relieved with water like molten brass, which scalds the faces.\" So this is their food and drink in their hunger and thirst.", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1350", "section_title_en": "The Torments of the Fire: Serpents, Scorpions, and the Anguish of the Damned", "section_index": 1350, "anchors": "- Root: حس (ḥ-s-s) — [AVOIDED per instruction]\n- Root: نار (n-r) — Fire\n  * Lisan al-Arab: \"النار: معروفة، وهي نار جهنم، ذات لفح وحرّ.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النار: قد تطلق على نار الدنيا ونار الآخرة، وهي التي أعدها الله للكافرين، وفيها من أصناف العذاب ما لا يعلمه إلا الله.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"نار: حقيقة في النار المحرقة، ومجاز في نار الحرب والحرب.\"\n- Root: حي (ḥ-y) — Serpent\n  * Lisan al-Arab: \"الحية: من الهوام، وتجمع على حيات، وأعظمها ما كان كأعناق البخت.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحية: دابة مؤذية، وفي الآخرة تكون أعظم وأشد إيلامًا.\"\n- Root: عقر (ʿ-q-r) — Scorpion\n  * Lisan al-Arab: \"العقرب: دويبة مؤذية تلدغ، وفي النار تكون كالبغال.\"\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Used to specify that serpents and scorpions are only empowered over those who were dominated by miserliness and evil character in the world.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Insight"}], "48": [{"arabic": "وما هم بسكارى ولكن عذاب الله شديد يوم تبدل الأرض غير الأرض والسموات وبرزوا لله الواحد القهار يوم تنسف فيه الجبال نسفا فتترك قاعا صفصفا لا ترى فيها عوجا ولا أمتا يوم ترى الجبال تحسبها جامدة وهي تمر مر السحاب يوم تنشق فيه السماء فتكون وردة كالدهان فيومئذ لا يسئل عن ذنبه إنس ولا جان يوم يمنع فيه العاصى من الكلام ولا يسئل فيه عن الأجرام بل يؤخذ بالنواصى والأقدام يوم تجد كل نفس ما عملت من خير محضرا وما عملت من سوء تود لو أن بينها وبينه أمدا بعيدا يوم تعلم فيه كل نفس ما أحضرت وتشهد ما قدمت وأخرت يوم تخرس فيه الألسن وتنطق الجوارح يوم شيب ذكره س", "english": "So prepare yourself, O wretched one, for this Day of immense magnitude, of prolonged duration, of overwhelming dominion, whose appointed time is near — a Day when you shall see the heaven split asunder, and the stars scattered in its terror, and the shining planets extinguished, and the sun folded up, and the mountains set in motion, and the pregnant camels neglected, and the wild beasts gathered together, and the seas set ablaze, and the souls paired with their bodies, and the Hellfire ignited, and Paradise brought near, and the mountains pulverized, and the earth stretched out — a Day when you shall see the earth shaken with its quaking, and the earth casts forth its burdens. On that Day shall people issue forth in scattered groups to be shown their deeds — a Day when the earth and the mountains are lifted and crushed with a single crushing. Then on that Day the Imminent Event shall come to pass, and the heaven shall split open, for on that Day it shall be frail, and the angels shall be at its edges, and eight shall bear the Throne of your Lord above them on that Day. On that Day you shall be presented — no secret of yours shall remain hidden. A Day when the mountains shall move and you shall see the earth laid bare — a Day when the earth shall be shaken with a violent shaking, and the mountains crumbled to dust, becoming scattered particles — a Day when people shall be like scattered moths, and the mountains like carded wool — a Day when every nursing mother shall forget what she nursed, and every pregnant female shall cast down her burden, and you shall see people drunk, yet they are not drunk, but the chastisement of God is severe — a Day when the earth shall be changed to other than this earth, and the heavens, and they shall appear before God, the One, the All-Compelling — a Day when the mountains shall be blown away as dust, leaving a level, bare plain, wherein you see no crookedness nor curve — a Day when you see the mountains and suppose them solid, yet they pass by like the passing of clouds — a Day when the heaven shall split open and become rosy like molten oil. Then on that Day no human nor jinn shall be asked about his sin. A Day when the disobedient shall be prevented from speech, and shall not be asked about their crimes, but shall be seized by their forelocks and their feet — a Day when every soul shall find what it did of good brought forth present, and what it did of evil, wishing that between it and that evil there were a far distance — a Day when every soul shall know what it has sent ahead, and shall witness what it has forwarded and what it has left behind — a Day when tongues shall be struck mute and the bodily members shall speak — a Day whose mention turned the hair of the Master of the Messengers white, when al-Ṣiddīq (may God be pleased with him) said to him: \"I see that you have turned white-haired, O Messenger of God.\" He said: \"Sūrat Hūd and its sisters have made me white-haired\" — namely, al-Wāqiʿah, al-Mursalāt, ʿAmma Yatasāʾalūn, and Idhā al-Shamsu Kuwwirat.", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1339", "section_title_en": "The Names and Attributes of the Day of Resurrection — An Exhortation to the Negligent", "section_index": 1339, "anchors": "- Root: يوم (yawm)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"اليوم: وقت طلوع الشمس إلى غروبها، وقد يعبر به عن مدة من الزمان أي مدة كانت\" — the day as a temporal span, extended metaphorically to any epoch or era.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"اليوم يعبر به عن وقت طلوع الشمس إلى غروبها. وقد يعبر به عن مدة من الزمان أي مدة كانت\" — theological usage denotes the appointed time of reckoning, magnified by divine attribution (أيام الله).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): a measured solar period. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"ذكر في أيام العرب كذا أي في وقائعها\" — days as epochal events; thus \"the Day\" is the supreme epochal event of divine justice.\n- Root: نفس (nafs)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: النفس: الروح والذات — the self, soul, or very essence.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النفس: الروح... وقوله: ويحذركم الله نفسه فنفسه: ذاته\" — nafs as the innermost reality of a being; in the eschatological context, souls are \"paired\" (زوجت) with bodies for judgment.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the soul as life-principle. Metaphorical: \"دفق نفسه أي دمه\" — nafs as the total self, accountable in its entirety.\n- Root: ارض (arḍ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: الأرض: الجرم المقابل للسماء — the earth as the corporeal substratum beneath the heavens.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الأرض: الجرم المقابل للسماء... ويعبر بها عن أسفل الشيء\" — the earth as the lowest register of creation, subject to total transformation on that Day.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the physical earth. Metaphorical: \"تأرض فلان: لزم الأرض\" — to cling low; on that Day the earth itself is leveled, flattened, and replaced.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The repeated \"يوم\" clauses function as temporal restrictors (ẓurūf) binding each predicate to that single, all-encompassing moment; the accumulation of names (أسامي) serves not as mere repetition but as focused predication upon the multifarious realities of a single event.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "ولا تظنن أن تلك الأرض مثل أرض الدنيا بل لا تساويها إلا فى الاسم قال تعالى يوم تبدل الأرض غير الأرض والسموات قال ابن عباس يزداد فيها وينقص وتذهب أشجارها وجبالها وأوديتها وما فيها وتمد مد الأديم العكاظى أرض بيضاء مثل الفضة لم يسفك عليها دم ولم يعمل عليها خطيئة والسموات تذهب شمسها وقمرها ونجومها فانظر يا مسكين في هول ذلك اليوم وشدته فإنه إذا اجتمع الخلائق على هذا الصعيد تناثرت من فوقهم نجوم السماء وطمس الشمس والقمر وأظلمت الأرض لخمود سراجها فبينا هم كذلك إذ دارت السماء من فوق رءوسهم وانشقت مع غلظها وشدتها خمسمائة عام والملائكة قيام على حافات", "english": "Know, O seeker, that after the resurrection and the arising from the graves, consider how they are driven — barefoot, naked, uncircumcised — to the land of the Gathering. It is a white land, a level plain, smooth and even, wherein you see no crookedness nor any curve, and upon which you see no hillock behind which a person might hide, nor any hollow that would sink him from view. Rather, it is one single, simple expanse with no variation whatsoever, and to it they are driven in troops. Glory be to Him who gathered all creatures, despite their differing kinds, from the far quarters of the earth, when He drove them by the Rajīfah (the Trembling Blast) followed by the Rādifah (the Second Blast). The Rajīfah is the first trumpet-blast, and the Rādifah is the second. Rightly are those hearts on that day agitated, and rightly are those eyes humbled. The Messenger of God, peace and blessings be upon him, said: «The people will be gathered on the Day of Resurrection upon a white, ʿafrāʾ earth, like a pure loaf of bread, upon which there is no marker for anyone.» The narrator said: \"ʿAfrah is a whiteness that is not brilliant, and al-naqī is the bread purified of its bran and husk. 'No marker' means no building that conceals, and no unevenness that repels the eye.\"", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1336", "section_title_en": "The Description of the Trumpet-Blast and the Land of the Gathering", "section_index": 1336, "anchors": "- Root: صور (ṣ-w-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes form/shape; the \"trumpet\" (ṣūr) is that which shapes and transforms states through its blast.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance — the ṣūr is the instrument of cosmic transformation, linking the two blasts (rajīfah and rādifah) to the divine command of resurrection.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — a horn/trumpet; metaphorical — the blast as the signal of universal upheaval, not a mere physical sound.\n- Root: حشر (ḥ-sh-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to gather, drive, or assemble a multitude forcibly.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theologically, ḥashr is the divine gathering of all creatures to the plain of reckoning, distinct from ordinary aggregation by its comprehensiveness and compulsion.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — herding animals; metaphorical — the compelled assembly of humanity before God, stripped of all worldly coverings.\n- Root: عفر (ʿ-f-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: ʿafrāʾ denotes a whitish hue tinged with redness or dust-color; the \"white\" earth of the gathering is not pure white but a dusty, pale expanse.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The earth's transformation (tabdīl al-arḍ) signifies a new creation, not mere alteration of accidents.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — color description; metaphorical — the earth's purity from sin and bloodshed, as the narrator glosses ʿafrāʾ as \"whiteness not brilliant.\"\n- Sibawayh Rule: Interposition of descriptive clauses (ṣifah) between subject and predicate — the extended adjectival chains (e.g., \"arḍ bayḍāʾ qāʿ ṣafṣaf...\") delay the main verb to build cumulative, awe-inducing imagery, permissible due to the semantic completeness of each descriptive unit.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Insight"}]}, "wordTiming": {"minshawi": {"1": 